const INTERMEDIATE_STORY = [
  { n: 1, title: "The Calibration", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe bread was four days old, which meant it had to be cut thin and toasted hard or it would sit in your stomach like ballast. Rooke had learned that the winter she turned nine. She had learned a great deal that winter.\n\nShe worked at the loaf with the good knife, the one Mother Halloway kept wrapped in oilcloth under the sink because Tam had a habit of using knives on things that were not food. Six slices. Six was the number that mattered in this house. She could have got seven out of the loaf if she had cut finer, but seven thin slices left everyone hungrier than six honest ones, and there was a difference between feeding people and merely giving them something to chew.\n\nThe pipe behind the wall knocked. Once, twice, then the long shudder.\n\nRooke did not look up. Nobody in the Under looked up at the knock any more than they looked up at their own heartbeat. Somewhere below the floor of the city, below the sewers and the sumps and the black flooded tunnels nobody had names for, the Great Engine turned over, and every pipe in Gifford answered it. Old men called it the Turn. Old women called it the Turn. Rooke had been calling it the Turn since before she could say the word properly, and she had been eleven years old before she understood that it was a machine and not a large and mostly benevolent animal.\n\nFour knocks and a shudder meant the vent was coming. She counted it out of habit and reached over to shut the window.\n\n\"You're up early.\" Mother Halloway stood in the doorway with her shawl held closed at the throat, grey hair loose, one hand flat on the frame because the stairs cost her more than they used to. \"Or you never went down.\"\n\n\"I went down.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" Halloway crossed to the range and put her palm against the iron, testing whether the fire had held through the night. \"Liar.\"\n\n\"I lay down. I never said I slept.\"\n\n\"Well.\" Halloway took her hand off the iron and rubbed the soot off it onto her skirt without noticing. \"It's the same for all of them, first time. Tessa was sick on the step. Do you remember?\"\n\n\"I remember. She said it was the fish.\"\n\n\"There wasn't any fish.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Rooke said. \"That's how I remember.\"\n\nOutside, the four o'clock vent let go. The Under filled up with it the way it always did: a low roar somewhere over Sootgate, then the hot white fog rolling up Ferrow Lane past the window in a wall, and the whole street went the colour of dishwater for a count of forty. Rooke had walked to work in it a thousand times. You held your breath at the front of it, took short ones in the middle, and if you were carrying anything metal you kept it under your coat because vent-fog would put a skin of rust on bare iron in a single morning. That was the whole reason anyone was called Rustbound. Some brass on the Rise had thought it was clever, a hundred years ago, and it had stuck the way names do when the people they are thrown at decide to keep them.\n\nBehind her the house was starting to wake, which in this house meant a slow rolling grumble from the back room where four of them slept in two beds. Nettle's cough went through it like a nail.\n\nRooke heard it and marked it, the way she marked everything, filing it beside the rent and the price of coke and the number of days until the coal-man's boy came round expecting his chalk settled.\n\nThe cough had been three weeks. Sister Maude had listened to Nettle's chest with a brass horn and said *give it time* in the specific tone that meant *give me money*, which was fair enough, because Maude gave away more than she took and the whole Under knew it.\n\nRooke had done the sum. She had done it the first night and she had done it every night since, and it always came out the same.\n\nFive marks and ten cogs a bottle. Two bottles for the full course.\n\nEleven marks.\n\nShe had been carrying that number around for three weeks the way you carry a stone in your boot.\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\nShe turned. Halloway was looking at her with the particular flatness she used when she had decided to say a thing and had not decided how.\n\n\"You'll do well today,\" Halloway said.\n\n\"I'll do what I do.\"\n\n\"That isn't what I said.\" Halloway lowered herself into the chair by the range, slowly, one hand on the table. \"I've had eleven through this house sit that exam. Eleven. Do you know how many took a bracket worth having?\"\n\n\"Two.\"\n\n\"Two. And how many took the top?\"\n\n\"None.\"\n\n\"None.\" Halloway nodded, as though the number had settled something between them. \"Because none of them were you.\"\n\nThe griddle spat. Rooke turned the bread with her fingers and did not feel it, because her hands had stopped registering that kind of heat years ago.\n\n\"There's no point in it,\" she said.\n\n\"There's every point in it.\"\n\n\"There's no point in it for me.\" She said it evenly, because she had rehearsed it lying in the dark from third bell onward, running it the way she ran a sum. \"If I take the top bracket they come for me the same afternoon. There's a carriage and a form and a signature and then I'm on the clatterlift going up the Rise with a bag, and that's seven years.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Seven years, Mother.\" Rooke turned round. \"Who does the Colvers' pump? Who does Marta's ledgers? Who counts what the coal-man's boy is skimming off the chalk every week, because it's eight grist a week and he thinks nobody's watching and I'm the only one in this house who can hold a column in her head. Tessa can't. Tessa can barely count her own fingers twice the same and I love her, but she can't.\"\n\n\"Tessa manages.\"\n\n\"Tessa manages because I'm here.\" Rooke's voice did not rise. That was the thing people found unsettling about her, and she knew it, and she used it. \"You manage because I'm here. Nettle gets syrup because I'm here. If I go up the Rise, I get fed and clothed and taught the true calculus by men in gowns, and this house is six weeks behind on the rent by the back end of autumn and out on the step by first frost. I've done the sum. I've done it eleven ways. It comes out the same every way.\"\n\nHalloway was quiet for a long moment. Under them the Turn went round and the pipe knocked twice.\n\n\"And what happens in seven years?\" she said. \"When you're a reckoner with a writ and a wage and you could buy this house outright with what you'd make in a season?\"\n\n\"In seven years Nettle's dead.\"\n\nIt came out flat and fast and far too loud, and it hung in the little kitchen with the smell of burning bread, and Rooke heard herself say it and could not take it back.\n\nFrom the back room, the cough. Three times, then the wet catch, then nothing.\n\n\"That's not certain,\" Halloway said quietly.\n\n\"No,\" Rooke said. \"It's likely. There's a difference and I know the difference. It's my whole trouble, knowing the difference.\"\n\nShe took the bread off the griddle. Six slices, black at the edges now, ruined in the way that meant everyone would eat them anyway and nobody would say a word.\n\n\"I'm going to fail,\" she said. \"Properly. Ninth stratum or lower. Nothing they'd want and nothing they'd look at twice. Then I'm coming home and doing the Colvers' pump, because they're paying a mark and eight cogs for it, and that's a mark and eight cogs against eleven marks.\"\n\nHalloway looked at her for a long time.\n\n\"You know what the cruel thing is,\" the old woman said at last. \"It isn't that you'd do it. It's that you worked out how to.\"\n\n---\n\nTallow Street ran along the bottom edge of the Rise, on the seam, in the part of Gifford where the two halves of the city had to touch each other and neither liked it. The examination hall was a great grey slab of a building with the Wrought crest cut into the lintel: the cogwheel, the open eye and the words underneath in the old script that nobody read any more because everyone had been told what they said. *By reckoning, order.*\n\nSix hundred and forty-one fourteen-year-olds queued down the pavement in the sootfall.\n\nIt was not rain. It looked like rain from a distance and it fell like rain and it was black, and by second bell it had put a fine wet grit on every shoulder in the line. Above them the clatterlifts went up and down the face of the Rise on their cables, six of them, brass cars full of people who had never queued for anything, and the noise they made was the noise the whole seam of the city made all day: a clanking, hauling, industrious racket that meant somebody else was going somewhere.\n\nRooke stood between a boy who would not stop cracking his knuckles and a girl who was mouthing her tables with her eyes shut, and she looked up at the open eye on the lintel and thought: they cut that in the year the Turn started. A hundred and three years of children standing in this queue in this filth, and not one of them ever here because they wanted to be.\n\nSomeone up ahead was crying. Someone further up was being sick.\n\nA clerk came down the line with a box of wooden tokens and a ledger and asked each of them their name, their district and their household, and wrote it down, and pressed a token into their hand with a number burned into the wood.\n\n\"Name.\"\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\nThe clerk's pen stopped. \"Full name.\"\n\n\"That's the whole of it.\"\n\nHe looked up. Wet collar, grey face, a man three hundred names into a job he would be six hundred names into by noon. \"Household, then.\"\n\n\"Halloway. Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"Foster?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe wrote it. He did not look at her again. He put the token in her palm and moved on.\n\nFour four seven one.\n\nRooke turned the token over in her fingers all the way up the steps and into the great cold hall with its six hundred desks in their perfect rows, and thought about how a number is only a number until somebody decides what to do with it.\n\n---\n\n**II. Corvain**\n\"Stand straighter,\" said Lady Adelina Corvain. \"You look like a clerk.\"\n\n\"I am, functionally, a clerk.\"\n\n\"You are an officer of the enforcement division of the Council of Gifford and you are wearing the coat, Elspeth, so stand in it properly.\"\n\nCorvain stood in it properly. It was easier than the alternative, and the coat did demand a certain posture; it had been cut for someone with shoulders, and she had been growing into it for four months without quite arriving.\n\nThey were on the observation gallery, a long balcony of dark wood and brass rail running the width of the hall, thirty feet above the floor. Below them six hundred children were being seated by examiners with clipboards. The sound was extraordinary: not loud, but dense, a mass of small movements and small breaths in a room built to carry them.\n\nThe gallery was full. That was the part she had not expected. She had assumed the Calibration would be administered the way a difficult surgery is administered, quietly and by professionals. Instead half the houses of the Rise had turned out in their good coats to watch it happen, and there were servants moving along the gallery with trays of hot spiced wine, and Lady Fenwick was three seats down telling anyone who would hold still about her nephew's tutors.\n\n\"Why is everyone here?\" Corvain said.\n\n\"Don't say *everyone* as though you were raised in a sump.\"\n\n\"Why is anyone here. It's an examination. It takes four hours. There is nothing to see.\"\n\n\"It is the single most important civic occasion in the calendar and attendance is noticed.\" Her mother did not look at her while she said it. She was scanning the gallery, and Corvain understood that the noticing worked in both directions and that her mother was doing her share of it. \"The Chancellor comes at second hour. Kell makes his address. Afterwards there is a reception at which a great many people who matter will be standing in one room, and you, who have been an officer for four months and have made no impression on anybody, will be standing in it with them.\"\n\n\"I'm here to observe the examination.\"\n\n\"You are here to be seen observing the examination.\"\n\n\"Captain Ashcombe assigned me. There's a duty roster.\"\n\n\"Reeve Ashcombe assigned you because I asked him to,\" her mother said. \"Do stop being provincial. Everyone's posting is arranged by somebody. The only question worth asking is whether the somebody was competent.\"\n\nBelow, a bell rang. Six hundred children sat down at once, and the sound of it was like a wave breaking a long way off.\n\n---\n\nGrandmaster Osric Kell walked to the lectern. He was a tall, dry, grey man of about seventy who moved as though every joint had been individually measured and found adequate. He used no notes.\n\n\"You are here,\" he said, and the acoustics of the hall took his voice and laid it flat and clear across every desk, \"because the Turn requires it.\"\n\nCorvain leaned on the rail.\n\n\"A hundred and three years ago this city was mud and fever and eleven thousand people dying of a bad winter. Then twelve reckoners built the Great Engine, and the Engine gave us light and water and heat and carriage and bread, and it asks one thing of us in exchange. It asks to be understood. Every year it asks. Every year we must answer, or it stops, and the mud comes back.\"\n\nHe paused. Nobody moved.\n\n\"You will be tested for four hours. Figures, shapes, the true calculus insofar as any of you have been taught to reach for it. You will be placed in a stratum according to what you show me. Some of you will go up the Rise. Some will be given writs and permissions and useful work. Some will be given nothing, because you will have shown me nothing.\"\n\nA boy in the second row began, quietly, to cry.\n\n\"That is not cruelty,\" Kell said. \"That is arithmetic. The Turn does not care whether you are hungry. It does not care who your mother was. It asks one question and it asks it of every child in this room in exactly the same words. There is no fairer thing in this city than the paper in front of you. Turn it over.\"\n\nSix hundred papers turned over at once.\n\n\"He believes that,\" Corvain said.\n\n\"Of course he believes it,\" said her mother. \"It's true.\"\n\n---\n\nBy third hour Corvain had stopped watching the room and started watching individual children, because the room had settled into a single texture of scratching pens and there was nothing left in it to see.\n\nIt was a bad habit of hers. Ashcombe had told her so, in the voice he used when he meant the opposite. *You look at the one thing instead of the hundred things. It will make you very good and very unpopular.*\n\nSo she looked at the one thing.\n\nThe one thing was in the third row from the back, on the left, under the high windows. A girl, small for fourteen, dark hair cut short and badly, in a coat mended at both elbows by somebody who could sew and had run out of matching thread.\n\nShe was working the paper backwards.\n\nCorvain watched for a full minute to be sure. The Calibration paper ran easy to hard; that was the entire architecture of it. You began at the front and got as far as you got, and where you stopped was your stratum. Every child in the hall was moving front to back at whatever speed they could manage.\n\nThe girl in the third row from the back had opened her paper at the last page.\n\nShe read the final question. She did not write. She turned back three pages, read something, turned forward again. She sat for perhaps ninety seconds without moving her pen at all, which at third hour of the Calibration was an unthinkable extravagance, and then she began to write, quickly and without pausing, and filled a page and a half, and stopped.\n\nThen she turned to the front of the paper and started question one.\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"Who's the examiner on the west floor?\"\n\n\"How on earth would I know?\"\n\nCorvain came up off the rail and went to find out.\n\n---\n\n**III. Marrow**\nThey put him in the overflow room, which was not a room but the old chapel at the back of the hall, and there were forty-one desks in it, and Marrow counted every one of them, because counting was what he did when he was angry and he had learned a long time ago that it was better than the other things.\n\nForty-one desks. Six hundred in the main hall.\n\nSix hundred and forty-one candidates in Gifford this year, and forty-one of them were in a chapel with a leaking roof, and every one of those forty-one had fur or horns or a snout or a tail. There was no rule anywhere that said it had to be so. Nobody had written it down. That was the trick of it. Nobody had written it down, so there was nothing to point at.\n\nHe sat. The desk was too small; they were all too small, built for human children. Two rows over a Laachmi girl had given up on hers entirely and was writing on her knees with the paper against a book, horns tipped forward, working. Marrow knew her a little, or knew her family; the Laachmi in Sootgate mostly worked the drop-forges, because the forges were the one trade in the Under where being seven feet tall and able to lift an anvil was worth actual money, and because nobody else wanted the work. Her people had a word for themselves that went back further than Gifford did. The brass on the Rise called them horns, or bullheads, and thought they were being witty.\n\nMarrow got his elbows arranged and his tail out of the way of the chair legs and told himself, again, the thing he had been telling himself for a month.\n\n*It does not matter where they sit you. It is the same paper.*\n\nKell had said it himself. Marrow had heard it through the wall, faint but clear enough. *There is no fairer thing in this city than the paper in front of you.*\n\nFine. Then let it be the paper.\n\n---\n\nThe invigilator in the chapel was a young man named Pemberton who had said good morning to nobody and who spent the first hour reading a periodical folded inside his ledger.\n\nThe paper was good. That was the strange thing. Marrow had spent two years preparing for it with a stolen syllabus and a slate with a corner off it and Ketu's kitchen table, and he had expected a wall. It was not a wall. It was a staircase. Every question was one step up from the last, and every step was findable if you were standing on the one below it properly.\n\nHe went through the first two thirds without stopping. Around the ninth section it turned hard, properly hard, hard in a way that put a prickle up the back of his neck, and he had to take the questions apart before he could get anywhere with them. Twice he had to leave a thing and come back to it.\n\nHe got there. Section nine, section ten, the long problem at the end about a load carried across an arch, which he answered wrong the first time and then saw, with a lurch of something close to joy, exactly why it was wrong, and answered again.\n\nHe put the pen down.\n\nThirty-five minutes left. He had never in his life had thirty-five minutes left at the end of anything.\n\nMarrow sat in a leaking chapel at a desk built for someone half his size and looked at four hours of his own handwriting, and let himself think the thought he had refused to think for two years, because thinking it had made it possible to lose.\n\n*I have done it.*\n\nNot the top. He was honest with himself about that; he had worked the old papers and he knew what the top of this looked like, and the top of this was somebody who did not have to slow down at section nine. But the upper strata. The real ones, the ones with writs and placements and a wage paid in silver rather than chalked on a slate at Marta's. Work in the Engine houses. A room with one person in it.\n\nHe thought about Ketu, who had come to Gifford forty years ago because her own people had put her out for reasons she had never once discussed, and who had scrubbed the stairs of a house on the Rise for thirty of those years, and who that morning had put her hand flat on the top of his head and said nothing at all, because she was not a woman who said things, and because there had been nothing to say that would not have sounded like hoping.\n\nHe would tell her tonight. Plainly, the way she liked things said.\n\nThe bell rang.\n\n---\n\nOutside, on the step, in the sootfall, a Wrought officer in a grey coat was stopping candidates as they came out and asking them things.\n\nMarrow watched from under the chapel arch and did not go over, because thirty years of Ketu's advice had settled into him as a single instinct: when the brass are asking, be elsewhere.\n\nThe officer was young. Twenty, perhaps. She had stopped a small girl in a mended coat and she was not shouting at her, which was unusual enough to keep him there.\n\nThe girl said something. The officer did not write it down.\n\nThen the officer said something back, and the girl's face did a thing Marrow recognised at once, because he had felt it on his own face inside the last hour: the look of somebody who has just been shown that a door they had carefully locked was never theirs to lock.\n\nMarrow put his hood up and went home through the vent-fog to tell Ketu he had done it.\n\n---\n\n**IV. Second Examiner Voss**\nThe marking ran through the night. It always did. Six hundred and forty-one papers, twenty-two examiners, eleven hours, and by the fourth of those hours the room upstairs at Tallow Street smelled of cold tea and lamp oil and the particular sourness of people who have agreed not to go home.\n\nAda Voss was on the west floor bundle, desks four hundred through five hundred: a hundred papers and a running tally on the strata sheet at her elbow. She was quick at it. Nine years quick. She could take a Calibration paper and put a stratum on it inside four minutes and be right, and the being right was not a small thing. The strata were checked and re-checked, and an examiner who drifted was an examiner who stopped being an examiner.\n\nShe had done sixty-one papers when she came to four four seven one and everything stopped.\n\nVoss read it through once at speed, the way she read all of them.\n\nThen she set her pen down and read it again properly.\n\nCandidate 4471 had scored in the ninth stratum. Ninth of eleven, which was bad. Ninth was where the guidance said *limited aptitude, no placement, no writ*. Ninth was a girl who would be handed a card in three days and told she was of no use to the Turn and could go home.\n\nThe paper was wrong from beginning to end. That was not the strange part.\n\nThe strange part was *how* it was wrong.\n\nVoss went back to the front. Question one: two numbers, add them. Candidate 4471 had added them incorrectly. She had produced a sum that was out by eleven.\n\nQuestion two. Out by eleven.\n\nQuestion three. Out by eleven.\n\nVoss's mouth went dry.\n\nShe got up, took last year's guidance down off the shelf and did not need to open it, because she knew what it said and had never once had cause to use it. Then she sat down and worked forward through four four seven one with a fresh sheet at her elbow, and she stopped correcting the answers and began reading the errors as though the errors were the work.\n\nBy section four it was unmistakable. The candidate had taken the true value of every answer and then moved it. Not randomly. Not the way a struggling child moves a value, which is to say by dropping a digit or reversing a pair or losing a sign. Every answer had been displaced by a quantity that was itself set by the question number, and the rule was clean, and it held across sixty-eight answers, and it held even in the sections where the candidate was required to show her working.\n\nThe working was what made Voss's hands go cold. The working was beautiful. To get an answer wrong that precisely, and to build a plausible chain of reasoning that arrived at exactly that wrong answer, sixty-eight times running, at speed, under invigilation, you had to solve every question correctly first and then solve a second and considerably harder problem about how to hide it.\n\nVoss thought: this is not a child failing an examination. This is a child sitting a different examination, one she set herself, and passing it.\n\nShe sat back.\n\nThe correct procedure was on the shelf. Log the paper as a suspected irregularity and refer it upward to the Grandmaster's office, where it would be assessed, and if the assessment held the candidate would be brought back for supervised re-examination.\n\nVoss looked at the ninth-stratum tally on the sheet at her elbow, and at the name in the register. *Rooke, no other given. Halloway household, Ferrow Lane.*\n\nFerrow Lane was eight streets from where Voss had grown up. She had sat this examination herself, twenty-six years ago, in the main hall, in a coat her aunt had mended, and she had come out fourth stratum, which had been enough for a clerkship and then a training and then this room. Fourth stratum. She had been quietly proud of it for a quarter of a century.\n\nShe knew exactly what a girl from Ferrow Lane was buying, with eleven, sixty-eight times over.\n\nVoss also knew, because she was not a fool and had worked in this building for nine years, that a referral to the Grandmaster's office was a stone dropped down a well. Kell would assess it correctly, and Kell would be right, and somewhere between Kell's rightness and the girl's front door a great many people with a great many interests would get a look at the file.\n\nSo she did not write it up.\n\nShe wrote a note instead, on a plain sheet, and folded it, and at a quarter past fourth bell she carried it down two floors to the duty room, where the young officer from the observation gallery had left her name and her posting and a request, in a hand far too careful to belong to anybody who had been doing the job long, that any irregularity concerning the west floor be brought to her attention directly.\n\nVoss did not know why she chose the girl in the grey coat over the Grandmaster of Examinations.\n\nShe would think about it, on and off, for years.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Retake**\nThey came for her at second bell, which was worse than coming at night. At night nobody sees. At second bell on Ferrow Lane the whole street was out, the fog had lifted, and the carriage was black with the cogwheel on the door. Tam was on the step. Nettle was at the window. Mother Halloway came out with her shawl held closed at the throat and put herself between Rooke and the two men in grey coats and said, \"On what grounds.\"\n\n\"Examination irregularity,\" said the taller one. \"She's not banked. She's requested.\"\n\n\"Requested where?\"\n\n\"Tallow Street.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\n\"Ma'am, I've a form with a name and an address on it. It doesn't say how long.\"\n\nRooke touched Halloway's arm.\n\n\"It's all right,\" she said.\n\nIt was not all right, and Halloway knew it was not all right, and the whole street watched Rooke walk to the carriage on her own two feet as though she had chosen it, because the alternative was being carried, and there was no version of being carried that Ferrow Lane would ever forget.\n\n---\n\nThe hall was empty.\n\nThat was the first blow and she had not braced for it. Yesterday there had been six hundred of them and the noise of six hundred. Today there were six hundred empty desks in their perfect rows, sootfall on the high windows, and nothing else, and the size of the room had gone from a shelter to an accusation.\n\nOne desk had a paper on it. Third row from the back, on the left, under the windows. Her desk.\n\nThere were four people in the hall.\n\nGrandmaster Osric Kell stood at the lectern with his hands behind his back. On the floor beside her desk stood a woman of about fifty in the black coat of a senior examiner, iron-grey hair scraped into a knot, a pocket watch open in her palm. At the back of the room, near the doors, arms folded, stood the young officer in grey who had stopped Rooke on the step yesterday and asked her, mildly, whether she had found the paper difficult.\n\nThe fourth was Second Examiner Ada Voss, standing a little apart from all of them, not looking at Rooke, hands not quite steady.\n\n\"Candidate four four seven one,\" said Kell. \"Do you know why you are here?\"\n\nRooke's mouth was very dry. She thought about eleven marks. She thought about the Colvers' pump and a mark and eight cogs.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\n\"That is a lie,\" said the woman with the watch, \"and we have four hours, so let us not spend them on it.\"\n\nShe came forward. Up close she was shorter than Rooke had thought and her face was all straight lines.\n\n\"My name is Vera Kross. I am Senior Examiner of this hall and I have been marking these papers for thirty-one years.\" She closed the watch with a click. \"Do you know what an examiner actually does, girl? Everyone thinks we count. We don't count. Any clerk can count. We read the errors. A right answer tells you almost nothing; it says *I knew this*. A wrong answer says *here is the exact shape of the hole in me*. Thirty-one years I have been reading holes.\"\n\nShe set the watch down on the desk beside the paper.\n\n\"There is no hole in you,\" Kross said. \"I have read your paper four times. There is a hole in the paper, and it is a perfect one, and it is exactly eleven wide from one end to the other, and no child in the history of this hall has ever been stupid in a straight line.\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"So.\" Kross tapped the paper. \"Sit down.\"\n\n\"On what grounds,\" Rooke said, and hated how thin it came out.\n\n\"On the grounds that I do not yet know whether you cheated to fail or cheated to pass and turned clever at the last moment, and it happens to be the same procedure for both. Sit *down*.\"\n\nRooke did not move.\n\n\"Why does it matter?\" she said.\n\n\"I beg your pardon.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter which stratum I'm in.\" Her voice was steadier now, which surprised her. \"You had six hundred and forty-one of us. You'll fill the top out of the Rise the way you do every year. You've got what you need. What does it matter what one girl off Ferrow Lane puts on a piece of paper?\"\n\nKross looked at her a moment. Then she said, without any particular heat:\n\n\"Because I do not permit lies in my hall. Not from the Rise, not from Ferrow Lane, not from you. Everything in this city stands on the reckoning being honest. You put a lie into the reckoning. I don't care what you did it for.\"\n\n\"Senior Examiner,\" said Kell from the lectern, mildly.\n\n\"Grandmaster.\"\n\n\"She asked a fair question and you gave her a true answer that was not the answer.\" Kell came down off the step, slowly, and stopped a few feet away. He looked at Rooke, and for the first time she understood that he was very old and very tired and that the tiredness had not come from last night. \"It matters, child, because the Turn does not care what we intend. Every year it puts a question to this city. Every year we must find, out of six hundred and forty-one children, the ones who can hear it. If a girl who can hear it decides to sit at the back of the hall and pretend she cannot, then the city gets a little worse at answering. And it gets worse at answering every year. And one year we will not be able to answer at all, and on that day the lights go out and the water stops and eleven thousand people die of a bad winter. That is why it matters. Not because you lied. Because we cannot afford you.\"\n\nThe sootfall went on against the high windows.\n\n\"And what do I get?\" Rooke said.\n\nKell blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"You said you can't afford me. All right. What do I get.\" She let her voice go hard. \"There's a house on Ferrow Lane with six people in it and one of them is nine and can't stop coughing, and the syrup is five marks ten a bottle and she needs two. Eleven marks. Nobody in that house has eleven marks. I earn it. Me. So if I sit down at that desk and do it properly, what happens to them?\"\n\nNobody answered.\n\n\"That's the sum,\" Rooke said. \"That's the actual sum, and everybody in this room wants to talk about the Turn and eleven thousand dead a hundred years ago and not one of you has done the actual sum. I did it. Eleven ways. It came out the same eleven times. So I sat down and I put eleven on every line, and I would do it again, and if you'd left me alone I'd be at the Colvers' with a valve-key right now earning a mark and eight cogs of it.\"\n\nFrom the back of the hall, quietly, the officer in grey said:\n\n\"A mark and eight.\"\n\nRooke turned.\n\nThe young woman came forward a few steps, hands in her coat pockets, frowning. Not unkindly. In the manner of somebody working through a piece of arithmetic they do not enjoy.\n\n\"That's what you weighed it against,\" she said. \"A mark and eight cogs, and a pump.\"\n\n\"Eleven marks. And a house.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" The officer stopped. \"I understand the house. I want to be clear that I understand the house, because I am about to say something that will sound as though I don't.\" She took a breath. \"I stopped you on the step yesterday because I watched you open your paper at the back. Every child in that hall went front to back. You read the last question first, sat for ninety seconds, wrote a page and a half without pausing, then went to the front and started being wrong. I have thought about that for a day and a night. You did not fail your examination. You solved it, all of it, inside the first ninety seconds, and then you spent four hours building a lie on top of it.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So the lie was the hard part,\" said the officer. \"You did the hard part on purpose. For a mark and eight cogs at a time.\"\n\nShe said it without contempt, which was what got through. Rooke had armour for contempt.\n\n\"Elspeth Corvain,\" the officer said. \"I am the one who had you brought in. Not the Grandmaster. Not the Senior Examiner. Me. So if you want to be angry at somebody, and you should be, be accurate about it.\"\n\nRooke looked at her. Twenty at the most. Good coat. Clean hands. Some house up the Rise with a name and a crest and no cough in it anywhere.\n\n\"You've ruined us,\" Rooke said. \"You know that? You came down off the Rise and you were clever and you've ruined us, and you'll go home tonight and have your supper and feel very fine about the reckoning being honest.\"\n\n\"That's possible,\" said Corvain. \"I have thought about that too.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I did it anyway.\" She held Rooke's eyes. \"Because I watched a fourteen year old spend four hours making herself smaller in front of six hundred people, and I have never in my life been so certain that I was looking at something that was not supposed to happen.\"\n\nSilence.\n\nThen Vera Kross picked up the pocket watch, snapped it open and stood it on the desk face-up.\n\n\"Four hours,\" she said. \"The paper in front of you is not yesterday's paper. It is the reserve, which is harder, because the reserve is written for the eventuality of precisely this and nobody has needed it in nine years. You will sit. You will work. I will be at your shoulder for the whole of it, and Second Examiner Voss at the other, and the Grandmaster at the lectern, and Officer Corvain at the door, and nobody will speak to you and nobody will help you.\"\n\nShe stepped back from the desk.\n\n\"You may lie again if you like,\" Kross said. \"You are very good at it. But understand what you would be doing. Yesterday you lied to a room that was not watching. Today there are three people in this hall who have read your working and know exactly what you can do. If you sit down and put eleven on every line, I will not be fooled and I will not be angry. I will write *ninth stratum* on the form, and you will go home to Ferrow Lane with your mark and eight cogs, and in thirty-one years I will still be able to tell you the precise shape of the hole you decided to become.\"\n\nRooke stood in the empty hall with six hundred desks behind her.\n\nShe thought about Nettle's cough, three times and then the wet catch. About Halloway's hand flat on the table, taking her weight on the way to the chair. About six slices, black at the edges, and how nobody would ever say a word.\n\nShe thought: they are all wrong about what the choice is. They think it is honest against dishonest. It is two houses, and I only get to save one.\n\nThen she thought, with a cold clear lurch that felt like the moment in a sum where the shape of the thing finally shows itself:\n\n*Unless I stop letting them set the question.*\n\nRooke walked to the third row from the back, on the left, under the high windows.\n\nShe pulled out the chair. She sat. She picked up the pen, squared the paper to the edge of the desk the way she squared everything, and looked at the first page of a reserve examination nobody had needed in nine years.\n\n\"Four hours,\" said Vera Kross.\n\n\"I won't need four,\" said Rooke, and turned it over.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter One.*" },
  { n: 2, title: "Results Day", text: "**I. Marrow**\nThey posted the strata at Tallow Street at first bell on the third day, on eleven boards under the lintel, and by the time Marrow got there the crowd went back across the road and halfway up the steps of the coke exchange.\n\nHe had not slept. He had not really slept for three nights. He had lain on the pallet in Ketu's back room listening to the knock in the pipe and doing the arch problem again in his head, over and over, checking it, and every time it came out the way it had come out in the chapel, and every time he thought *good*, and then he thought *unless*, and then he did it again.\n\nThe crowd had a shape to it. Marrow had grown up reading crowds and this one had the shape of a crowd around bad news. There were girls sitting on the kerb with their heads down. There was a woman shouting at a clerk who was not listening, in the practised way of a man whose entire profession is not listening. There was a knot of boys near the second board being loudly delighted, which was worse than the crying.\n\nHe worked his way up the left side, where there was more space, because there was always more space where he was.\n\nEleven boards. First stratum at the top of the lintel, in a frame, with gilt on it. Eleventh at the far end, nailed flat, a single sheet.\n\nMarrow did not go to the eleventh. He was not afraid of the eleventh; he had never for one moment thought he was going to the eleventh. He went to the fourth, because the fourth was where he had put himself in every honest reckoning he had done in two years, and he ran his eye down the column looking for his own name.\n\nIt was not there.\n\nHe read it again. Slower. Bottom to top this time, because eyes lie going downward.\n\nNot there.\n\nHis chest did something unpleasant. He went to the fifth board, and then the sixth, and by the sixth he was not reading properly any more, he was scanning for the shape of his own name the way you scan for a face in a crowd, and it was not on the fifth and it was not on the sixth and it was not on the seventh.\n\nThen a hand came down on his shoulder from behind and he nearly took the man's arm off at the elbow.\n\n\"Steady.\" A clerk. Not Pemberton. Older, heavier, with the grey cuffs of the examinations office and a satchel under one arm. \"You're four one nine, aren't you. The Sirrath boy.\"\n\n\"Marrow.\"\n\n\"Right.\" The clerk looked at him with something that was not quite pity and was not quite anything else. \"You'll not find yourself on the boards. You've an amendment. You want the office, round the side, ask for the counter.\"\n\n\"An amendment.\"\n\n\"Round the side,\" the clerk said, and moved on.\n\n---\n\nThe counter was a hatch in a corridor that smelled of damp plaster and lamp oil, and there was a queue at it of nine people, and every one of the nine had a tail or horns.\n\nMarrow stood in that queue for forty minutes and worked out, somewhere around the twentieth, what he was standing in.\n\nThe Laachmi girl from the chapel was three ahead of him. The one who had written on her knees. She came away from the hatch with a paper in her hand and she did not look at any of them as she passed, and Marrow saw her horns come down and her shoulders go up and knew exactly what was on the paper without reading a word of it.\n\nWhen his turn came the woman at the hatch did not look up.\n\n\"Number.\"\n\n\"Four one nine.\"\n\nPaper. Stamp. The scrape of a drawer.\n\n\"Marrow, no household given, Sootgate district. Sirrath.\" She read it off flat, the way you read a docket. \"Your result is amended.\"\n\n\"To what.\"\n\n\"Ninth stratum.\"\n\nMarrow put both hands on the ledge of the hatch because he was not entirely certain his legs were going to keep doing their job.\n\n\"I was fourth,\" he said.\n\n\"Your assessed result was fourth stratum,\" the woman said. \"Your amended result is ninth. The amendment is on grounds of prior conduct. That's a bank, isn't it. Last winter.\"\n\n\"That was a bank for cogging. I was thirteen. I took a pressure gauge off a scrap barrow and the barrow-man got his money back and the magistrate gave me a caution and eight grist' costs and that was the end of it.\"\n\n\"It's a bank.\"\n\n\"It's a *caution*.\"\n\n\"The form says conduct.\" She turned the paper round and pushed it across the ledge at him. \"Prior conduct, adverse, magistrate's record. Amendment applied. There's the stamp.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the paper.\n\nIt was very short. That was the thing that would stay with him for the rest of his life, longer than the injustice, longer even than the anger: that the whole of it had taken four lines. Two years of Ketu's kitchen table. The stolen syllabus. The slate with the corner off. Section nine and the load across the arch and the lurch of joy when he saw why he was wrong. Four lines and a stamp.\n\n\"Where's the appeal,\" he said.\n\n\"There isn't one.\"\n\n\"There's an appeal for everything. There's an appeal if the coal-man overcharges you eight grist. There's a *form*.\"\n\n\"There's no appeal on an amendment.\" She was already reaching past him for the next docket. \"The amendment is the appeal. Assessed result, adverse conduct, amended result. That's the process working. Next.\"\n\n\"Who signed it.\"\n\n\"Next.\"\n\n\"Who *signed* it.\"\n\nThe woman looked up at him then, properly, for the first time, and Marrow watched her take in the whole of him: the height, the pelt, the white of it, the fact that he was leaning on her ledge with both hands and that his voice had gone somewhere he had not authorised it to go.\n\nShe did not look frightened. She looked tired, and then she looked careful.\n\n\"Sir,\" she said, \"you want to take your paper and go home.\"\n\n---\n\nNinth stratum.\n\nHe walked. He did not choose a direction and he went a long way in it. Down off the seam, down the Grieves Steps two at a time and then not at a time at all, down through the arches under the coke exchange where the drays came through in the mornings, down into the Under where the light went brown and the gas came on at three in the afternoon because it never properly got light down there at all.\n\n*Limited aptitude. No placement. No writ.*\n\nThat was what ninth said. He knew it by heart because everybody knew it by heart. Ninth was the stratum they gave to children who could not hold a column, who could not do a fraction, who had come into that hall at fourteen barely able to read the paper. Ninth was not a punishment. Ninth was a description. It was the city writing down, in a ledger that would follow him until he died, that Marrow of Sootgate had sat the Calibration and shown them nothing.\n\nHe thought: they did not even give me eleventh.\n\nEleventh would have been an insult. Eleventh would have been a thing you could point at. Ninth was *plausible*. Ninth was a number a person like him might genuinely have got. Whoever chose ninth had chosen it carefully, and the care was the part that made him want to put his fist through something, because the care meant a person had sat at a desk with his paper in front of them and thought about it.\n\nHe came out on Cinder Row about the time the gas went on.\n\nThere was a notice board at the top of the Row, the way there was in every district in the Under: cast iron frame, glass front, the Council's own boards for the Council's own notices. Rents. Writ hearings. Water interruptions. And this week, the same as every year in the week after the Calibration, a fresh printed sheet behind the glass, the same sheet on every board in the city.\n\nMarrow stood in front of it in the gaslight and read it.\n\n**BY ORDER OF THE COUNCIL OF GIFFORD**\n\n**The Calibration is complete. The strata are posted. To every candidate: your place is the place your work has earned. The reckoning does not err. The reckoning does not favour. Serve the Turn in the station you have shown yourself fit for, and know that the city stands because the reckoning is honest.**\n\n*By reckoning, order.*\n\nThere was a brick loose in the wall by his knee. He had noticed it on the way past, the way he noticed everything, and filed it, the way he filed everything.\n\nMarrow was not a fool. He knew, taking it out of the wall, exactly what it was worth. He knew that the glass in a Council board cost about two marks to replace, and that breaking one was a bank, and that a second bank for a Sirrath in the Under with an adverse conduct amendment already on his record was not a caution and eight grist' costs, it was six months in the works at Grieves and a mark on him for life.\n\nHe knew all of that. He had the whole sum.\n\nHe did it anyway, and it was the only genuinely stupid thing he had ever done, and for about a second and a half it was the best he had ever felt.\n\nThe glass came out of the frame in one piece and went across the cobbles in nine thousand bits, and the sound of it went up Cinder Row and down it, and somewhere on the corner a whistle started.\n\n---\n\n**II. Tessa Brindle**\nThe carriage came at second bell, black, with the cogwheel on the door.\n\nTessa was on the step with the wash bucket, which is where she had been since first bell, because Mother Halloway had said *do something with your hands* and there had been nothing to do with them except the wash.\n\nShe had known. That was the thing nobody understood afterwards, when they talked about it on Ferrow Lane. They talked as though the carriage had been a surprise. It had not been a surprise since the morning three days ago when the two grey coats had taken Rooke to Tallow Street and Rooke had walked to the carriage on her own feet as though she had chosen it. Tessa had watched her go and had known, in her stomach, before her head caught up.\n\nBecause Rooke came back that night and would not say what she had done.\n\nThat was how you knew. Rooke told you everything. Rooke told you what the coal-man's boy was skimming and how the Colvers' pump worked and why the water on the Row went brown on a Tuesday, whether or not you had asked, whether or not you could follow it, because she could not help herself; things went in and had to come out. And she had come home from Tallow Street at gone eighth bell and eaten her bread and gone to bed and said nothing, and in the morning she got up and did the pump at the Colvers' and came back with a mark and eight cogs and put it in the tin and said nothing.\n\nThree days of nothing.\n\nSo Tessa was on the step with the wash bucket when the carriage came, and she had her hands in cold water up to the elbow, and she did not stand up.\n\nTwo men. Grey coats. Not the same two. One of them had a leather case and the other had a clipboard, and the one with the clipboard did all the talking, and he was, Tessa would remember, perfectly polite.\n\n\"Halloway household?\"\n\n\"Inside,\" Tessa said.\n\n\"Is there a Rooke resident here? No other given name?\"\n\n\"Inside.\"\n\nShe still did not stand up. He waited a moment for her to, and then he went in past her, and the other one went in after him, and Tessa sat on the step with her hands in the bucket and listened.\n\nShe could not make out the words. She made out the shape. A man's voice going on for a long time in the level cadence of somebody reading. Then Mother Halloway, low. Then the man again. Then nothing at all for about a count of thirty.\n\nThen Rooke, very short, three or four words.\n\nThen Halloway again, and Halloway's voice broke in the middle of it, and Tessa put both hands over her ears like a child of six.\n\n---\n\nThey gave her twenty minutes to pack.\n\nTessa stood in the doorway of the back room and watched her do it, and it was the worst twenty minutes of Tessa's life, and afterwards she was ashamed of that, because it was obviously not the worst twenty minutes of Rooke's.\n\nRooke packed like she did everything else. She put the good boots in first because boots go at the bottom. She took two shifts and left three, and Tessa said *take them all*, and Rooke said \"Nettle can grow into these,\" and put them back on the shelf, and Tessa had to go and stand in the passage for a minute.\n\n\"First stratum,\" Tessa said, from the passage.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Out of six hundred and forty-one.\"\n\n\"Out of six hundred and forty-one.\"\n\n\"Are you the top?\"\n\nA pause. \"Yes.\"\n\n\"The actual top? The very top?\"\n\n\"There's a mark for it on the form,\" Rooke said. \"It's a printed form and there's a box on it and someone had to write a number in the box and the number was one. Yes.\"\n\nTessa came back to the doorway. Rooke was folding a shirt with a great deal of attention.\n\n\"Then why do you sound like somebody died,\" Tessa said.\n\nRooke stopped folding.\n\n\"Because I could have had this three days ago,\" she said. \"I could have had this on the first morning without any of it. I sat in that hall and I could have put the true answers down and had this exact form, and I chose not to, and I've had three days to work out that the only thing my choosing bought anybody was three days.\"\n\n\"That's not what happened. They caught you.\"\n\n\"They caught me because I was clever about it,\" Rooke said. \"That's the joke. If I'd been *stupid* about it, if I'd just written any old rubbish down like a normal person having a bad day, nobody would have looked at me twice. I'd be in the ninth with the rest of them and I'd be at the Colvers' tomorrow.\" She put the shirt in the bag. \"I was too good at failing. Do you understand how funny that is? I have thought about almost nothing else for three days and I still cannot decide whether it is funny.\"\n\n\"It isn't,\" said Tessa.\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"Probably not.\"\n\n---\n\nOut on the step Mother Halloway held her by both shoulders and did not say anything useful, because there was nothing useful, and Rooke stood there and let her.\n\nNettle was at the window. Nettle had been told to stay in bed and had not stayed in bed, and Rooke looked up at the window and Nettle put her hand flat on the glass, and Rooke lifted a hand about halfway and then put it down again as though she had thought better of it.\n\nThen she got into the carriage.\n\nShe did not cry.\n\nTessa sat on the step with the wash going cold in the bucket long after the carriage had gone up the lane and turned onto the Wick and out of sight, and the thing that would not leave her alone was that: she did not cry.\n\nBecause Rooke was fourteen, and Tessa was sixteen, and Tessa had cried at everything her whole life, at the fish and at nothing and at the pig they killed on the Row three winters ago, and Rooke had gone up the Rise with a bag holding one pair of boots and two shifts and had not cried, and Tessa understood, sitting there in the cold, that she had never once in six years asked herself what that cost.\n\nThere were now five people in the house and one of them was the eldest.\n\nShe was going to have to learn the pump. She was going to have to learn Marta's ledgers, and she could not hold a column, and Rooke had known that, and had said so out loud in the kitchen three days ago, and had been right.\n\nTessa took her hands out of the water and looked at them, and then she got up and went inside and asked Mother Halloway where Rooke kept the tin.\n\n---\n\n**III. Corvain**\n\"Well,\" said Captain Reeve Ashcombe. \"That's a fine start.\"\n\nHe had the file open on the desk between them and he had read it twice, and he was doing the thing he did with his thumb along his jaw when he was deciding how much of what he thought to say out loud.\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Don't *sir* me, you're insufferable when you're being modest.\" He turned a page. \"First stratum. Out of six hundred and forty-one. And it would have gone down as ninth if you hadn't stood on a gallery for four hours doing what nobody on that gallery has done in a hundred years, which is *look at the children*.\" He shut the file. \"Kell has written to the division. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He has. Two paragraphs, dry as a docket, and one of them says that the officer's observation was of a quality he has not encountered from the enforcement division in thirty years of administering the examination. Do you know what Osric Kell's praise is worth in this city?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Neither does he. That's what makes it worth something.\" Ashcombe pushed the file back across the desk. \"So. Commendation on your record, four months in. Your mother will be unbearable. Now tell me why you look like a woman who has swallowed a wasp.\"\n\nCorvain took the file and did not open it.\n\n\"Because of the amendment,\" she said.\n\n\"Which amendment?\"\n\n\"That's rather my point, sir.\"\n\n---\n\nShe had gone looking for the girl's file. That was all. She had wanted to see the reserve paper, out of nothing more respectable than curiosity, because she had stood at the back of that hall for four hours and watched a fourteen year old work in total silence and she had wanted to know what had been on the page.\n\nThe file had been in the records office at Tallow Street, and it had been complete, and it had been closed, and it had been *fast*. That was the first thing she noticed. Assessment, referral, reserve examination, result, placement, carriage docket, all of it stamped and sequenced and finished inside three days, and Corvain had been an officer for four months but she had been Adelina Corvain's daughter for twenty years and she knew exactly how long paper took to move in this city when nobody was pushing it.\n\nThree days. In the enforcement division, three days was how long it took to get a requisition for lamp oil.\n\nSo she had looked at what else had moved through that office in the same three days, on the reasonable grounds that a clerk who can be made to move fast for one file might have been made to move fast for others.\n\nThere were forty-one amendments.\n\nForty-one. All of them dated within the same three days. All of them the same form, the same four lines, the same stamp. Assessed result. Adverse conduct. Amended result.\n\nAnd every single one of the forty-one had gone *down*.\n\n\"That's not necessarily anything,\" Ashcombe said.\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"Conduct amendments exist. They've existed since the thing was invented. If a child's been banked, it's material. That's not a conspiracy, that's a system with a memory.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. Except.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Except a conduct amendment is meant to be applied at assessment, sir. That's the guidance. Conduct is a factor in the assessment, so it goes in when the paper is marked, and the marked result already accounts for it.\" Corvain put the file down. \"These didn't. These were applied *after*. Every one of the forty-one has an assessed result and then an amended result, and the gap between them is somebody looking at a number they didn't like and changing it.\"\n\nAshcombe was quiet.\n\n\"And there's the distribution,\" Corvain said.\n\n\"The what?\"\n\n\"Forty-one amendments, sir. Forty-one non-human candidates sat the Calibration this year. They were all put in the chapel behind the main hall, which is a thing I also did not know until Tuesday.\" She met his eye. \"It's not a coincidence rate. It's a hundred per cent.\"\n\nThe office was very quiet. Outside, a clatterlift went up the face of the Rise with its cable hauling, and the whole building took the vibration of it for a moment, the way every building on the seam did, forty times a day.\n\n\"Every one of them,\" Ashcombe said.\n\n\"Every one of them. And the one at the top of the pile, the one whose assessed result was highest, took the biggest fall. Fourth stratum down to ninth. A Sirrath boy out of Sootgate on a caution for taking a pressure gauge off a barrow when he was thirteen.\" She paused. \"Sir, I've read his file. He got section nine. There were four hundred human candidates in the main hall who didn't get section nine, and they're all in the strata where the marking put them, and he isn't.\"\n\nAshcombe put both hands flat on the desk.\n\n\"Corvain,\" he said. \"Listen to me very carefully, because I'm going to give you the only piece of advice that has ever been any use to me. In this city, being right and being safe are two entirely separate questions, and you have to answer them in that order but you cannot skip the second one.\" He looked at her. \"Are you certain?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good. That's the right answer.\" He straightened up. \"Then be certain. Properly certain. Not *this looks wrong*, which is what you've got. Certain. Because the moment you take a hundred per cent to anybody above me in this building, somebody is going to ask you whether you've considered that forty-one is a small number and the Under is a rough place and non-human candidates do, statistically, get banked more often, and if you have not got an answer to that question you will spend the rest of your career filing lamp oil requisitions in Sootgate.\"\n\n\"They do get banked more often,\" Corvain said. \"I checked. Three times the rate.\"\n\n\"Then that's the answer they'll give you.\"\n\n\"It isn't an answer, sir. It's a reason. Those are different.\" She picked up the file. \"If conduct is genuinely material and it's genuinely applied at assessment, then a higher bank rate should show up as *lower assessed results*. It doesn't. Their assessed results are all over the strata, same spread as anybody. It only appears afterwards. Whatever's happening in that office is not the system remembering that these children have been banked. It's the system finding out how well they did and then doing something about it.\"\n\nAshcombe looked at her for a long moment.\n\n\"Go and be certain,\" he said. \"Quietly. And Corvain.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"If you're right, you understand what it is you'll be holding.\"\n\n\"A records irregularity, sir.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ashcombe. \"The Calibration is the only thing in Gifford that everybody, top to bottom, brass and rust, actually believes is fair. It's the one honest thing. It's the reason a woman on Ferrow Lane sends her child up those steps instead of putting a brick through a window.\" He turned to the window. \"If it isn't honest, then there is nothing at all holding the lid on this city, and you will be the person who takes the lid off. So be certain.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Silas Vane**\nVane read the strata the way other men read the racing pages, which is to say from the bottom.\n\nHe had them spread on the long table in the back room over the Ashlight with a glass at his elbow and a lamp turned low, and he had the boards' postings, which anybody could get, and he had the amendment dockets, which nobody could get, and had cost him eleven marks and a favour to a clerk with a chalk problem at three houses on the Wick.\n\n\"You're at it again,\" said Growl, from the door.\n\n\"I am always at it. It is the only thing I do.\" Vane did not look up. \"Do you know what the Calibration is, properly? Everyone thinks it is an examination. It is not an examination. It is the single most thorough talent survey conducted anywhere in the civilised world, run at enormous expense, once a year, by people who then throw ninety per cent of the results in a hole.\"\n\n\"Right,\" said Growl.\n\n\"Six hundred and forty-one children, tested to destruction, sorted, ranked and written down. And the brass take the top few dozen up the Rise, and everything below the fourth stratum they hand back to the Under with a card and a shrug.\" Vane turned a page. \"It is the most generous thing they do. They find them for me. They find them, they measure them, and then they tell them they are worthless, and then they let them walk home.\"\n\nGrowl came in and sat down heavily on the bench by the stove. He was Sirrath, older than Marrow by ten years and rougher by considerably more than that, and he had come down off the same boat as half of Sootgate and had never once pretended he was going to do anything other than this.\n\n\"So who is it this year,\" he said.\n\n\"Three worth the walk.\" Vane laid a finger on the sheet. \"A girl in the fifth out of Grieves who put down a method in section eight that the examiner has annotated as *unorthodox but sound*, which is the closest thing to a love letter you will ever find on a Council form. A Laachmi girl in the sixth. And this.\"\n\nHe turned the docket round.\n\n\"Marrow,\" said Growl. \"Sootgate. I know him a bit. Ketu's lodger.\"\n\n\"Assessed fourth stratum,\" said Vane. \"Amended ninth.\"\n\nGrowl leaned over and read it, and Vane watched him read it, and watched the exact moment the older Sirrath's jaw set, because Vane had chosen to have this conversation with Growl rather than with any of the other four men available to him for precisely that reason.\n\n\"Prior conduct,\" Growl said.\n\n\"A caution. Age thirteen. A pressure gauge.\"\n\n\"Turn take them.\"\n\n\"Quite.\" Vane sat back and picked up his glass. \"Now. Understand what I am asking you to do, because I do not want it done clumsily. I am not asking you to recruit that boy. If you go down to Sootgate tonight and offer that boy a place with us, he will spit in your eye, and he will be right to, because he has spent two years believing the thing they told him in that hall and he is not going to stop believing it in three days.\"\n\n\"So what.\"\n\n\"So you are going to do nothing at all,\" said Vane. \"You are going to be somewhere he can find you. You are going to be pleasant, and unhurried, and you are going to say one true thing to him and then you are going to leave.\"\n\n\"Which true thing?\"\n\nVane thought about it.\n\n\"That the brass have already told him what he is worth,\" he said. \"And that he can spend the next forty years asking them to change their minds. Nothing else. Not the work, not the money, not us. He will do the rest himself. They always do the rest themselves; that is the beauty of the arrangement.\" He drained the glass. \"The Council makes them, Growl. I only collect.\"\n\nGrowl stood.\n\n\"And if he doesn't come?\"\n\n\"Then he doesn't come, and he cleans stairs on the Rise for thirty years like the woman who took him in, and in due course somebody hands his own child a card with a nine on it.\" Vane turned back to the sheets. \"Go on. There's no hurry. He'll do something stupid within the week and then he'll need somewhere to be.\"\n\nGrowl was at the door when the boy came up the stairs at a run to say there was a whistle going on Cinder Row and a Council board out on the cobbles in nine thousand pieces.\n\nVane did not look up from the sheets.\n\n\"Under the week,\" he said. \"Well. He is bright.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. The Patrol on Cinder Row**\n**Tracker Sergeant Davin Cross** did not run at first. That was the whole of his method and he had never seen any reason to improve on it.\n\nHe came onto Cinder Row nine minutes after the whistle, from the Wick end, on foot, with his coat open and his hands empty, and he stood over the glass for a moment and looked at what was left in the frame and at the pattern the pieces had made on the cobbles, and then he looked up the Row and down it.\n\nThen he began to walk.\n\nCross was forty-four years old and had been with the enforcement division for twenty-two of them, and for the last nine he had held a warrant that read *tracker* rather than *constable*, which meant that when the Under produced somebody who was genuinely difficult to find, the division sent for him and stopped asking questions about his methods. He did not carry a baton. He had never in his career been in a foot race he had lost, because he had never in his career been in a foot race.\n\nWhat Cross did was funnel.\n\nYou cannot outrun a man in a city, but a city is not open ground. A city is a set of choices, and there are far fewer of them than the man running believes. Cinder Row had two ends and eleven side-turnings, and of the eleven, four were dead, three came back out onto the Row itself, one went up to the Wick where there were four constables standing about because there had been a whistle, and three went down into the sump lanes toward the outfalls.\n\nSo there were three choices. Cross knew that before he took his first step. The man running did not.\n\nHe walked up the Row at an unhurried pace, and he watched the windows, and he watched the doorways, and about two thirds of the way along, without breaking stride, he said conversationally to the alley on his right:\n\n\"That's a bad turning, son. It comes back out forty feet up.\"\n\nSilence. Then a scrape.\n\nThen Marrow came out of the alley going flat out the other way, and Cross began, at last, to run.\n\n---\n\nMarrow ran like something out of a bad dream, which is to say he ran extremely fast and had no idea where he was going.\n\nThat was the trouble and he knew it and he could not fix it. He knew Sootgate. He knew Sootgate the way he knew his own hands, every wall of it, and he could have lost this man in Sootgate in four minutes. He was not in Sootgate. He was in Cinder Row, eleven streets east, because he had walked here without choosing a direction, and the geography under his feet was a stranger's.\n\nHe went right, down into the sump lanes, because down was away from the Wick and away from the whistles, and because down smelled of water and water meant outfalls and outfalls meant holes in walls.\n\nBehind him, footsteps. Not fast. Steady.\n\nThat was somehow worse.\n\nHe came out into a yard with a dead pump in the middle of it and four ways out, and he stopped for two breaths, and he thought, with the part of his brain that had never once stopped working no matter what the rest of him was doing:\n\n*He is not trying to catch me. He is choosing which way I go.*\n\nMarrow put his back against the pump housing and made himself stand still.\n\nHe counted. Four ways out. He had come in by one, so three. The one on the left went uphill and lightward, back toward the Wick, which was where the constables were, and therefore that was the one Cross wanted, which meant that was the one Cross would leave open.\n\nThe middle one was dark and he did not know it.\n\nThe right-hand one went down toward the outfalls, and he did not know that either, but he could smell it. Wet stone and rust and, under that, hot.\n\nHot.\n\nMarrow's head came up.\n\nHe had counted the knock in the pipe every day of his life the way everybody did. It was going now, under the yard, under his feet: *knock, knock, knock, knock,* and the long shudder.\n\nThe vent.\n\nFour knocks and a shudder meant a vent coming, and he had heard the shudder somewhere back on the Row, which meant he had, what, thirty seconds? Twenty? He had never in his life had cause to time it to the second, because nobody times a thing they have never needed. He knew the sequence and not the interval.\n\nThat was the whole problem, and he had it in one clean piece: *I know the order of events and not the spacing of them, and I have to act on the spacing.*\n\nSo work it out.\n\nHe knew the vent went at four bells and four times a day and he knew, because you cannot live in the Under and not know, that the fog took about forty counts to fill a lane and about a hundred and fifty to clear. He knew he had heard the shudder while he was still on the Row and that he had been running since, and running badly, and that he could cover the length of the Row in about sixty counts flat out.\n\nHe knew the fog came out of the outfalls. Everybody knew that. It came up out of the grates and it came out of the low arches by the water and it filled the sump lanes first because the sump lanes were lowest, and *then* it went up.\n\nWhich meant the right-hand turning, the one going down, the one that smelled hot, was about to become the only place in this district where a man who does not know the ground could stand ten feet from another man and not see him.\n\nAnd Marrow could not see in it either. He would be as blind as Cross.\n\nBut Cross needed to see him to funnel him. Marrow only needed to not be seen.\n\nFootsteps in the entry behind him, still unhurried.\n\n\"You've stopped,\" said Cross's voice, from somewhere close. \"That's the first sensible thing you've done. Stay stopped, son, and this is a board and a fine. Run again and I'll put conduct on you, and you'll not like what conduct does to a lad in the ninth.\"\n\nMarrow shut his eyes and made himself count.\n\n*Not yet. Not yet.*\n\n\"You there?\"\n\n*Not yet.*\n\nThe knock came up through the soles of his boots, and the yard drain by the dead pump made a sound like something very large clearing its throat.\n\nMarrow went right, and down, and into the dark hot mouth of the outfall lane, and behind him he heard Cross start to move properly for the first time, fast, faster than Marrow had expected, and much closer.\n\nThen the vent let go under Sootgate and Cinder Row and the whole eastern Under, and forty seconds of white came up out of the ground.\n\n---\n\nCross stood in the middle of it with his hand on a wall and did not move at all, because a man who moves in vent-fog in a sump lane he does not personally own is a man who goes into the water.\n\nHe waited it out. It took ninety counts to thin and another eighty to clear, and when it cleared the lane was empty in both directions and there were four ways out of it that had not been on the survey he had memorised that morning, because the survey had been drawn in the year the Row was built and the outfalls had been rebuilt twice since.\n\nHe stood there a while longer.\n\nThen he went back up to the Row, and stood over the glass, and took out his notebook, and wrote, in a small neat hand:\n\n*Sirrath, male, white pelt, sixteen or thereabouts, six foot four. Cinder Row, board, glass. Ran east then down. Used the vent.*\n\nHe looked at that last line for a moment.\n\nThen he underlined it, because in twenty-two years he had banked nine hundred people in the Under and not one of them had ever thought of it, and a man in his profession learns to write down the day the ground changes under him.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Two.*" },
  { n: 3, title: "Two Worlds", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe room had a bed in it and nothing else living, and that was the problem.\n\nRooke had been at the University of the Engine for six days and she had slept for perhaps eleven hours of them, and the reason was not the bed, which was better than any bed she had ever been in, and it was not the food, which was so plentiful that on the second day she had been quietly sick from it. The reason was that at night the room was silent.\n\nThere was no cough. There was no Tam talking in his sleep. There was no Pip climbing in at the foot of the bed at second bell because Pip always climbed in at the foot of the bed at second bell and Rooke had spent four years pretending to be asleep when it happened so that Pip would not be embarrassed about it. There was no knock in the pipe, because the buildings on the Rise had their pipes lagged in felt and boxed in behind panelling so that the Turn could not be heard by anybody who mattered.\n\nThey had taken the sound of the Engine out of the walls. They had a hundred and three years of claiming they alone could hear it and they had paid good money to have it silenced in their bedrooms.\n\nRooke lay in the dark and listened to nothing and did the sum she did every night now, which was: two marks a week rent, four and a half marks a week for six people to eat if Tessa shopped as well as Rooke had shopped, which she would not, so five, plus coke, plus the chalk at Marta's, against whatever Tessa and Tam could bring in, which was two marks in a good week.\n\nIt came out at a shortfall of five marks a week and no eleven marks for syrup, ever, on any timeline.\n\nSix days. She had run it about forty times.\n\n---\n\n\"You're not eating,\" said Ivy Thorncastle.\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"You had a roll.\"\n\n\"I had two rolls.\" Rooke put her spoon down. \"I'm not being difficult. I got ill on Tuesday from eating too much and now I'm frightened of it, which is the stupidest thing that has ever happened to me, and I would rather not discuss it in a room with two hundred people in it.\"\n\nIvy considered this. She was fifteen, small, ferociously untidy in a way that took real effort in a place where everybody's clothes were pressed by somebody else, and she had sat down next to Rooke at supper on the first evening for no reason that Rooke had been able to establish since.\n\n\"That's not stupid,\" Ivy said. \"That's a perfectly ordinary physiological response to a change in caloric intake. My aunt could tell you the whole of it. She'd draw you a graph.\"\n\n\"Your aunt.\"\n\n\"Professor Thorncastle.\"\n\nRooke turned and looked at her properly for the first time in six days.\n\n\"Your aunt is Thorncastle,\" she said. \"The Thorncastle who runs the second-year reckoning.\"\n\n\"And the third and the fourth, and she is not going to be pleased to hear you put it in that order. Yes.\" Ivy went back to her supper. \"Before you ask, no, I didn't sit next to you because she told me to. She doesn't know I know who you are. Nobody's supposed to know who you are, they've been very correct about it, and I found out on Monday because I read the placement docket upside down on her desk while she was talking to me about my own results, which were mediocre.\" She said it without any self-pity at all. \"I'm going to be a very good historian and a very bad reckoner and everyone in my family has decided to be gracious about that, which is worse than shouting.\"\n\n\"Then why did you sit next to me?\"\n\n\"Because everyone else was looking at you and nobody was talking to you,\" Ivy said, \"and I have been the person nobody talks to at supper, and it is horrible, and I decided at about the age of nine that when I could stop it happening to somebody I would.\"\n\nRooke looked at her plate for a while.\n\n\"That's the first honest thing anybody's said to me since the carriage,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes, well. The Rise doesn't go in for it much.\" Ivy tore a roll in half and put the larger half on Rooke's plate without appearing to notice she had done it. \"You'll get used to that. Or you won't, and you'll be much more interesting.\"\n\n---\n\nThe diagnostic was on the seventh day, in a small room on the north side with two slate boards and a window looking down the face of the Rise into brown fog, and Professor Linnea Thorncastle set it in front of her and then sat down in the corner with a book and did not look up once for three hours.\n\nRooke had expected an examination. It was not an examination. It was eleven problems on eleven separate sheets and they were not in order of difficulty, and by the third sheet she understood that they were not in order of anything, that they had been chosen the way you choose keys off a ring when you do not know which door you are dealing with.\n\nShe did nine of them.\n\nThe tenth she got most of the way into and then found she was walking in a circle, and she sat with it for forty minutes and then wrote, underneath her working, in her small flat hand: *This is wrong from the fourth line and I cannot see where. The method should hold and it does not, so I have got a thing backwards. I would like to be told which thing.*\n\nThe eleventh she read four times and did not touch at all.\n\nShe turned it over and wrote on the back: *I do not know what this is asking. I do not know most of these words. I know it is not arithmetic and I do not think it is geometry either. If it is the true calculus then I have never seen the true calculus and I would like somebody to say so plainly instead of assuming I have.*\n\nThen she squared the eleven sheets to the edge of the desk and said, \"I'm finished.\"\n\nThorncastle put the book down.\n\nShe read the nine correct ones in about four minutes, which Rooke found insulting until she understood, watching the old woman's eyes, that she was not reading them, she was checking them, the way you check that a wall is straight by looking along it.\n\nThen she read the tenth. She spent a long time on the tenth.\n\nThen she turned over the eleventh and read the back of it, and she sat completely still for a moment, and then she laughed once, sharply, with no humour in it whatsoever.\n\n\"Well,\" said Professor Thorncastle. \"God help us all.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\"\n\n\"Sit down, child.\"\n\n\"I am sitting down.\"\n\n\"So you are.\" Thorncastle got up and went to the window and stood with her back to the room. \"The tenth. You have got a thing backwards, and the thing is that you have assumed the relation holds in both directions, and it does not, and there is no possible way you could have known that because it is not written down anywhere you would ever have been able to get at. I will teach you in ten minutes on Thursday. That is not the interesting part.\"\n\n\"What's the interesting part.\"\n\n\"You knew you were wrong.\"\n\nRooke waited.\n\n\"Do you understand how rare that is?\" Thorncastle turned round. \"I have taught in this building for thirty-one years. Every year they send me the finest young minds in Gifford, tutored from the age of six, and when they hit a wall they do one of two things. They put down an answer they know is rubbish and hope I am too busy to notice, or they put down nothing and tell me afterwards that they had run out of time. In thirty-one years, I have had four students write *I am wrong and I cannot see where and I would like to be told*. Four.\" She came back to the desk. \"It is the single most useful sentence in the entire discipline and almost nobody can say it, because to say it you have to be able to hold two things at once: that you are capable, and that you are wrong. The Rise raises children who can hold the first one. It does not, on the whole, produce the second.\"\n\n\"Where I'm from,\" Rooke said, \"if you're wrong about a pump you get wet.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" Thorncastle sat down opposite her. \"Yes, I imagine that is exactly it.\"\n\nShe picked up the eleventh sheet.\n\n\"Now this. This is the part where I have to be careful, and you should watch me being careful, because you will want to know later that I was.\" She laid it flat. \"You are right. It is not arithmetic and it is not geometry. It is the true calculus. Or rather, it is a problem set in the notation of the true calculus, which is not the same thing and I want you to notice that I made that distinction.\"\n\n\"I noticed.\"\n\n\"Good. You have never seen it because it is not taught below the fourth year and it is not published, and the reason given is that it is dangerous in untrained hands, and the reason is nonsense, and I have said so in writing twice and been thanked for my contribution.\" Thorncastle's mouth was thin. \"You will begin on it in the spring. You will find it easy. I am telling you now, before you find out, because I would rather you heard it from somebody who likes you: you will find it easy, and a great many people in this building will find that very difficult indeed, and one or two of them will find it intolerable.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you are fourteen and from Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"That isn't a reason.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Thorncastle. \"It's a cause. There's a difference and I suspect you know it.\"\n\nRooke sat back.\n\n\"Professor.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"On the docket for my placement. Is there a stipend?\"\n\nThorncastle blinked. It was, Rooke noted, the only time in three hours that she had managed to knock the woman off her line.\n\n\"There is a maintenance allowance,\" she said. \"Fourteen marks a term. It is intended for books and coal and boots.\"\n\n\"Fourteen marks a term.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Four terms a year.\" Rooke did the sum out loud, flatly, because she had already done it and wanted to hear somebody else confirm it. \"Fifty-six marks a year. And I'm here seven years. Three hundred and ninety-two marks, and I don't need books because the library's free and I don't need coal because the rooms are heated and I have boots.\"\n\n\"Child—\"\n\n\"How much of it can I send home?\"\n\nThorncastle looked at her for a long moment. Behind her, out of the window, a clatterlift went down the face of the Rise with its cable hauling, taking somebody down into the brown.\n\n\"All of it,\" she said. \"And it will not be enough, will it.\"\n\n\"Fourteen marks a term is three marks a week and a bit,\" said Rooke. \"They're five short. So no. It's most of the way and it isn't enough, and I've had six days to think about it and I can't make the number bigger by thinking.\"\n\n\"So what will you do?\"\n\n\"Find something that pays,\" said Rooke. \"I always have.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\nHe was three days at Sister Maude's before he could put weight on the ankle, and the ankle was the least of it.\n\nHe had gone into the outfall lane blind and come out of it into a culvert he did not know, and somewhere in the middle of that he had put his foot into a gap between two courses of brick that the fog had hidden, and gone down hard, and had then got up and gone another mile and a half on it because the alternative was Cross. By the time he came up out of the sump lanes into Sootgate the ankle had gone the colour of a bad plum and he could not remember the last four streets.\n\nHe did not go to Ketu's. He was very clear about that afterwards, when people asked. He had stood at the end of her lane in the dark for a long time and had worked out, with the part of his brain that would not stop, that a Sirrath with a fresh bank on him and a Council board in nine thousand pieces behind him was a thing you did not carry into the house of a woman who had scrubbed stairs on the Rise for thirty years and had never once been in trouble.\n\nSo he went to Maude's, because everybody went to Maude's, and Maude asked him no questions at all.\n\nThat was the whole method of the place. Eight iron cots in a room over a dyer's yard, mismatched blankets, a pot-bellied stove, a shelf of bottles, damp on the ceiling in the shape of a country nobody had ever named. Maude took your name if you offered it and did not ask twice if you did not, and she strapped the ankle and told him it was a bad sprain and not a break, and that he would be walking on it in four days and running on it in three weeks and that if he ran on it in four days he would be lame at forty.\n\n\"Understood,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"You'll do it anyway.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" She pulled the strapping tight with a jerk that made him hiss. \"Everybody does. There's a slate on the wall behind you with the names of about two hundred people who did. I scrub one off every few weeks.\"\n\n---\n\nOn the fourth day, when he could get to the stove and back, he sat on the end of the cot with his foot up and did the sum.\n\nHe had eight grist in his coat and no writ and a ninth stratum against his name in a ledger that would follow him until he died, and a bank he had not yet been caught for. That was the position.\n\nThe trade options for a ninth stratum Sirrath in Sootgate were four, and he had known all four of them since he was eleven, the way you know the exits from a room.\n\nThere was the drop-forges, where the Laachmi worked, and they would take him because he was big and there was always a place for big, and it paid three marks a week and took your hearing in nine years and your lungs in fifteen.\n\nThere was portering on the seam. Two marks and a bit, and you were on your feet fourteen hours, and the seam was where the brass could see you, so you got stopped twice a week.\n\nThere was the water gangs in the outfalls, which paid five marks because people drowned.\n\nAnd there was Ketu's trade, which was to go up the Rise every morning at fourth bell and clean a house belonging to people who would not learn your name, for two marks a week and whatever was left on the plates, for thirty years, until your knees went.\n\nThat was the list. That was the whole list. Two years of Ketu's kitchen table and a stolen syllabus and the load across the arch, and the list had not changed by so much as a single item.\n\nMarrow sat with his foot up and understood, with a cold clarity that was worse than the anger, that the amendment had not taken anything away from him. It had simply told him the truth about what he had never had.\n\n\"You're the one broke the board on Cinder Row,\" said a voice.\n\nHe looked up.\n\nThe Sirrath in the doorway was ten years older than him and a good deal rougher, orange-brown, one ear notched, a heavy seam of scar tissue across the muzzle. He had a coat on that was too good for Maude's and he was standing in the door in the specific way that people stood in doors when they had been told not to come in.\n\n\"No,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Right.\" The older Sirrath came in anyway and sat down on the next cot, which creaked. \"Growl. I know Ketu. Cleaned for the Fenwicks on Aster Row about the time my mother did. Small people, small houses.\"\n\nMarrow said nothing.\n\n\"You've an amendment,\" Growl said.\n\n\"Everybody in that room had an amendment.\"\n\n\"Forty-one of you. Yeah.\" Growl leaned back against the wall. \"I had one. Nineteen years ago. Assessed sixth. Amended tenth. Prior conduct.\" He held up his hand and turned it over. \"I was thirteen and I hit a man who had hold of my sister's arm, and there's not one word about the arm anywhere on the docket, and there's four lines about the hitting.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"So you're here to recruit me,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows what the Ashlight is.\"\n\n\"Then you know I'd be wasting an afternoon.\" Growl did not move from the wall. \"You've had it three days. You still think there's a version of this where you write a letter to somebody and it gets put right. I'm not going to argue you out of that, because nobody argued me out of it either. Took me about two years.\"\n\n\"Then why are you here.\"\n\nGrowl thought about it. He seemed to be genuinely thinking, which Marrow had not expected.\n\n\"Because the man who runs the Ashlight sent me to say one thing to you and then leave,\" he said, \"and I've decided I'm going to say a different thing, and he'll not know, and if you ever repeat it I'll say you're a liar.\"\n\n\"Go on then.\"\n\n\"The thing he wanted me to say is that the brass have already told you what you're worth, and you can spend forty years asking them to change their minds.\" Growl stood up. \"It's true. That's what makes him good at this. Everything he says to you is going to be true and it's going to be true the way a hook is a piece of metal.\"\n\nHe went to the door.\n\n\"So what's yours,\" said Marrow.\n\nGrowl stopped.\n\n\"Get to Bramwell's,\" he said. \"Off Kettle Yard, behind the dye works, door with a gear nailed to it. Old man, mad as a bag of cats, been cogging Engine parts for fifty years and never once been banked for it. He'll shout at you. Let him.\" He put his hand on the frame. \"He's the last honest room in the Under and there's a reason for that, and the reason is that Vane's asked him four times and been told no four times, and nobody's ever worked out why Vane lets him get away with it.\"\n\n\"Why does he?\"\n\n\"Nobody knows. That's the point of telling you.\" Growl looked back over his shoulder. \"You'll come to us in the end. I'd put money on it and I'm not being unkind, I'd put money on it because I did. But you've maybe got a year, and a year's a year, and there's a thing you can learn in Bramwell's shop that you'll never learn in ours.\"\n\n\"What thing?\"\n\n\"How to make something,\" said Growl, \"instead of how to take it.\"\n\nThen he went out, and Marrow heard him go down the stairs, and heard him stop halfway and say something to Sister Maude in a voice that had entirely changed, and heard the chink of coins going into the box by the door.\n\nMore than eight grist, by the sound.\n\n---\n\n**III. Clerk Pemberton**\nThe trouble with the young noblewoman was that she was polite.\n\nPemberton had worked the examinations records office at Tallow Street for six years and he had developed, in that time, a complete and reliable defence against every category of person who came to his hatch. There were the angry ones, and you gave them the form and the words *there's no appeal on an amendment* and let them shout at the glass. There were the crying ones, and you looked at the docket while they did it. There were the clever ones who tried to catch you out on procedure, and procedure was Pemberton's entire body of knowledge and they never won.\n\nAnd then there was this one, who came in on the Tuesday, and did not shout, and did not cry, and did not try to catch him out. She asked him about seasonal variation in passing rates.\n\n\"Seasonal,\" said Pemberton.\n\n\"Year on year.\" Officer Corvain had her elbows on the ledge of the hatch, which nobody did, and she was looking at him as though he were interesting, which nobody did either. \"The Calibration's a hundred and three years old. There must be variation. Some years the cohort's stronger, some years it's weaker.\"\n\n\"There's variation, yes.\"\n\n\"How much?\"\n\n\"That'd be in the returns.\"\n\n\"Where are the returns?\"\n\n\"Bound. Annually.\" Pemberton heard himself say it and heard how helpful it sounded, and could not for the life of him work out why he had said it that way. \"There's a set in the reading room upstairs. Last thirty years.\"\n\n\"Only thirty?\"\n\n\"Reading room set's thirty. It's what people ask for.\"\n\n\"And the rest?\"\n\nAnd there it was.\n\nPemberton had a small internal experience at this point which he would think about, on and off, for the rest of his life. What happened was that he opened his mouth to say *the rest are in store*, which was true, and completely unremarkable, and the answer he had given perhaps a hundred times to researchers and clerks and one persistent old man from the Historical Society, and instead what came out was:\n\n\"There's a full set.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Down.\"\n\n\"Down.\"\n\n\"Sub-level. It's not a reading room.\" He was talking too fast now and he knew it. \"It's store. You'd need a requisition.\"\n\n\"From whom?\"\n\n\"Grandmaster's office.\"\n\n\"Which takes how long?\"\n\n\"Eleven days.\"\n\n\"Eleven days,\" said Corvain. \"For a bound volume of numbers that are posted on eleven boards outside this building every summer where anybody can read them.\"\n\n\"That's the procedure.\"\n\n\"I'm not arguing with you,\" she said, and she did not sound as though she were. \"I'm trying to understand why a set of published figures needs eleven days and a Grandmaster's signature. Because that's unusual. In the enforcement division I can requisition a murder file in four days and the murder file has people's names in it.\"\n\n\"I don't set the procedure.\"\n\n\"No.\" She straightened up off the ledge. \"Mr Pemberton, is it?\"\n\n\"Pemberton.\"\n\n\"Mr Pemberton. What's actually down there?\"\n\nAnd Pemberton, who had a complete and reliable defence against every category of person who came to his hatch, said: \"Nothing. Returns. Just returns.\"\n\nHe said it a fraction of a second too fast and about two degrees too loud, and he watched her hear it, and he watched her decide to be kind about having heard it, which was the worst part of the whole exchange.\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Officer Corvain. \"You've been very helpful.\"\n\nShe went out. Pemberton stood at the hatch for some time.\n\nThen he went and sat down at his desk with his ledger open in front of him and the periodical folded inside it, and he did not read either, and what he thought about was this:\n\n*She doesn't want the returns. Nobody has ever wanted the returns. She wanted to know whether there was somewhere the returns were kept that wasn't the reading room. And I told her.*\n\nHe thought about the vault door on the sub-level, three feet of iron with the dial grid on the face of it, and the little brass spring clip below the grid where the current docket slip sat, filled in in pencil, changed every quarter, so that the four clerks who actually had to go down there twice a week could get in without troubling anybody in the true calculus about it.\n\nHe thought about the fact that in six years nobody had ever once asked him how he got in.\n\nThen he thought, with a genuine and rising sense of grievance, that this was not fair, that he had done nothing at all except answer a question honestly, that he was a clerk on eleven marks a week with a chalk at three houses on the Wick, and that whatever was about to happen in this building was going to happen to him first and to the people who had built the vault last.\n\nHe got up and went to find the quarter's docket slip and change it early.\n\nHe was two hours too late.\n\n---\n\n**IV. Chancellor Isolde Prewitt**\nThere were two items on the informal list, which was the list Prewitt kept in her head and had never once written down in twelve years.\n\nThe first was a name.\n\n*Rooke. No other given. Halloway household, Ferrow Lane. Candidate 4471. Assessed ninth stratum on the original paper by deliberate concealment. First stratum on the reserve. Highest reserve score in the recorded history of the examination.*\n\nPrewitt had read Thorncastle's preliminary note twice. It was four lines long and Linnea Thorncastle had been careful in it, which was itself informative, because Thorncastle was not a careful woman and did not become one by accident.\n\n*The candidate's aptitude is not in question. I would prefer not to characterise it further in writing until I have taught her a full term.*\n\nPrewitt turned that sentence over for a while.\n\nYou did not decline to characterise a fourteen year old in writing because she was merely very good. Very good was a thing you put in a report, because very good reflected credit on you. You declined to characterise her in writing when you had looked at what she could do and had understood that the number, written down, would be read by people who would draw a conclusion from it that you were not yet prepared to have drawn.\n\nThere was only one such conclusion available.\n\nThe Wrought held Gifford on a single proposition, which was that the descendants of the Twelve were born able to hear the Turn, and that this was why they must govern it. It had been true of the Twelve. It had been broadly true for two generations after. It had been getting less true for eighty years, in the ordinary way of these things, because a gift does not stay in a bloodline any more than a fortune does; it thins out, and reverts, and the great-grandchildren of geniuses are for the most part perfectly pleasant people who are good at tennis.\n\nPrewitt knew this. Every Chancellor for sixty years had known it. It was the central fact of her working life and it was managed, carefully, annually, by the Calibration, which went out into the city and found the children who could actually do the work and brought them up the Rise and gave them a gown and a title and, in due course, a name that sounded as though it had always been there.\n\nThe system worked. It had worked for a hundred and three years. Prewitt had no moral difficulty with it whatsoever; she had watched what happened in Vaskeld when their council fell and their engine stopped, and she had read the number, and the number was forty thousand.\n\nThe difficulty was one of degree.\n\nA promoted Rustbound child who was very good could be absorbed. It happened every year. Four or five of them, sanded down, married off, absorbed.\n\nA promoted Rustbound child who was demonstrably better at the true calculus than any living member of the Wrought was not an absorption. She was a proof. She was a walking, breathing, fourteen year old proof that the proposition on which the city rested was false, and the only thing standing between that proof and everybody who might draw it was that nobody had yet written the number down.\n\nPrewitt made a small note in her head against the name and moved on.\n\n---\n\nThe second item was also a name, and it annoyed her more, because it was avoidable.\n\n*Corvain, E. Enforcement division, four months. Adelina's girl.*\n\nTwo enquiries at the examinations records office in five days. One requisition submitted for the full historical returns and refused by the Grandmaster's office on procedural grounds without ever reaching Prewitt's desk, which was the system working correctly. One informal conversation with a records clerk.\n\nAnd, this morning, a note from Reeve Ashcombe, who was a good officer and an honest man and who had committed the only unforgivable error available to an honest man in a position of moderate authority, which was to put a thing in writing in order to protect the person who had told him.\n\n*Officer Corvain has identified an anomaly in the application of conduct amendments arising from this year's Calibration and has my authority to establish the facts. I judge her competent and the matter narrow. I would rather it were resolved within the division than raised elsewhere.*\n\nPrewitt read that three times, and each time she liked Ashcombe a little more and found her position a little worse.\n\nBecause he had done exactly the right thing. He had contained it, minuted it, put his own name on it and quietly signalled upward that he intended to handle it himself and would appreciate not being interfered with. It was the act of a decent man managing a subordinate he believed in. And its effect, entirely unintended, was that there was now a piece of paper in this building with the words *anomaly in the application of conduct amendments* on it, signed by a captain.\n\nOnce a thing is minuted it cannot be un-minuted. It can only be answered.\n\nPrewitt sat for a while with her hands folded on the closed ledger.\n\nThere were four ways to answer it and she went through them the way she went through everything, which was slowly and without any particular feeling.\n\nShe could have the girl transferred. Cheap, quick, and it converted a curious officer into an aggrieved one, and aggrieved officers talk.\n\nShe could have the amendments this year quietly reversed. Elegant. It would fix the immediate problem, and forty-one non-human candidates would go up the strata, and it would establish for all time that amendments could be reversed, which would produce, within three years, an appeals process, which would produce within ten years a public argument about what conduct amendments were actually for.\n\nShe could give the girl the answer. The real one. Which was madness and she noted it only to be thorough.\n\nOr she could let her have it.\n\nPrewitt considered the fourth option for a long time, and the shape of it pleased her, in the abstract, the way a well-turned proof pleases anybody.\n\nLet the girl work. Let her be right. Let her establish, over some months, at some cost, with real evidence, that the Calibration is managed. And then, at the moment she brings it in, be the person who receives it.\n\nBecause a young woman with a discovery has two futures and only two. She can be destroyed by it, which makes a martyr and a story. Or she can be believed, and thanked, and told the truth, and shown the number from Vaskeld, and given a chair in the room where the thing is decided.\n\nAnd a person who has been given a chair in the room does not take the lid off the city. A person in the room becomes, within about eighteen months, the most careful custodian of the whole arrangement, because they have seen the alternative and they have signed their name to something.\n\nThat was how Prewitt had come to this desk. Twenty-two years ago. She remembered the afternoon very clearly, and she remembered the man who had shown her the number, and she remembered walking out onto the terrace afterwards and being sick into a flowerbed, and she remembered that by the following spring she had stopped thinking about it more than once a week.\n\nShe reached for the bell to call her secretary, and then did not ring it.\n\nLet her work, then.\n\nBut not down there.\n\nPrewitt drew a single sheet toward her and wrote, in her own hand, six words to the Grandmaster's office, requesting that the sub-level store at Tallow Street be reviewed for its suitability as a records repository at the earliest convenience.\n\nShe dated it. She sanded it. She put it in the tray.\n\nIt would go out on Thursday, and the review would take about nine days, and by the middle of the following month the historical returns would be in eleven crates in a locked room in the Council building with a proper requisition process, and Officer Corvain would be able to look at them any time she liked, once she had asked.\n\nAt the time Prewitt put the note in the tray it was twenty past six in the evening on the Wednesday.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Sealed Archive**\nThe corridor was not on any plan of the building that Corvain had been able to find, which was not sinister; there were four sub-levels under Tallow Street and none of them were on anything, because the building had been extended eleven times in a hundred years and nobody had ever redrawn it.\n\nShe had come down the back stair at half past ten with a candle, a valve-key she had bought that afternoon in the Under for four grist, and her own coat turned inside out so the buttons did not show, which she had thought was rather clever until she had got to the bottom and realised the coat was grey on both sides.\n\nShe was, she was aware, committing a criminal offence. She had decided about that on the stairs and had come to the conclusion that she was going to do it anyway and that it was important to be honest with herself about which of the two possible reasons was operating. The first reason was that a hundred and forty children a year were having their futures amended in a back office. The second reason was that she wanted to know.\n\nThe second one was the true one. Corvain thought that was probably how most people ended up doing the right thing, and that it did not make it less right, and that she would think about the rest of it later.\n\nThen she came round the turn and the candle found the door and she stopped walking.\n\nIt was three feet thick and it filled the corridor. Iron, black with age, and set into the face of it in six rows was a grid of brass dial housings, each dial numbered nought to nine, thirty-six of them, worn bright at the thumb-hold from a century of use.\n\nAbove the grid, cut in a script so small and so weathered she had to hold the candle to it, three lines:\n\n*When the Engine ticks, the city keeps.*\n*When the birds fly, time moves.*\n*When the bells ring, all hear.*\n\nCorvain stood in front of it for a long time.\n\nThen she said, out loud, in the empty corridor: \"Oh, you absolute *frauds*.\"\n\n---\n\nShe had spent the walk down there braced for exactly this. She had spent it telling herself the thing she had been telling herself since Tuesday, which was that she was not a reckoner, that she had come fourth in her year at a very expensive school at a subject she was competent at and no more, that Rooke of Ferrow Lane would look at a lock like this and open it in ninety seconds and Elspeth Corvain would look at it and see thirty-six brass dials and a poem.\n\nAnd that was true. She looked at it and she saw thirty-six brass dials and a poem, and she could not begin to touch it, and she knew she could not.\n\nWhich was the entire point.\n\nBecause Corvain had stood on a gallery for four hours and watched six hundred children take an examination, and she had spent five days in a records office, and she had learned one thing about this city that she had not known before, and it was this: the people who guard the Wrought's secrets are not Wrought.\n\nThey are clerks.\n\nThere were four clerks who went down to this sub-level, twice a week, to file the returns and fetch them. Pemberton was one of them. Pemberton had a ledger with a periodical folded inside it and a chalk at three houses on the Wick and eleven marks a week, and Pemberton had come fourth or fifth stratum at his Calibration twenty years ago and had never seen the true calculus in his life and never would.\n\nPemberton could not open that lock. Not on his best day, not with a week and a slate.\n\nAnd Pemberton went down there twice a week.\n\nCorvain brought the candle down and held it low, under the grid, and looked at the bottom of the door.\n\nThere was a small brass plate screwed to the iron. Mundane, functional, obviously added long after the door was made, the screws a different metal and a different age. In the plate, a spring clip. In the clip, a folded paper slip about the size of a playing card, greasy at the edges from handling.\n\nShe took it out.\n\nIt was a docket slip. She had seen five hundred of them in the last five days. Printed heading, *EXAMINATIONS OFFICE, TALLOW STREET*, a box for the quarter, a box for the series and a long ruled line, and on the line, in pencil, in a small tired hand:\n\n**Q3 — 114 / 2260 / 0709 / 6631 / 1188 / 4471**\n\nCorvain read the last four figures three times.\n\nThen she laughed, once, in the dark, and put her hand over her mouth because the sound went a very long way in a stone corridor.\n\nSix groups of four. Thirty-six dials in six rows of six. No. Six rows, and the numbers were four long, which meant, which meant—\n\nShe held the candle up again and counted properly instead of guessing, which was, she would reflect later, the single most useful thing anyone had ever taught her, and it had been Ashcombe, and he had said it about crime scenes.\n\nSix rows. Six dials in each row. Thirty-six.\n\nAnd six groups of four figures.\n\nNot enough. Two dials spare in every row.\n\nCorvain stood with the slip in one hand and the candle in the other and made herself be slow.\n\n*They are clerks. Whatever this is, four clerks can do it twice a week in a cold corridor holding a lamp and a box of returns. It is not clever. It cannot be clever. It has to be something a tired man can do one-handed.*\n\nShe looked at the slip again.\n\n**Q3.** The quarter. Third quarter.\n\nShe looked at the six dials in the top row and turned the first one to 1, and the second to 1, and the third to 4, and stopped, and looked at what she had done, and then very deliberately did not turn the fourth or the fifth or the sixth.\n\nThen she read the engraving again, and this time she read it the way a clerk would read it, which is to say as an instruction rather than as poetry.\n\n*When the bells ring, all hear.*\n\nThird quarter. Third bell.\n\nCorvain reached up and turned the last two dials of the top row to 0 and 3.\n\nSomething in the door went *click*, very small, like a watch being wound one turn.\n\nShe stood absolutely still.\n\nThen she began on the second row, and her hands were shaking so badly she had to put the candle down on the floor.\n\n---\n\nIt took her nineteen minutes and she got it wrong twice, and both times something in the mechanism made a sound like a swallow and she had to start the row again, and by the fifth row she had stopped being frightened and had started being angry, and the anger was so clean and so total that she could feel it in her teeth.\n\nBecause it was *nothing*.\n\nIt was a quarter-code and a bell-number, six times over. It was the kind of thing a shipping office used. It was changed every quarter and written down in pencil on a slip of paper and kept in a spring clip screwed to the front of the door it opened, six inches below a poem in a dead script about the birds and the bells, and the poem was there so that anybody who came down this corridor would look at the thirty-six dials and the founder's flourish and think: *this is beyond me, this belongs to people who can hear the Turn.*\n\nA hundred and three years of it. A whole city taught from the age of four that the reckoning was a mystery and that the mystery was in the blood, and at the bottom of the last stair the mystery was a docket slip.\n\nCorvain got the sixth row and the mechanism went through a sequence of noises that lasted about four seconds and sounded, unmistakably, like a machine sighing.\n\nThen the door came off its seal with a breath of cold dry air that smelled of paper and dust and old iron, and swung four inches, and stopped.\n\nShe picked up the candle.\n\nBehind the door, receding into the dark further than the light would go, shelf after shelf after shelf of bound volumes, dark green, gilt-stamped by year, in an unbroken run.\n\nA hundred and three of them.\n\nCorvain went in.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Three.*" },
  { n: 4, title: "The Shape of a Thing", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe worst thing about the Rise was that she was happy there, and she found that out on a Thursday, in the library, at about four in the afternoon.\n\nIt had been six weeks. The library was on the third floor of the west range and it had eleven windows down one side and a gallery running round three walls above, and it held, according to the catalogue, a hundred and forty thousand volumes, of which Rooke had established that four thousand were about the reckoning and about nine hundred were any use.\n\nShe had a table in the corner under the fourth window. It was not her table. She had simply been at it every day for six weeks and by the end of the second week other people had stopped sitting there, in the way that these things happen without anybody deciding.\n\nOn the Thursday she was working through Vasterling on periodic functions, which was two hundred years old and wrong in three places she had found so far, and Thorncastle had given it to her on purpose because it was wrong, and had said, *Find the errors. Do not tell me how many there are. I want to know how many you find.*\n\nRooke found the third one at about four o'clock. It was in a footnote. It had been sitting in that footnote for two hundred years and nobody had troubled it, and she went back and checked it four times because a girl who has been in a university for six weeks does not casually decide she is right and a dead man is wrong, and then she checked it a fifth time, and then she sat back in her chair with a strange feeling in her chest.\n\nShe thought: I have never been this warm and this fed and this quiet at the same time in my life. I have four thousand books and a table and nobody wants anything from me before Thursday. This is what it is like. This is what they all have. Every single one of them in this building has had this since they were six.\n\nAnd then she thought, immediately, in the same breath: Nettle is still coughing.\n\nShe sat with both of those for a while.\n\nThat was the moment she understood the shape of the thing that was going to be wrong with the rest of her life, and it was not guilt, because guilt was easy and she had expected guilt. It was that both were true. The library was good. The library was genuinely, straightforwardly good, and she wanted it, and she was better at being here than she had ever been at anything, and none of that made Ferrow Lane less cold.\n\nShe had been raised to think in sums and she could not make this one balance because it was not a sum. It was two sums about two different things and the answer to one had no bearing at all on the answer to the other, and there was no operation you could perform to bring them together.\n\nThe letter came the same afternoon.\n\n---\n\n*Rooke.*\n\n*Marta wrote this down for me and I am paying her in ledger work which she says is worth two grist an hour and I say is worth more but there we are.*\n\n*Do not send money. That is the first thing and I am putting it first because Mother said to.*\n\n*Mother is not well. It is her chest and it is not the same as Nettle it is the old thing that comes in the autumn, but this year it has come in September and she has not been down the stairs in nine days. Sister Maude has been twice and would not take anything either time which I have not told Mother.*\n\n*We are four weeks on the rent. Barrow has been round twice and he was not unkind the first time. Tam is portering on the seam and brings a mark and four most weeks. I have the ledgers at Marta's and I am slow but I am getting less slow and she has not shouted at me since the second week.*\n\n*Nettle is the same. Not worse. She asks about you.*\n\n*Do not send money Rooke. Mother says and I say. You sent eleven marks in August and I know what you get and I did the sum which I know is a stupid thing to say to you but I did it and eleven marks was most of what you had for the whole term. Mother cried when it came and it was not the good kind.*\n\n*We are not going to be out on the step. Barrow has a chalk with everybody on this lane and he cannot put us all out. Tessa.*\n\n*P.S. Marta says to tell you that her nephew has stopped skimming since I started on the ledgers which is not because I am good it is because he thinks I might be.*\n\n---\n\nRooke read it three times in the library and then took it out into the corridor and read it twice more by the window at the end, where nobody went.\n\n*Do not send money.*\n\nShe put the letter in her coat and stood looking down the face of the Rise at the brown fog over the Under and did the arithmetic she had been doing every night for six weeks, and it had not changed.\n\nFourteen marks a term. Four terms. Fifty-six marks a year, and the shortfall on Ferrow Lane was five marks a week, which was two hundred and sixty a year.\n\nShe had sent eleven marks in August because eleven was the number and she had wanted, very badly, to send eleven exactly. It had bought two bottles of syrup, and it had not bought the rent, and it had made Mother Halloway cry the bad way.\n\nTwo hundred and sixty marks a year. There was no version of a maintenance allowance that touched it. There was no version of any legitimate thing a fourteen year old student could do in a term that touched it.\n\nRooke stood at the window with her forehead nearly on the glass and thought, quite calmly:\n\n*Then I need a different source.*\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\nThe door had a gear nailed to it, which turned out to be the least eccentric thing about the entire premises.\n\nMarrow had gone to Kettle Yard four times in a fortnight before he got in. The first time nobody answered. The second time somebody shouted through the door that they were not buying. The third time the door opened four inches, an eye came to the gap, and the door shut again.\n\nThe fourth time he brought the pressure gauge.\n\nIt was not the pressure gauge he had been banked for; that one had gone back to the barrow-man. It was one he had spent nine days building out of two broken ones, a length of brass tube, a spring out of a dead clock and a glass face he had ground down himself on a wet stone with a great deal of swearing. It read low by about a twelfth across its whole range, consistently, which he had established by comparing it against the works gauge on the standpipe at the end of Ketu's lane over four days.\n\nHe held it up to the eye at the gap.\n\nThe door opened.\n\n---\n\n\"It's wrong,\" said Old Bramwell.\n\n\"By a twelfth. Low. Right the way across.\"\n\n\"Then it's *wrong*.\"\n\n\"It's wrong the same amount everywhere,\" said Marrow. \"So it's not wrong, it's offset. You can read it. You just have to know.\"\n\nBramwell looked at him over the top of a pair of brass-rimmed magnifying goggles pushed up onto his forehead. He was about seventy, wild grey hair going in eleven directions, a leather apron burned through in three places, hands ruined the way hands get ruined by forty years of doing something properly.\n\n\"Say that again,\" he said.\n\n\"You can read it. You just have to know.\"\n\n\"No, the other bit.\"\n\n\"It's not wrong, it's offset.\"\n\nBramwell took the gauge and turned it over twice and tapped the glass and handed it back.\n\n\"Come in,\" he said. \"Don't touch anything and don't sit on the bench with the cloth on it, and if I shout it isn't about you.\"\n\n---\n\nThe workshop had eaten its own building. That was the only way Marrow could ever describe it to anybody afterwards. Three storeys with the floors mostly taken out, and salvaged Engine components hanging from the joists on chains, and six part-cannibalised machines on six benches in various stages of being either murdered or resurrected, and a wall of labelled jars containing nothing but gears, sorted by tooth count, and no clear floor anywhere at all.\n\nAnd it was warm. That was the thing that surprised him most. There was a stove going at the back and a kettle on it and two mismatched chairs, and somebody lived here, actually lived here, in among all of it.\n\n\"Ninth stratum,\" said Bramwell, over his shoulder.\n\nMarrow stopped in the doorway.\n\n\"Growl told you.\"\n\n\"Growl tells me everything, he's been telling me everything since he was nine, it's a curse.\" Bramwell was doing something to a spring vice with a file. \"Assessed fourth. Amended ninth. Prior conduct. Sirrath.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Right.\" The old man did not turn round. \"Now listen, because I'm going to say this once and then we're never going to discuss it again and if you bring it up I'll throw you out.\n\n\"There's two things you can do with that. One is you can spend the rest of your life being a man who was robbed. It's a whole trade, that. There's men in the Ashlight been doing it thirty years and they're very good at it and they've got the whole thing off by heart, and every one of them is exactly as clever now as he was at fourteen, because he stopped.\" He put the file down. \"The other thing you can do is get *better*. Not to show them. Not to prove anything. There's no proving anything, they've got the ledger and the ledger doesn't care. Just get better, because it's the only thing in this world that nobody can take off you with four lines and a stamp.\"\n\n\"That's the same speech Vane gives, isn't it,\" said Marrow. \"Just the other way round.\"\n\nBramwell turned round.\n\nHe looked at Marrow for a good four seconds and then he laughed, a horrible barking laugh, and slapped the bench.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Yes it is, isn't it. Same speech. Same true thing at the bottom of it. And that's why he's had four goes at me and I've said no four times, and it isn't because I'm a better man than Silas Vane, because I'm not, I've done things you'd cross the road about.\" He pushed the goggles further up his forehead. \"It's because he uses it to point people at something and I use it to point people at a bench. That's the whole difference. It's not a big difference. Some days I can't find it.\"\n\nHe picked the file back up.\n\n\"Sweep,\" he said. \"Start at the back. There's forty years of it and I'll want the brass swarf kept separate, it's worth eight grist a pound.\"\n\n---\n\nHe swept for nine days. On the tenth day Bramwell let him hold a lamp while the old man worked, and asked him what he was looking at, and Marrow said, \"You're taking the governor out backwards,\" and Bramwell said, \"Why's that stupid?\" and Marrow told him, and Bramwell said, \"Right,\" and carried on taking it out backwards, and it came out, and it was the wrong way to do it and it worked.\n\n\"Why?\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Because the collar's swelled. You couldn't know that. You could know it if you'd had your hands on nine hundred of them.\" Bramwell put the governor on the bench. \"That's the whole of it, son. That's the only thing I've got that you haven't. It isn't cleverness. You're cleverer than me and we both know it and I'd not be able to sit in that hall for four hours doing what you did. It's nine hundred governors.\"\n\nMarrow held the lamp.\n\n\"They don't teach that up the Rise either,\" he said.\n\n\"No, they do not, and it's the reason half of them can't change a washer.\" Bramwell reached for a scraper. \"Now. You've been sweeping ten days and I've been watching you look at things, and I've got a question and you're to answer it honestly. What do you actually want?\"\n\nMarrow thought about it for a long time. The lamp hissed.\n\n\"I want a room with one person in it,\" he said. \"And I want to be paid in silver.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer, that's a wage.\"\n\n\"It's the only answer they'd let me have.\"\n\n\"That wasn't what I asked, was it.\" Bramwell held out his hand for the lamp. \"Try again. Not what you'd settle for. What do you *want*.\"\n\nAnd Marrow, who had not said it out loud to anybody, not to Ketu and not to the woman at the hatch and not to Growl on the end of a cot at Sister Maude's, heard himself say:\n\n\"I want to know what's actually in the Turn.\"\n\nBramwell went very still.\n\n\"Come again.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows.\" Marrow's voice came out faster than he meant it to. \"That's what I worked out, doing the old papers. Everybody talks about the Engine like it's a thing they understand and then when you get to the actual questions about it, the real ones, about what it's doing and how it's doing it, there's *nothing*. There's four books and they're all a hundred years old and they all say the same eleven pages and none of them says what happens inside. And I thought, right, that's because it's the true calculus and that's up the Rise and it's not for me. But it isn't up the Rise either, is it? I've read what they publish. They publish nothing. Not because it's secret. Because there's nothing to publish.\"\n\nThe stove ticked at the back of the shop.\n\n\"They don't know,\" said Marrow. \"Do they.\"\n\nBramwell put the lamp down on the bench very carefully, as though it had become heavier.\n\n\"Sit down,\" he said. \"The chair, not the bench with the cloth.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Captain Reeve Ashcombe**\nShe had not slept, and she had stopped bothering to hide it.\n\nAshcombe had been watching officers stop sleeping for twenty-six years and he had catalogued the ways it showed. There was the drinking sort, and the loud sort, and the sort that got careless with their kit. Corvain had gone the other way, which was the way he had always found most difficult to manage, because it did not look like a problem. She had gone quiet and extremely precise. Her paperwork had got better. She had started coming in at fifth bell.\n\n\"Sit down,\" he said.\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, and put the file on the desk, and don't open it.\"\n\nShe sat down. She put the file on the desk. She did not open it. She looked at him and waited, and the waiting was patient and absolutely immovable, and Ashcombe thought, not for the first time in six weeks, that Adelina Corvain had accidentally produced something considerably more dangerous than she had intended.\n\n\"Tell me what you've got,\" he said. \"Without the paper. Out loud, like a person.\"\n\n\"There are a hundred and three volumes of annual returns,\" said Corvain. \"I've read fifty-one. That's from the founding forward, and I did it that way round on purpose.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because if I'd started at this year I'd have known what I was looking for and found it.\"\n\nAshcombe grunted. That was the correct answer and it was not a common one.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Conduct amendments don't appear in the returns for the first thirty-eight years. Not once. The word isn't in the volumes.\" She had her hands flat on her knees. \"They start in the thirty-ninth year, which is eighteen years after the founding generation died out. There are four of them in the first year they appear. Nine the next. Then it goes up, and it keeps going up, and by the sixtieth year it's running at about thirty a year, and it's never gone below thirty since.\"\n\n\"And this year?\"\n\n\"Forty-one. Same as the number of non-human candidates. But that's this year and it's the least interesting part.\" She paused. \"Sir, the amendments aren't about conduct. I can prove that now and I couldn't a fortnight ago.\"\n\n\"Prove it to me, then.\"\n\n\"An amendment has an assessed result and an amended result. Both are recorded, which was careless of them, but they had to be, because the examiners' pay used to be tied to assessment volume.\" She said it drily. \"So for a hundred and three years there is a written record of what every amended candidate actually scored before somebody changed it. Fourteen thousand of them.\"\n\nAshcombe sat forward.\n\n\"Fourteen thousand.\"\n\n\"Fourteen thousand two hundred and six. I counted.\" She met his eye. \"Sir, if conduct amendments were about conduct, then the assessed results of amended candidates would be spread across the strata the way anybody's are. Some high, most middling, some low. They are not. Ten thousand of the fourteen thousand were assessed in the top four strata.\"\n\nThe office was very quiet.\n\n\"Say that again,\" said Ashcombe.\n\n\"Seventy-one per cent of every conduct amendment in the history of the Calibration was applied to a child who had scored in the top four strata.\" Corvain's voice did not change at all. \"It is not a punishment system. Punishment doesn't care how well you did. It is a filter. It goes looking for the ones who did *best*, and it takes them out, and the record of what they actually scored is sitting in a green volume with gilt on the spine two floors under Tallow Street where nobody has looked at it since it was bound.\"\n\nAshcombe got up and went to the window.\n\nThe clatterlift cable ran up past the glass, hauling, and the building took the vibration the way it did forty times a day, and he stood there with his hands behind his back and let himself understand it properly, because there was no point in being a captain if you could not stand still for ten seconds and let a thing be true.\n\n\"Rustbound,\" he said.\n\n\"Sixty-eight per cent Rustbound. Twenty-two per cent non-human, which is enormously out of proportion. Ten per cent from the Rise, and I looked at those, sir, and every one of the ten per cent is from a minor house, and eleven of them are servants' children raised in Wrought households.\" She stopped. \"There is not one single amendment in a hundred and three years applied to a child of a major house. Not one. I checked twice and then I got a clerk to check it, and I did not tell him what he was checking for.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\n\"Nobody who'll talk to anybody, sir.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\nAshcombe turned round.\n\n\"Right,\" he said. \"Now the other question. The one you haven't asked yourself, and I want to see you not be able to answer it, because you need to know it's there before somebody else shows it to you.\" He came back to the desk. \"Why?\"\n\nCorvain opened her mouth and stopped.\n\n\"You've got the *what*,\" said Ashcombe. \"The what is beautiful and it's airtight and I'd take it to a magistrate tomorrow. Now tell me why. Why does the Council of Gifford spend a hundred and three years and God knows how much money finding the cleverest children in this city and then reaching into the ledger and pushing them back down. Because if the answer is spite, or snobbery, or keeping the rust in the Under, then it's a bad system run by bad people and we go to the magistrate and win. And you don't believe that, do you.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"Because it's too expensive,\" said Corvain. \"Spite doesn't build a filing system. Somebody has been maintaining this for a hundred and three years across, what, twenty Chancellors, and it has never once leaked, and the mechanism is fussy and administrative and it costs real money to run. People don't do that out of contempt. They do that because it's load-bearing.\" She looked up. \"Somebody thinks the city falls down without it. That's the only thing that fits.\"\n\nAshcombe sat down heavily.\n\n\"Good,\" he said. \"Then you're where I am. And Corvain, I want you to hear the next bit properly, because I'm going to say it once.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"When you find out why, and you will, you are going to be offered something.\" He said it flatly. \"Not a threat. They don't threaten, at that level, it's vulgar and it leaves a mark. You're going to be offered a chair. Somebody very calm is going to sit you down and show you a number, and the number is going to be real, and they're going to say: now you know, and you can see why, and we'd like you to help.\n\n\"And here is the thing, and this is the only piece of it I have ever had to work out for myself. The number will be real. The reason will be *good*. It'll be a proper reason, not an excuse, and you'll believe it, because it'll be true.\" He looked at her. \"And it still won't be the answer. Because a good reason for doing a wrong thing quietly for a hundred years is how you get a city where four clerks and a docket slip hold up the whole arrangement and nobody has thought about it since before your grandmother was born.\"\n\n\"Sir,\" said Corvain. \"How do you know what they'll offer?\"\n\nAshcombe was silent for a moment.\n\n\"Because it's a small city,\" he said, \"and I'm fifty-five, and I've been in this coat a long time. Take the file. Get some sleep. And Corvain.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"When you go up against it, come to me first. Not because I can protect you. I can't, and you should be clear about that.\" He pushed the file across. \"Because I'd like the chance to decide what I am before somebody decides it for me.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Whistle**\nThe shop on Cull Alley had a low ceiling, and Whistle had not stood up in it since the second year, which was nineteen years ago.\n\nThat was fine. Whistle sat. The counter had been built to be worked at while sitting, and the pigeonholes went floor to ceiling on every wall, four thousand of them, and every one of them held folded paper, and the arrangement of that paper was not written down anywhere and never would be.\n\nIt had been a busy month.\n\nThree separate parties had come asking about the Calibration returns, and none of the three knew about the other two, and Whistle had served all three, and the pleasure of it was not the money.\n\n**The first** had been the young Sirrath, and he had come in September with eight grist and a homemade pressure gauge he was clearly not willing to part with, and he had asked, straight out, with no attempt at cleverness at all: *Who signed my amendment.*\n\nWhistle had told him: nobody signs an amendment. That is the answer and it is worth what he paid, which was nothing, because Whistle had told him for free. There is no signature block on the form. It is initialled by a clerk under a delegated authority and the delegation is renewed every quarter by an office rather than a person. There has not been a name on an amendment since the fifty-first year.\n\nThe boy had stood in the shop for a long moment and then said, \"That's on purpose.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then who set it up that way?\"\n\nAnd Whistle had said: *that is a different question, and it costs.*\n\n**The second** had been the officer. Grey coat, twenty, far too polite, and she had come in November and asked something so specific that Whistle had put down the pen.\n\n*Has anybody ever, at any time, tried to publish the assessed results.*\n\nNot the amendments. The assessed results. She had already worked out that the crime was recorded in the ledger, and what she wanted to know was whether anybody had ever tried to take it out of the ledger and into the street, and what had happened to them.\n\nWhistle had quoted her four sovereigns, which was deliberately outrageous, to find out whether she had four sovereigns and whether she would pay them.\n\nShe had paid.\n\n**The third** had come in December, and the third was the interesting one, because the third had not come at all. The third had sent a letter, from an address on the Rise, in a hand that had been taught to write properly by somebody who was paid to teach it, and the letter had contained no name and one question and a folded note for six marks.\n\n*What does a person on the Rise buy, that is legal, that pays well, and that they cannot be sent home for?*\n\nWhistle had read that four times.\n\nIt was not the question of somebody wanting to break a rule. That was the ordinary trade; Whistle sold rule-breaking eleven times a day. This was somebody who had established, carefully, in advance, that they could not afford to be sent home, and who was therefore looking for the narrow band of things that were both lucrative and defensible, and who had worked out that such a band existed and that somebody would know where it was.\n\nThat was a fourteen year old asking an adult question.\n\nWhistle had turned the six marks over on the counter for a while and had then done something not done in nineteen years of trading, which was to answer at less than the going rate.\n\nThe reply had been three lines.\n\n*Contentions. The university permits them and the Council does not regulate them because they are a tradition of the Rise and nobody has ever needed to. Stakes are set by the parties and are enforceable as a debt of honour, which in that building means enforceable in fact.*\n\n*Nobody has staked money in one for forty years. There is no rule against it.*\n\n*You will only be able to do this twice.*\n\n---\n\nWhistle logged all three in the pigeonholes, in three different places, under three systems.\n\nThen, at the end of the month, sitting alone in the shop with the lamp hooded and the slate wiped, Whistle did the thing that made the money worth having, which was to lay the three of them side by side.\n\nA Sirrath boy asking who built the amendment mechanism.\n\nAn officer asking whether anybody had ever tried to publish the assessed results, and what had become of them.\n\nA child on the Rise asking how to earn money without asking permission.\n\nThree people, all fourteen or thereabouts, all set in motion by the same examination in the same summer, all pulling on the same rope from three different ends, and not one of them aware that the others existed.\n\nWhistle sat with that for a long time and then wrote a single line on a fresh slip and put it in a pigeonhole that had nothing else in it.\n\n*They will all three come back. Raise the price.*\n\n---\n\n**V. The Calculus Duel**\nA Contention was declared in the Long Hall, at supper, in front of everybody, because that was the entire point of it and had been for two hundred years.\n\nLord Antony Vex stood up between the second and third courses and did it perfectly, and Rooke, who had known it was coming since Tuesday because Ivy Thorncastle heard everything, sat with her hands in her lap and watched him do it and thought: he has practised this.\n\n\"Fellows and scholars.\" He had a good voice. Eighteen, tall, immaculately turned out in a way that took either forty minutes or a servant. \"I ask leave to declare a Contention.\"\n\nThe Long Hall did the thing halls do, which is go quiet in patches and then all at once.\n\n\"Against whom?\" said the Proctor, wearily, from the high table.\n\n\"The first-year scholar Rooke.\"\n\nThere was a sound in the room, a kind of collective intake, and Rooke heard about four separate people laugh and stop.\n\n\"On what matter?\"\n\n\"On the matter of the fourth-year seminar.\"\n\nAnd there it was. Rooke had wondered how he would frame it and he had chosen well, because it was the only genuine grievance available and everybody in that hall knew about it. Thorncastle had moved Rooke into the fourth-year seminar in the ninth week of her first term. It had never been done. It had never been done because it could not be done; you could not sit in the fourth-year seminar without three years of the notation, and Rooke had walked in with eleven weeks of it and had, on the third Thursday, corrected the Reader.\n\n\"The scholar Rooke has been advanced past three years of study on the recommendation of one Professor,\" said Vex, \"and the rest of us are asked to take that on faith. I don't ask her to be removed. I don't wish her removed. I ask her to demonstrate it, in the hall, in the old way, so that nobody has to take anything on faith at all.\"\n\nHe said it reasonably. He was very good at reasonably.\n\n\"That's actually fair,\" Ivy muttered.\n\n\"I know,\" said Rooke. \"That's what's clever about it.\"\n\n\"Scholar Rooke,\" said the Proctor. \"Do you accept?\"\n\nRooke stood up.\n\nSix weeks ago she would have said yes out of temper, and she knew that about herself now, and she had spent three days making very sure that she was not going to.\n\n\"I accept,\" she said. \"I'd like to name the stake.\"\n\nThe Proctor blinked. \"The stake is traditionally—\"\n\n\"The stake is traditionally set by the parties,\" said Rooke. \"I looked it up. Statute of the Long Hall, article nine. *Stakes to be agreed between the contending parties before the setting of the problems, and thereafter enforceable upon the honour of the houses concerned.* There's no restriction on what the stake can be. There used to be a Contention over a horse.\"\n\nA long silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Vex, with a small smile, because he could feel the room enjoying itself and he was not stupid. \"Name it.\"\n\n\"Sixty marks.\"\n\nThe Long Hall made a noise.\n\nIt was, Rooke would think later, one of the more interesting noises she had ever caused. It was not outrage. It was worse than outrage. It was embarrassment: two hundred people all simultaneously experiencing the specific social discomfort of hearing money mentioned by name at supper.\n\n\"Money,\" said Vex.\n\n\"Sixty marks.\"\n\n\"You want to stake *money*.\"\n\n\"I want you to stake money,\" said Rooke. \"I haven't got sixty marks. If I lose I'll take a forfeit, whatever you like, I'll clean the seminar boards for a year, I don't care. If I win, sixty marks.\"\n\n\"That is not—\" Vex laughed, and looked round for the room, and got it, and Rooke watched him get it and felt something cold and useful settle in her stomach. \"Forgive me. That is not what a Contention is *for*.\"\n\n\"No,\" Rooke agreed. \"It's for one person proving something to a room. I've read the statute and it doesn't say what either party has to want out of it. You want to make a point. I want sixty marks. There's no rule that says we have to want the same thing.\"\n\n\"You are asking me to *pay* you.\"\n\n\"I'm asking you to risk sixty marks on being right about me,\" said Rooke. \"You've just stood up in front of two hundred people and said you're sure. Sixty marks is nine days' allowance to you. I looked that up as well.\"\n\nThe room went very quiet indeed.\n\nAnd Vex, who had been raised from birth by people who had taught him that the one unforgivable thing is to be seen to flinch, said:\n\n\"Done.\"\n\n---\n\nThey cleared the floor of the Long Hall and brought in the two slate boards, back to back, which was how it had always been done so that neither party could see the other's working, and the Proctor set the problems from a sealed envelope prepared by two Readers who were not in the room.\n\nBest of three. No books, no tables, no instruments.\n\nRooke took off her coat and rolled her sleeves back and picked up the chalk, and while the Proctor was reading the preamble she did the only piece of thinking that actually mattered, which was this:\n\n*He is very good. He is properly, genuinely good, and everybody keeps telling me he's arrogant as though that makes him bad at it, and it doesn't. He has had twelve years of the best teaching in Gifford and he does not have to slow down at section nine.*\n\n*So do not race him. He is faster than me at the things he has practised, and he has practised everything on the syllabus.*\n\n*Beat him at the shape of it.*\n\n**The first problem** went to Vex in four minutes. It was a standard reduction. He did it beautifully and he did it fast and there was applause and he turned round and inclined his head to it, and Rooke was still on her third line.\n\nShe had known that would happen. It still hurt more than she had budgeted for.\n\n**The second problem** was longer and had a trick in it, and Rooke found the trick at ninety seconds and Vex found it at about two and a half minutes, and she took it by a margin of perhaps forty seconds, and there was almost no applause at all because two hundred people were now paying a completely different kind of attention.\n\n**The third problem** the Proctor read out twice, because it was long.\n\nIt concerned a periodic system with a driving force applied at an interval, and it asked for the condition under which the system remained stable, and it was fourth-year work and it was on the syllabus and Antony Vex had done nine of them.\n\nHe started immediately. Rooke heard the chalk go on the other side of the board and did not move.\n\nShe stood there for a hundred and ten seconds with her hands at her sides.\n\nSomebody in the hall started to whisper and was hushed.\n\nBecause there was something wrong with the problem, and she could see it, and she had seen it in the first fifteen seconds, and she had spent the next ninety-five making absolutely certain, because a girl who has been in this building eleven weeks does not casually decide that two Readers have set a broken question.\n\nThe interval in the driving force. It was not arbitrary. It had been chosen to look arbitrary, and it was in fact exactly the interval that made the standard method fail, because the standard method assumed you could treat two things as independent and at that specific interval they were not independent, they were the same thing looked at twice, and if you ran the standard method you would get a clean, plausible, entirely wrong condition, and it would look right, and it would be wrong in the fourth line.\n\n*You have assumed the relation holds in both directions and it does not.*\n\nThorncastle had taught her that in ten minutes on a Thursday in September, off the back of a diagnostic sheet, because Rooke had written *I am wrong and I cannot see where and I would like to be told*.\n\nIt was not on the syllabus. Rooke was suddenly and completely certain of that. It was not on the syllabus, and Vasterling had it wrong in a footnote two hundred years ago, and it had sat there ever since, and the two Readers who set this problem had almost certainly set it out of Vasterling.\n\nShe thought: they don't know either.\n\nThen she thought: if I write the right answer, I lose.\n\nBecause she would be marked against the model solution, and the model solution would come out of the same book, and the Proctor would read it and see that Vex's answer matched and hers did not, and she would lose sixty marks and Ferrow Lane would be four weeks on the rent going on five.\n\nRooke stood in front of the slate with the chalk in her hand and the whole hall watching, and she made the decision in about two seconds, and it was not a noble decision and she was clear-eyed about that afterwards. It was arithmetic. She had run it and the honest answer paid better.\n\nShe wrote the wrong solution first.\n\nShe wrote it out in full, the standard method, cleanly, quickly, exactly as the model would have it, and she wrote it small and she wrote it high on the board so there was room underneath.\n\nThen she drew a line under it.\n\nThen, underneath, she wrote:\n\n*The above is the answer to the question as set. It is wrong. The interval given makes the fourth line invalid, because at that interval the two terms are not independent. The problem as set has no stable condition of the form requested. Below is the actual condition. It does not appear in Vasterling, who has the same error in a footnote on page 340.*\n\nAnd then she did it properly, and it took her nine minutes, and it filled the rest of the board.\n\nShe put the chalk down.\n\n\"Finished,\" said Antony Vex, from the other side.\n\n\"Finished,\" said Rooke.\n\n---\n\nThey turned the boards.\n\nThe Proctor read Vex's board and nodded, because it was clean and it was correct against the model, and there was applause, and Vex stood with his hands behind his back and took it well.\n\nThen the Proctor came round to Rooke's board.\n\nHe read the top third, and his face did nothing.\n\nHe read the line.\n\nThen he read the paragraph underneath it, and stopped, and read it again, and then turned and looked at Rooke, and then turned back and read the nine minutes of working below it, all of it, slowly, in front of two hundred silent people, and it took him a very long time.\n\nThen he said, to nobody in particular: \"Fetch Professor Thorncastle.\"\n\n\"She's at dinner in the Deanery,\" said somebody.\n\n\"I am aware of where she is.\"\n\n---\n\nThorncastle came in with her napkin still in her hand.\n\nShe read the board from four feet away without moving. Then she came closer and read the paragraph. Then she said, out loud, in a voice that carried to the back of the Long Hall:\n\n\"Page three hundred and forty.\"\n\n\"Professor,\" said the Proctor.\n\n\"I have been telling this university about page three hundred and forty for nineteen years.\" Thorncastle turned round. Her colour was very high. \"I have raised it in three faculty meetings. There is a note from me in the seminar file dated the year before this child was born. And it is *still* being set out of Vasterling, in the fourth year, by two Readers who have both been through my seminar, and it took a first-term scholar eleven weeks off Ferrow Lane one hundred and ten seconds to see it from the far side of a slate board.\"\n\nShe turned to the high table.\n\n\"I should like it minuted,\" she said, \"that the problem was defective, and that the model solution is defective, and that if this Contention is decided against her then this hall will have formally resolved that the correct answer loses.\"\n\nThe silence went on for some time.\n\nThen Antony Vex said, from the other side of the boards: \"May I see it?\"\n\nHe came round. He read the top third. He read the line and the paragraph. Then he read the working, and he read it properly, with his hands behind his back, and it took him about three minutes, and nobody in the hall said a word.\n\nThen he straightened up.\n\nRooke watched his face and understood, with a lurch of something that was not at all what she had expected to feel, that Antony Vex was not stupid, and was not a coward, and had just been shown a thing he was going to have to carry around for the rest of his life.\n\n\"The Contention is hers,\" he said.\n\n\"Lord Antony—\"\n\n\"It's hers, Proctor. I answered the question. She answered the *problem*.\" He said it steadily, and only somebody standing where Rooke was standing could have heard what it cost. \"And I would rather be the man who lost sixty marks than the man who stood in this hall and argued that being right was outside the terms.\"\n\nHe turned to Rooke.\n\n\"Sixty marks,\" he said. \"You'll have it in the morning.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I don't like you,\" said Vex.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I want to be clear that this has not changed that at all.\"\n\n\"That's all right,\" said Rooke. \"I need the money, not the friend.\"\n\nAnd Antony Vex laughed, once, entirely without meaning to, and then looked extremely annoyed about having done it, and walked out of the Long Hall with his shoulders back.\n\n---\n\nIvy found her afterwards, sitting on the steps outside with her coat over her knees, in the cold, doing something with a pencil on the back of Tessa's letter.\n\n\"You won sixty marks,\" said Ivy.\n\n\"Twelve weeks,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Sixty marks. The shortfall's five marks a week.\" Rooke did not look up. \"Twelve weeks. That's what it is. Everyone in there thinks I just won an argument about a footnote and what I actually won is that nobody gets put out on the step until March.\"\n\nIvy sat down on the step next to her.\n\n\"You said you'd only be able to do that twice,\" she said.\n\nRooke's pencil stopped.\n\n\"I never said that to you.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ivy. \"You said it to yourself on Tuesday, out loud, in the library, and I was in the gallery looking for something on the Vaskeld famine and you didn't know I was up there.\" She pulled her knees up. \"Why twice?\"\n\nRooke was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"Because it only works while they think I might lose,\" she said.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Four.*" },
  { n: 5, title: "Hungrier Science", text: "**I. Rooke**\nShe went down on the clatterlift with sixty marks in a cloth bag against her ribs and a lie already prepared, and she used the lie on the porter at the university gate and it worked, and that was the first thing that told her she had changed.\n\nSix months ago she could not have lied to a porter. Six months ago she had been a girl who put eleven on sixty-eight lines because she could not think of a cheaper way to be dishonest. Now she said, \"Professor Thorncastle's sent me to the instrument-maker's on Fenn Street,\" without a flicker, and the porter wrote it in his book, and she was through.\n\nThe lift went down the face of the Rise in eleven minutes. Rooke stood at the front with her forehead nearly on the glass and watched the light change.\n\nIt went in stages. That was the thing you did not notice going up, because going up you were being taken away from something and you did not look. Going down you watched it happen: clean grey daylight at the top, then the first level of the fog, thin, and everything went sepia, and then thicker, and the gaslights started appearing in the murk below you like things underwater, and by the last two minutes the windows had gone brown and you could see the lamps on the cables and nothing else at all.\n\nThe doors opened onto the seam and the smell hit her, and Rooke had not once in eleven weeks thought about the smell, and she stood in the doorway of the lift car with her hand on the frame and understood that she had forgotten it.\n\nCoke smoke. Wet iron. The dyer's works. Something rotten in the sumps under the arches. Bread. It was the smell of her entire life and she had lost it in eleven weeks, and getting it back should have felt like coming home and did not. It felt like the first mouthful of food on the second day at the university, when she had eaten too much and been sick.\n\nShe was wearing a coat that fitted her.\n\nThat was the other thing, and she did not work it out until Ferrow Lane. She had come down in her own clothes, deliberately, the mended coat, the boots, and she had thought that was enough. It was not. Because the coat had been washed properly eleven times by somebody who was paid to wash things, and she had been eating four times a day for eleven weeks, and she had grown three quarters of an inch, and the coat which had been two sizes too large in the summer now nearly fitted her, and Rooke came round onto Ferrow Lane and the first person she saw was Barrow the rent man and he did not recognise her.\n\n---\n\nTam knew her from the end of the lane and came at her at a dead run and hit her about waist height and would not let go, and that was the only easy thing that happened all day.\n\nPip hung back in the doorway. Pip had been six weeks off seven when Rooke left and had climbed into the foot of her bed at second bell every night for four years, and Pip stood in the doorway with one hand on the frame and looked at her and would not come out.\n\n\"Pip.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Pip, it's me.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Pip.\n\nAnd went inside.\n\n---\n\nMother Halloway was in the front room, because they had brought the bed down. That was the thing Rooke saw before she saw anything else and it went into her like a nail: they had brought the bed down from the back room, which meant nine days on the stairs had become a decision about the stairs, and nobody had put that in a letter.\n\n\"There she is,\" said Halloway.\n\nHer voice had gone. Not weak; wrecked. There was a thing in the bottom of it that rattled.\n\nRooke sat down on the edge of the bed and took her hand and did not say anything for a while, which was, she understood later, the only genuinely right thing she did that day.\n\n\"You've grown,\" said Halloway.\n\n\"Three quarters of an inch.\"\n\n\"Course you've measured it.\" The old woman laughed and then paid for the laugh for about fifteen seconds. \"Well. That's what it's for, isn't it. Feeding you.\"\n\n\"Mother, I've got sixty marks.\"\n\nHalloway's hand tightened on hers.\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\n\"It's *mine*. Nobody's given it to me and I haven't done anything wrong to get it, I won it, there's a thing they do up there called a Contention and it's a formal thing with a statute and a Proctor and it's older than the Engine and there's no rule against staking money and I won it in front of two hundred people.\" It came out fast and she heard it come out fast. \"Sixty marks. That's the rent clear and the arrears clear and Nettle's syrup and something over.\"\n\n\"You won it.\"\n\n\"I beat a boy called Antony Vex at the fourth-year reckoning in front of the whole hall and he paid me the next morning in silver and he was decent about it, which I did not expect.\"\n\nHalloway lay still for a moment.\n\n\"Sixty marks,\" she said. \"In front of two hundred people.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So two hundred people up there now know that the girl from Ferrow Lane needs money.\"\n\nRooke stopped.\n\nShe had run that sum eleven ways. She had run it for three days before she accepted the Contention, and she had run it while she was standing in front of the slate, and she had run it on the steps afterwards with Ivy, and she had not run it that way. Not once.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said.\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"That's a cost. I hadn't counted it.\"\n\n\"No, love.\" Halloway's thumb moved on the back of her hand. \"You never count that one. None of you ever do, and I've had eleven of you.\" She turned her head on the pillow. \"You always count the money and you never count what people know about you. It's the same in a lane and it's the same up a hill. What people know about you is the whole of it.\"\n\n---\n\nNettle came down at four, wrapped in the blanket, and sat on the end of the bed and did not cough for nine whole minutes, and Rooke sat and counted them, and then Nettle coughed, three times and then the wet catch, and Rooke's whole body went cold in a way it had not done in eleven weeks.\n\n\"You've still got it.\"\n\n\"It's better,\" said Nettle.\n\n\"You've had the syrup?\"\n\n\"Two bottles. In August.\" Nettle picked at the blanket. \"Tessa said.\"\n\n\"And it's better?\"\n\n\"It's better than August.\"\n\nRooke looked at her. Nine years old, thin, flushed across the cheekbones in a way that was not health.\n\n\"Nettle. Look at me and tell me honestly. Is it better or is it the same and you've stopped mentioning it?\"\n\nNettle looked at the blanket.\n\n\"It's the same,\" she said.\n\n---\n\nShe did not tell them she was going. She got up at half past eight to catch the last lift and Tessa followed her out onto the step.\n\n\"Say it,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"I wasn't going to say anything.\"\n\n\"You've been going to say something since two o'clock. Say it.\"\n\nTessa stood in the doorway with her arms folded against the cold. She was seventeen now and she looked about thirty and she had ink on the side of her right hand, which had never in her life been true before.\n\n\"Don't come back for a while,\" she said.\n\nRooke did not move.\n\n\"Not like that. I'm saying it wrong, I always say it wrong, that's why you did the letters.\" Tessa pushed her hair back. \"Pip wouldn't come out of the doorway. Did you see?\"\n\n\"I saw.\"\n\n\"Pip cried for two hours after you went in August. Not while you were here. After.\" Tessa's voice was steady and it was costing her. \"And Pip's fine most days now, and then you come down for six hours and the whole thing starts again, and you go up on the lift, and I'm the one that's here at second bell. So don't come back for a while. Come back at the end of the year. Write. Write more, write every week, Marta'll read them out and she doesn't charge for that, she only charges for writing.\"\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"And take the money back.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Take some of it back.\" Tessa's chin came up. \"Not all of it. Keep forty down here, that's the rent clear and the arrears and Nettle. Take twenty back up with you.\"\n\n\"I don't need twenty marks. Everything's paid for up there, that's the whole point of it—\"\n\n\"Rooke.\" Tessa stepped down off the step. \"You went up that hill with two shifts and one pair of boots. And you've been down here six hours and you've told us about a Contention and a Proctor and a statute and a boy called Vex, and I've been listening, and I don't understand one word in four, and I'm not stupid, I'm slow at columns and I'm not stupid.\" She was crying and ignoring it. \"You had to fight a lord in front of two hundred people to get sixty marks. What happens the next time we need money? Are you going to fight another one?\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"That's what I thought.\" Tessa wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"So take twenty. Because you're going to need to be able to say no to something up there, one day, and you can't say no to anything if you haven't got a penny of your own. That's not me being clever. That's just what happens to people.\"\n\nRooke stood in the lane with the fog coming up.\n\n\"Where did you learn that?\" she said.\n\n\"Marta,\" said Tessa. \"Forty years behind a counter. She says everyone who's ever gone under did it because they couldn't afford to walk out of a room.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke went up on the last lift with twenty marks in the cloth bag and Ferrow Lane going brown and then sepia and then grey below her, and about halfway she stopped looking down, and she was not proud of that, and she did it anyway.\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\n\"Nobody knows what's in it,\" said Bramwell. \"That's not a secret. It's just not a thing anybody says out loud, which isn't the same. Sit still and hold the light.\"\n\nThey were four hours into a job on a pressure governor off a dray, and the shop was warm, and it was the fourth week and Marrow had learned by now that Bramwell only talked properly when his hands were busy.\n\n\"You've been inside it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Twice. Nineteen and thirty-one years ago. Both times on a contract, both times with a gang of eleven, both times in and out in a shift and never past the second gallery.\" Bramwell held out his hand for the scraper. \"And I'll tell you what I saw, because it's not much and it's got about in the trade anyway. It's big. That's the first thing and everybody says it and nobody's ready for it. You go in expecting a machine and it's a *building*, and the building's the machine, and there's cogwheels in there the size of this shop turning about as fast as an hour hand and you can't hear them, you can *feel* them, in your teeth.\"\n\n\"And the second thing?\"\n\n\"The second thing.\" Bramwell worked at the collar for a while. \"Half of it's not on the plans.\"\n\nMarrow shifted the lamp.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"There's a plan set. There's four copies and they're in the Engine houses and they're a hundred and three years old and I've had my hands on a page of one, which is not a thing I'm going to tell you how.\" Bramwell squinted. \"And the machine that's in that hole is not the machine on the plan. It's bigger. There's whole banks of it that aren't drawn anywhere, and they're not new, they're as old as the rest of it, they're the same brass and the same casting marks, they were built at the same time by the same people. They just aren't on the plan.\"\n\n\"Why would you build a thing and not draw it?\"\n\n\"You wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Then—\"\n\n\"Unless you drew it,\" said Bramwell, \"and then somebody took the drawing out.\"\n\nHe set the scraper down.\n\n\"Third thing,\" he said. \"And this one's mine, and I've never said it to anybody but Growl, and Growl thinks I'm mad, which is fair.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"The year before you were born, the eastern feed went down. You'll not remember it, nobody does, it was nine hours and they said it was a valve.\" Bramwell wiped his hands. \"It wasn't a valve. I was on the gang that went in after. What had happened was one of the Engine house reckoners had set a governor wrong, up on the fourth gallery, and it had put the whole eastern bank about a twelfth out of true, and nine hours later it came back on its own and it came back *right*.\"\n\n\"They fixed it.\"\n\n\"They did not fix it. That's the point. Nobody touched it. There were forty of us stood in that gallery for nine hours and the reckoners were up there arguing about the calculus and not one hand went on that machine, and at the eighth hour the whole eastern bank ran itself back through about eleven positions, one at a time, slow, like somebody counting, and stopped, and the feed came up.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"That's a governor resetting,\" said Marrow. \"That's just a feedback loop. That's not—\"\n\n\"It ran back eleven positions and stopped at the *right one*, son. Not at the middle. Not at the stop. At the one position out of a hundred and something that made the whole eastern bank true, and it did it in eleven separate moves, and there's no linkage in a governor that does that, and I have taken nine hundred governors apart.\" Bramwell picked the scraper up again. \"So either there's a piece of that machine that isn't on the plan and isn't in the books and does something nobody's written down, or I'm an old man who saw what he wanted to see in a bad shift thirty-one years ago.\"\n\n\"Which do you think?\"\n\n\"I think both, most days.\" Bramwell went back to the collar. \"But I'll tell you the bit that keeps me up. It took eight hours. And there were eleven reckoners on that gallery with the best education in this city, shouting at each other in the true calculus, and not one of them worked it out in eight hours. And the machine did it in eleven moves.\"\n\nMarrow held the lamp and did not say anything for a long time.\n\n\"Bramwell.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"What if it wasn't doing it on its own?\"\n\n\"Meaning?\"\n\n\"What if it was waiting for somebody to ask it properly,\" said Marrow, \"and nobody in that gallery could, so after eight hours it gave up and did it itself.\"\n\nBramwell stopped working.\n\nHe sat back on his stool and looked at Marrow for a long moment with the goggles pushed up on his forehead, and there was something in his face that Marrow could not read at all.\n\n\"Don't say that to anyone,\" he said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I've had that thought,\" said Bramwell, \"and I've had it for thirty-one years, and I've never once said it out loud, and you got to it in four weeks off the back of me telling you a story about a valve.\" He turned back to the bench. \"And because the last man I know of who said that sort of thing in public was a reckoner named Halloran and he was writing a book about it, and the book never came out, and he went to a chair at the university in Vaskeld in the summer of the year I turned forty.\"\n\n\"What happened to him?\"\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the trouble. Absolutely nothing happened to him at all. He went to Vaskeld and he had a very nice career and he died at seventy-six and he never wrote another word about the Engine.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Ratchet**\nRatchet liked the boy, which was inconvenient, and he had known it was going to be inconvenient from about the second week.\n\nHis actual name was Ratcliffe and nobody had called him that since he was eleven, and he was twenty, and he had been with the Ashlight for four years, which made him neither new nor anybody. He ran messages. He kept the book on the Tuesday game. He sat on a crate in the yard behind the Ashlight most afternoons eating something out of paper, and because he sat in the yard he saw everybody who came and went, and because he was harmless nobody minded what he saw.\n\nMarrow came through the yard three times in December, and never once to see Vane.\n\n\"He's not here,\" Ratchet said, the first time.\n\n\"I'm not looking for him.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Ratchet moved his legs off the crate. \"Growl's inside.\"\n\n\"I'm not looking for Growl either. There's a man called Ferris does castings out of the shed on the far side and Bramwell wants a pattern off him and won't come himself.\"\n\n\"Why won't he come himself?\"\n\n\"Because Vane's asked him four times,\" said Marrow, \"and he says if he walks through this yard it counts as an answer.\"\n\nRatchet laughed at that for about a minute.\n\n---\n\nBy the third time they had got as far as sitting on the crate together, and Ratchet had a bag of chestnuts and gave him half, and Marrow told him about the eastern feed going down the year before he was born, and Ratchet did not understand one word in three of it and enjoyed himself enormously, because nobody in the Ashlight had ever once talked to him about anything except the Tuesday book.\n\nThen Marrow said, \"What does Vane actually do?\"\n\nAnd Ratchet stopped chewing.\n\nBecause that was the question, and Ratchet had been in the yard four years and had watched perhaps nine boys come through it, and every single one of them had asked that question at some point, and the ones who asked it early were the ones who were going to be all right and the ones who asked it late were already in.\n\n\"Depends who's asking,\" said Ratchet.\n\n\"I'm asking.\"\n\n\"Right. Then, cogging. Parts. Everybody knows that.\" Ratchet turned a chestnut over. \"There's a market in Engine components because the Engine houses hold the writs and if you haven't got a writ you can't buy a bearing, and there's about four thousand small workshops in this city that need bearings and can't have them, so there's a trade. He runs the trade. That's about sixty per cent of it.\"\n\n\"And the rest?\"\n\n\"The rest is that he lends money,\" said Ratchet. \"That's the bit nobody says. And he lends it cheap, and he lends it to people the chalk won't cover, and he does not break your fingers, and that's why half the Under would put him in the Council tomorrow.\"\n\n\"So what's wrong with it?\"\n\nRatchet looked at his chestnuts for a while.\n\n\"He doesn't want it back,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The money. He doesn't want it back. He'd rather you couldn't pay.\" Ratchet put the paper down. \"Because if you can't pay he doesn't do anything about it, he just doesn't do anything about it *for years*, and then one day there's a thing he needs doing and it's you that does it, and you say yes, and you don't say yes because you're frightened, you say yes because you're *ashamed*, and that's a much better hold on a man than frightened. Frightened wears off.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I owe him nine marks from when I was sixteen,\" said Ratchet cheerfully, \"and he's never mentioned it, not once, not in four years, and I think about it about nine times a day.\"\n\nMarrow was quiet.\n\n\"You should stay at Bramwell's,\" said Ratchet.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, you don't. Everybody says they know.\" Ratchet was not looking at him. \"Growl told you it's the last honest room in the Under, right? He tells everybody that. And what he doesn't tell you is why he says it, and it's not because he wants you saved. It's because Growl came up through this yard at fifteen and there was no Bramwell's for him, and every time he sends someone up there it's a bet he's placing against himself nineteen years too late.\"\n\n\"That's not a bad thing.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ratchet. \"But it means he'll only send you once. He's not going to come and get you.\" He picked the paper back up. \"Nobody's going to come and get you. That's the actual thing about the Ashlight and it took me four years. There's no door. Nobody drags you in. You just keep coming through the yard because the yard's warm and people talk to you, and then one day there's a thing that needs doing.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Corvain**\nShe stopped reading the volumes in the middle of December and started finding the people, and it took her three weeks to understand that this had been a mistake, and by then she could not stop.\n\nThe first was a woman called Ada Marchbank, sixty-one, who kept a pipe-fitter's yard off the Grieves Steps and who had been assessed in the second stratum in the sixty-fourth year and amended to the tenth.\n\nCorvain sat in her office among the fittings with a cup of tea she did not want and asked her, carefully, what she remembered about the Calibration.\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Ada Marchbank. \"It was forty-seven years ago.\"\n\n\"Nothing at all?\"\n\n\"I remember I was sick on the way in.\" She shrugged. \"I remember the card. Tenth. And my mother took it very hard and I told her not to be daft because we all knew I wasn't clever.\"\n\n\"Did you think you weren't clever?\"\n\n\"I *knew* I wasn't. I'd had the card.\" Marchbank laughed. \"That's what the card's for, love.\"\n\nAnd Corvain sat there with the volume in her bag, in which it was written in a clerk's hand that Ada Marchbank had scored second stratum out of six hundred and eighty candidates in the sixty-fourth year, second, out of the entire city, and that eleven days later somebody had made it tenth on grounds of adverse conduct, and the conduct was that her father had been banked twice for drink.\n\nShe did not show her.\n\nShe had gone to that yard fully intending to show her. She had the volume in her bag and she had the page marked, and she sat in the office and she looked at a sixty-one year old woman laughing about not being clever, and she understood that if she opened the bag she would be handing over forty-seven years, and that Ada Marchbank had built an entire life on top of that card and had built it *well*, and that there was no version of the next ten minutes that did not take a hammer to it.\n\nShe said thank you for the tea and she left.\n\nShe was sick in an alley off the Grieves Steps and then she went home.\n\n---\n\nThe second was a man called Corrin Sallow, fifty-three, who had been assessed third and amended ninth, and who now worked the water gangs in the outfalls, and who had a wet cough and eleven fingers' worth of scars and who asked her twice whether she was from the Council about the money.\n\nCorvain did not show him either.\n\n---\n\nThe third one she did not choose. The third one chose her, because on the eleventh of January a note came to the duty room at Tallow Street in a careful hand, and it said:\n\n*You have been to see two of them. There will be more. Before you go to a third, come and see me, and I will tell you the thing that neither of them can, which is what it looks like from the other side of the hatch.*\n\n*Ada Voss. Second Examiner. I will be in the reading room on Thursday from sixth bell.*\n\n---\n\nThe reading room on the third floor at Tallow Street was empty on a Thursday evening except for the woman at the far table with thirty green volumes stacked in front of her and the lamp turned low.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer.\"\n\nCorvain sat.\n\n\"I sent you the note about the anomaly in September,\" said Voss.\n\n\"I know. Thank you.\"\n\n\"Don't thank me. I want to be extremely clear about that.\" Voss's hands were flat on the table. \"I did not send you that note because I am brave. I sent it to you rather than to the Grandmaster because sending it to the Grandmaster would have put my name on a file, and sending it to a four-month officer from a good house put *your* name on a file. I chose you because you were expendable and well-connected, which is the safest possible thing for somebody like me to aim at. I have thought about that every day for four months and I would like it said out loud once.\"\n\nCorvain sat with that.\n\n\"All right,\" she said.\n\n\"All right.\" Voss let out a breath. \"Now. You have been to see Marchbank and Sallow, and you did not tell either of them, and you have been sick at least once, and you are about to decide that the humane thing is to stop looking at people and go back to the volumes.\" She looked up. \"Don't. The volumes are the crime. The people are the *reason*, and if you lose the reason you will end up like everybody else who has ever got this far, which is to say you will end up thinking of it as a records problem.\"\n\n\"Has anybody got this far?\"\n\n\"Eleven times,\" said Voss.\n\nCorvain went cold.\n\n\"There is a list,\" Voss said. \"It is not a secret list, that is the extraordinary thing about it, it is simply not a list anybody has ever compiled. But if you sit in the examinations office for nine years and read the internal minutes going back a century, which nobody does because they are the dullest documents ever produced by human hand, you find eleven occasions on which somebody inside this building noticed the amendment pattern and wrote it down.\"\n\n\"What happened to them?\"\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Voss. \"Nothing at all. That is the answer and I am sorry, but you had better have it now.\" She opened the top volume. \"Two of them were promoted. Three were given the review they asked for and the review concluded that the amendment process was operating as designed, which was true. One resigned and went into insurance. One took it to a magistrate and the magistrate found there was no offence, which was also true, because there is no law in Gifford against amending an examination result and there never has been. Four dropped it.\"\n\n\"Four just dropped it.\"\n\n\"Four just dropped it, and I have read what they wrote and I would like you to understand that all four of them were better people than me.\" Voss turned the volume round. \"Here. Sixty-first year. A Second Examiner named Halloran. He wrote nine pages. It is the best thing anybody has ever written about this and it is in an internal minute file that has been opened, by the log, three times in forty-five years, twice by me.\"\n\nCorvain read it.\n\nIt took her twenty minutes and she did not speak once.\n\nThen she said: \"He knew why.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"He knew *why*, and he doesn't say it, he circles it four times and then he stops, and on page seven he says—\" she found it— \"*I have satisfied myself that the practice is not malicious and that its purpose is protective, and I find that I am not able to write down what it protects.*\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I have had forty-five years of other people's spare time to think about that sentence,\" said Voss, \"and I think it means somebody had already told him.\"\n\nShe closed the volume.\n\n\"He left in the summer of that year. Chair of reckoning at the university in Vaskeld. Very fine appointment, everybody said so.\" Voss stood and gathered her coat. \"And I will give you the last thing for nothing, Officer, and then I am going home and I would rather you did not write to me again for a while.\n\n\"Halloran went to Vaskeld in the sixty-first year. Nine years later the Vaskeld council fell and their engine stopped, and it was winter.\"\n\n\"I know about Vaskeld,\" said Corvain. \"Everybody knows about Vaskeld.\"\n\n\"Everybody knows the *number*,\" said Voss. \"Do you know what the number is?\"\n\n\"Forty thousand.\"\n\n\"Forty-one thousand six hundred.\" Voss put her coat on. \"And Halloran was there for it. He was sixty-one years old and he was in that city for the whole of that winter and he survived it, and he never wrote another word about engines for the rest of his life.\"\n\nShe stopped at the door.\n\n\"So when you find out why, Officer Corvain,\" she said, \"and you will, I think, because you are better at this than the eleven were. When you find out why, you want to have decided in advance what you are going to do with a good reason. Because that is what is waiting at the end of this. Not a villain. A perfectly good reason, held by tired people, that has eaten fourteen thousand children.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Bramwell's Trial**\nThe Resonance Regulator sat on the end bench under the caged lamp, and it had been sitting there since the day Marrow first came through the door, under a cloth, which was the bench Bramwell had told him not to sit on.\n\n\"Right,\" said Bramwell, on the second Thursday in January. \"Come here.\"\n\nHe pulled the cloth off.\n\nIt was about the size of a dog: brass and iron, all exposed springs and calibration screws, a cracked glass dial face and a bell housing on top with the bell taken out of it. Filthy. Beautiful, underneath the filth, in the way that things made by people who cared are beautiful whatever state they end up in.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"Resonance regulator off a compressor house. Sootgate, number four house, and it's been dead nine years and they've been running that house nine years without it, which is why everybody within four streets of Kettle Yard has a headache all day and doesn't know why.\" Bramwell folded the cloth. \"There were eleven of these made. This is the last one anybody's got their hands on and the pattern's gone. Nobody can make another because nobody knows what the pattern was.\"\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Bramwell said it flatly. \"I've had it eleven years. I've had it apart nine times. I know what every part of it does and I know exactly what's wrong with it and I cannot fix it, and I'm going to tell you why in a minute, and then you're going to fix it, and then I'm going to decide whether you can stay.\"\n\nHe laid out six components on the bench in a row. He did it slowly and deliberately, and Marrow understood that the order was not accidental.\n\n\"Six,\" said Bramwell. \"The regulator wants three parts I haven't got: a spring, a collar and a cam. Those six are what forty years of cogging has turned up.\n\n\"These two are right.\" He touched the first two. \"Spring and collar. Right size, right material, right taper. Don't thank me, that's the easy end.\n\n\"This one's the cam and it's *close*. Eleven thousandths over on the throw. It'll go in. It'll work. It'll put the whole regulator about a twelfth out and it'll wear the seat in about four years.\n\n\"This one's a cam and it's *right*, and it's cast wrong, there's a void in it, and it'll shear. Might be a month, might be nine years.\n\n\"This one's a cam out of a marine governor and it's the wrong profile entirely and I've only kept it because it's bronze.\n\n\"And this one.\" He picked up the last and turned it in the light, and it was, unmistakably, the finest object on the bench: bright, sharp, unworn, the casting marks crisp. \"This one is a perfect cam. Right profile, right throw, right steel, and it came off a Council dray in November and it is nine sizes too big and it will not go anywhere near that regulator and it never will.\"\n\nHe put it down.\n\n\"There,\" he said. \"That's the whole of it. Go on.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the bench for a long time.\n\n\"There's no right answer,\" he said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"You want me to pick which wrong.\"\n\n\"I want to know *how you pick*,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the only thing I've ever wanted to know about anybody. Any fool can do a job when there's a right answer, the right answer does the work for you. Sit down and don't touch it for a bit. Think.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow sat down on the stool and looked at six pieces of metal for two and a half hours, and Bramwell went and did something at the far end of the shop and did not come back once.\n\nAt the end of the two and a half hours Marrow said:\n\n\"I need to know what it's for.\"\n\nBramwell's voice came from the dark. \"It's a resonance regulator.\"\n\n\"That's what it is. I asked what it's *for*.\" Marrow did not turn round. \"You said number four compressor house has been running nine years without it and everybody within four streets has a headache. So it's not keeping the house safe, because the house hasn't blown up in nine years. It's keeping the house *quiet*. It's damping the beat.\"\n\nA pause.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"So if I put the close cam in, the one that's eleven thousandths over, the regulator runs a twelfth out of true. Which means the damping's a twelfth out. Which means it damps most of it and misses a bit.\" Marrow leaned forward. \"And a headache you can nearly not hear is worse than one you can. That's the thing about a beat. If it's there and you can hear it you get used to it. If it's nearly gone you spend all day listening for it.\"\n\nThe scraping at the far end of the shop had stopped.\n\n\"So not that one,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"Not that one. And not the void one, obviously, because it shears, and when a regulator shears on a live compressor house the housing goes and there's four men on that floor.\"\n\n\"Might be nine years.\"\n\n\"Might be a month, and I'd know I'd done it.\" Marrow picked up the fifth one, the marine cam, and turned it over. \"This one's the wrong profile.\"\n\n\"Completely wrong.\"\n\n\"How wrong?\"\n\nBramwell came out of the dark and stood at the end of the bench.\n\n\"Say what you're thinking,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm thinking a profile's a shape and a shape is a set of numbers,\" said Marrow slowly, \"and this one's bronze, and bronze cuts. And I've swept up eleven pounds of brass swarf out of this shop in three months so there's a lathe in here somewhere under all of it, and the profile I want, I can get. It's on the old cam. The old cam's cracked but the *shape* isn't cracked. I can take the shape off the broken one and put it on this one, and it'll take me about nine days, and I'll ruin it twice first.\"\n\n\"It's bronze,\" said Bramwell. \"Bronze wears. Steel's better. Steel's what it was made in.\"\n\n\"Bronze wears *predictably*.\" Marrow looked up. \"That's the whole thing, isn't it. That's the twelfth on my pressure gauge. Steel that's wrong is wrong and you don't know how much. Bronze that's right wears about a thousandth a year and you can *measure* it, and in nine years somebody comes and puts a shim in, and I'll write down how thick the shim wants to be and leave the note in the housing.\"\n\nBramwell said nothing at all.\n\n\"And the perfect one,\" said Marrow, \"the beautiful one, off the Council dray, I'd sell. Because it's nine sizes too big and it's never going in anything in this shop, and it's worth about four marks to somebody with a dray, and four marks buys the bronze stock for the next one.\"\n\nHe put the marine cam down.\n\n\"That's my answer,\" he said. \"The wrong-shaped one that I can make right, over the right-shaped one that's wrong in a way I can't see. And I keep the note in the housing because in nine years I won't be here and somebody's going to open it up and want to know why there's a bronze cam in a steel machine and think whoever did it was an idiot.\"\n\n---\n\nIt took him eleven days, not nine, and he ruined two blanks, and on the eighth day he had to stop and re-cut the whole profile because he had taken the shape off the cracked cam without accounting for the fact that it was cracked, and the crack had opened the throw by four thousandths, and he had faithfully reproduced the fault.\n\nBramwell watched him find that and said nothing and did not help.\n\nOn the eleventh day they took it up to number four compressor house at fourth bell with the regulator in a sack and Bramwell's writ in his pocket, which was the only writ in the whole of Kettle Yard and which he had held for forty-one years and never once explained.\n\nIt went in.\n\nThey stood on the floor of number four with four fitters and a foreman and watched the bell housing, and the beat came up through the boards the way it had come up for nine years, and then the regulator took hold, and over about forty seconds the beat went out of the floor.\n\nNot most of it. All of it.\n\nThe foreman put his hand flat on the wall and stood there for a bit.\n\n\"That's gone,\" he said.\n\n\"That's gone,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"Nine years.\" The foreman looked round at the fitters. \"Nine years I've had a headache. I thought that was me.\"\n\nMarrow stood in the middle of the compressor floor with a sack in his hand and his ears full of the absence of something, and he did not say anything, because there was a thing happening in his chest that he did not entirely have a name for.\n\nOn the way down the stairs Bramwell said, without looking at him:\n\n\"There's a room upstairs at the shop with a bed in it. It's full of gears. You can have it if you clear it out, and I'll pay you two marks a week and you'll be worth about four, and I'll put it up when you're worth six and not before.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\n\"And the note in the housing.\" Bramwell stopped on the landing. \"You wrote it in the third person. *The cam is bronze. The shim allowance is as follows.* Why?\"\n\nMarrow thought about it.\n\n\"Because if I sign it,\" he said, \"whoever opens it in nine years reads the name first, and if it's a Sirrath name off a ninth stratum they'll stop reading.\"\n\nBramwell looked at him for a long moment.\n\n\"Aye,\" he said. \"Well. That's the first thing you've got wrong in eleven days.\" He went on down the stairs. \"Sign it. Sign the bloody thing. Let them stop reading. It'll still be in there in ninety years and there'll come a day when somebody opens up a machine in this city and finds a Sirrath name on a repair that held, and you'll not be there to see it, and that's the whole reason to do it.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Five.*" },
  { n: 6, title: "Pressure", text: "**I. Marrow**\nHe found out about Ketu on a Tuesday in February, and he found out because he had money.\n\nThat was the joke of it and he saw the joke immediately and it did not help. Four months at Bramwell's, two marks a week and then three, and a room upstairs he had cleared of nine hundredweight of gears, and for the first time in his life Marrow had a little that was his. So on the Tuesday he went up to Ketu's lane with four marks in his coat and a bag of coke and the specific intention of settling her chalk at Marta's, because he had worked out that she was carrying about two marks of it and had been carrying it since the autumn.\n\nMarta looked at the money and then looked at him and then wiped her hands on her apron in a way that took slightly too long.\n\n\"There's no chalk on Ketu,\" she said.\n\n\"There is. Since October.\"\n\n\"There was. It got settled.\" Marta put both hands on the counter. \"Fifth of January. Twelve marks, cleared, the whole of it, and something over for the coke through to March.\"\n\nMarrow stood in the shop with four marks in his hand.\n\n\"Twelve,\" he said.\n\n\"Twelve and four.\"\n\n\"She hasn't got twelve marks. She's never had twelve marks in her life, she cleans stairs, she gets two a week and she's sixty-eight—\"\n\n\"Marrow.\" Marta said it quietly. \"Don't make me be the one.\"\n\n---\n\nHe walked to the end of the lane and stood there for a while and then went in and asked her.\n\nKetu was at the table with the lamp on and a bowl of something. She was very small, these days. She had been enormous when he was nine.\n\n\"Who paid Marta,\" he said.\n\nShe did not look up.\n\n\"Ketu.\"\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"Who paid Marta.\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, boy, I'm not shouting up at you like a bird in a chimney.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\nKetu put her spoon in the bowl and folded her hands, and Marrow watched her do it and understood that she had been expecting this conversation for some time and had prepared for it, and that the preparation had not made it any better.\n\n\"Nine years ago,\" she said, \"you had a fever in the March and you were seven and you did not eat for eleven days.\"\n\n\"I remember.\"\n\n\"You remember the fever. You do not remember the eleven days, because I made sure you did not.\" She turned her hands over. \"There was a woman on Aster Row wanted a night girl as well as a day one and I took it, and I was doing five in the morning to nine at night for six weeks, and it was not enough, because the medicine was six marks a bottle and you had four bottles.\"\n\nMarrow said nothing.\n\n\"Twenty-four marks. In 1889.\" Ketu's voice did not change. \"I did not have twenty-four marks. I did not have four. I went to the Ashlight and I asked, and a man I had never met before came out and sat down with me and did not laugh at me and did not ask me anything about myself, and he lent me twenty-four marks at no interest at all, and he said: pay it when you can.\"\n\n\"And you never paid it.\"\n\n\"I have paid it four times over,\" said Ketu. \"In pieces. Ten grist here and a mark there for nine years, whenever there was anything, and every time I took it up there they wrote it in the book and thanked me and every time the number at the bottom of the book was the same number.\" She looked up at him for the first time. \"Because it is not a debt, boy. I worked that out about the fourth year. It is a *rope*. And I have been paying rope-money into that book for nine years so that nobody ever came to this house and said your name.\"\n\nThe lamp hissed.\n\n\"And on the fifth of January,\" said Marrow, \"somebody went to Marta's and paid twelve marks.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"He came himself,\" said Ketu.\n\n---\n\nThe room over the Ashlight was warm and smelled of oil and tobacco and there was a long table with paperwork on it, and Silas Vane looked up when Marrow came in and did not stand.\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then stand.\" Vane pushed a chair out anyway with his foot and turned back to the table. \"You'll want the ledger. Third book, on the shelf behind you, the green one. Take it down and read it yourself, because if I read it to you you'll think I'm shaping it.\"\n\nMarrow took it down.\n\nIt was all there. Nine years of it, in four different hands. *Ketu, no other given, Sootgate. 24m advanced 12/3/89. No term.* And then nine years of payments in and the running total at the bottom of every page unchanged, and then, on the fifth of January, in a fifth hand, an entry for twelve marks four out, marked *Marta's, cleared*, and beneath it, and this was the thing that made Marrow's hands go cold on the covers, a note in the margin.\n\n*Do not accept further payment from this account.*\n\n\"You closed it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"I closed it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because she is sixty-eight and her knees have gone and she has been putting ten grist a week into that book for nine years out of fear of me, and I have taken it, because taking it was the arrangement.\" Vane put his pen down. \"And because I am going to ask you for something, and I did not want you to be able to tell yourself that you were doing it for her.\"\n\nMarrow shut the ledger.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"All of it. It's true and it's a lie. You closed it in January and she found out in January and I found out in February off Marta, which means you didn't tell me, which means you waited for me to find out on my own, which means you *wanted* me to find out on my own, because if you'd come to Bramwell's and told me, I'd have known what it was.\" He put the ledger on the table. \"You paid twelve marks and you bought a Tuesday afternoon where I walk in here on my own feet. That's what you bought. Twelve marks four.\"\n\nVane looked at him for a moment.\n\nThen he smiled, and it was not a performance, and that was somehow worse.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"That is exactly what I bought, and you are the first one in nineteen years to say it to my face inside the first four minutes, and you have no idea how tiring it is being right about people.\" He sat back. \"So. Now you know what it is. Does it work?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it's still twelve marks she hasn't got to find.\"\n\n\"Quite.\" Vane reached for the decanter. \"Sit down. You may as well hear the job before you say yes to it. There's no dignity in agreeing to something you haven't heard.\"\n\n---\n\nThe job was nothing.\n\nThat was the whole of Vane's method and Marrow could see the method operating and sat in the chair and watched it operate on him.\n\nThere was a compressor house on the Wick, number nine, and it had a writ inspection coming on the Thursday, and Vane needed the inspection moved by eleven days, and to move it by eleven days somebody had to be in the yard behind the Engine house at Aster Row at fourth bell on the Wednesday and put a wedge in the bottom of a particular gate so that a particular dray could not get out until the afternoon post had gone.\n\n\"Nobody is hurt,\" said Vane. \"Nothing is stolen. The dray goes out at two instead of nine. A clerk misses a post. An inspection moves eleven days. There are four men on that compressor floor who keep their places for another quarter and a foreman who does not have to explain a gauge that has been reading low since November.\"\n\n\"Whose gauge?\"\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\n\"So it's a lie to a writ inspector.\"\n\n\"It is a wedge in a gate,\" said Vane. \"The lie is somebody else's and it was told in November. You are being asked to delay a dray.\"\n\nMarrow sat with it.\n\nHe knew exactly what it was. He had sat on a crate in the yard with Ratchet and had it explained to him in December in words of one syllable: *there's no door, nobody drags you in, and then one day there's a thing that needs doing.* He had heard it and he had understood it and he had walked out of that yard thinking that being able to see the shape of a trap was the same as being outside it.\n\n\"Say no,\" said Vane.\n\nMarrow looked up.\n\n\"Say no. Now. Out loud, in this room, and I will not ask you again, and nobody will come to Ketu's house, and the twelve marks are gone and I will not mention them for the rest of my life.\" Vane held his eye. \"I mean it entirely. Go back to Bramwell's, get very good at machines, be a decent man. It is a real offer and it is on the table and it will be off it in about a minute.\"\n\n\"Why would you do that?\"\n\n\"Because I have run this for nineteen years and the ones who are dragged in are worth nothing to me.\" Vane turned his glass. \"They are frightened, and frightened men are stupid and they talk. I have no use whatever for a boy who is here because of his grandmother's chalk. I want the one who does the arithmetic and puts a wedge in a gate on a Wednesday because he has looked at every other option available to a ninth stratum Sirrath in this city and found that they are the drop-forges, the outfalls, portering on the seam, or scrubbing stairs on the Rise until his knees go.\"\n\n\"There's Bramwell's.\"\n\n\"There is Bramwell's,\" Vane agreed. \"There is one Bramwell's, and he is seventy-one, and he has a bad chest, and when he dies the writ dies with him because a writ does not descend to a Sirrath with an amendment. And you know all that, because you are the only person in the Under who has ever worked out that the writ is the whole of what Bramwell has that you cannot get.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"So,\" said Vane. \"Say no.\"\n\nMarrow sat in the warm room over the Ashlight and did the sum, and it came out the way it came out, and he was completely clear-eyed about it, and the clear-sightedness did not help at all.\n\n\"What's the wedge made of?\" he said.\n\n---\n\nHe did it on the Wednesday at fourth bell and it took eleven seconds and nobody saw him and nothing happened.\n\nThat was the part nobody had warned him about. He had braced for the guilt and he had braced for the fear and what he had not braced for was the *nothing*. He put a piece of oak in the bottom of a gate and walked out of the yard and went home and ate his supper, and on the Thursday the inspection moved to the twenty-first, and on the Friday Bramwell shouted at him for four minutes about a bearing race and he laughed about it, and by the Saturday he had noticed that he had not thought about it since Wednesday night.\n\nHe sat on the end of the bed in the room over the shop with a bronze blank in his hands and thought:\n\n*That is how it works. It is not that it feels bad and you do it anyway. It is that it feels like nothing at all, and then the next one is slightly bigger, and it also feels like nothing.*\n\nHe thought: I should stop.\n\nThen he thought, and this was the sentence that he would come back to in a cellar in August with sawdust under his feet:\n\n*I will stop when it stops feeling like nothing.*\n\n---\n\n**II. Corvain**\nThey took the case off her on the twelfth of March at eleven in the morning, and they did it properly, which she had not expected.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Senior Detective Marcus Vale.\n\nHe was sixty-two and heavy through the shoulders and beautifully dressed in a way that had nothing to do with fashion, and he had a file open in front of him, and the file was hers. Not a copy. Hers, with her own pencil in the margins.\n\nAshcombe was standing at the window with his back to the room, which told Corvain everything about the last hour that she needed to know.\n\n\"You've had my file,\" she said.\n\n\"I've had your file since Tuesday. Sit down.\" Vale waited until she did. \"I've read all of it. Sixty-one pages, forty-two of them annexes, and the annexes are better than the report, which is the correct way round and almost nobody does it.\" He closed it. \"It's very good work. I want to say that first, because you're about to decide that I'm your enemy and I'd rather you did it on the evidence.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"You've established that conduct amendments are applied disproportionately to high-scoring candidates. Fourteen thousand two hundred and six cases, hundred and three years, and you've got the assessed results, which nobody's ever pulled together.\" Vale folded his hands. \"It's the best piece of records work this division has produced in my career. Now tell me what offence it discloses.\"\n\nCorvain did not answer.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"None,\" she said.\n\n\"None.\" Vale nodded slowly. \"None. There is no statute in Gifford governing the amendment of an examination result. There is no statute requiring the Calibration to be conducted at all. The Council administers it under prerogative and it may administer it in any manner it chooses, and it has published the strata every year for a hundred and three years, which is more than it is obliged to do.\" He tapped the file. \"You have documented, at very great length, something that is entirely lawful. You know that. It's on page nine, in your own hand: *no offence appears.*\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Then why did you keep going?\"\n\n\"Because it's wrong, sir.\"\n\n\"Right,\" said Vale, and something in his face changed very slightly, and for one moment Corvain thought she had reached him. \"Yes. That's the answer. Hold on to it. Now here's mine.\"\n\nHe drew a second file out from under the first.\n\n\"Ninth of March. A writ inspection at number nine compressor house on the Wick was delayed eleven days by interference with a gate at the Aster Row Engine house yard. Second of February, four hundredweight of bearings out of the Grieves store. November, a gauge substitution at number nine that's been running low four months with four men on the floor.\" He set it down. \"There is an organisation in the Under trading in Engine components without writ and it is turning over something in the region of nine hundred sovereigns a year and I have been trying to put a case on Silas Vane for eleven years. That is an *offence*, Officer. That is about nine of them. And it is going to kill somebody in a compressor house inside two years, and when it does there will be an inquiry and the inquiry will find that this division knew.\"\n\n\"Sir, those aren't connected—\"\n\n\"They are now,\" said Vale. \"As of this morning your file is annexed to mine.\"\n\nThe room went very quiet.\n\n\"Say that again,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Your work is being folded into the Ashlight investigation.\" Vale said it without any pleasure at all, which was the worst thing about him. \"It's a natural fit and it will survive any review. Fourteen thousand children pushed down the strata over a century, disproportionately Rustbound, disproportionately non-human: that is the recruiting ground. It's motive. It's the best explanation anybody has ever produced for why the Under keeps handing Vane its cleverest boys, and it's *true*, Officer, that's the thing you'll want to sit with. Every word of it is true.\"\n\nCorvain sat absolutely still.\n\n\"And in the case as annexed,\" she said, \"the amendments stop being the crime.\"\n\n\"The amendments become context.\"\n\n\"They become the reason a Rustbound gang exists.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"So the finding is that the Under is dangerous because its clever children are embittered. Which means the recommendation is more enforcement in the Under. Which means the Council reads a report that begins with fourteen thousand amended children and ends by asking for eleven more constables in Sootgate.\"\n\nVale did not deny it.\n\n\"That is a possible outcome,\" he said.\n\n\"It's the only outcome.\"\n\n\"It is the *likely* outcome, and I'd have thought you'd take it, because it is eleven more constables than there were yesterday and about four hundred people in Sootgate get a functioning compressor house.\" He stood up. \"I've been doing this thirty years and I'll tell you what I know, and you may take it or leave it. You cannot indict a system. There's no dock big enough. What you can do, on a good day, with a great deal of paper, is put one man in one dock for one thing. And the price of that is always, every single time, that the thing you actually cared about goes in as an annexe.\"\n\n\"And in thirty years how many times have you paid that price?\"\n\n\"Every time,\" said Vale. \"Every single time, and it is why the compressor houses in this city are safer than they were and why the Under is not, and I am not going to pretend to you that those two facts are unrelated.\" He picked up both files. \"You're seconded to me for the duration. You'll write the annexe yourself, because you know the material and because you'd never forgive anybody else doing it. And Officer.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"You have got about four days,\" said Marcus Vale, \"before the volumes go into evidence, at which point they are sealed and neither of us can look at them again without a court order. I'm not telling you that as a kindness. I'm telling you because I'd rather you spent those four days doing something useful than deciding what you think of me.\"\n\nHe went out.\n\nAshcombe stayed at the window a moment longer.\n\n\"Sir,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"He's not lying to you,\" said Ashcombe. \"Not once, not in any part of it. He's the best detective in this building and he means every word and if you go up against him you will lose, and in nine years you'll be him.\" He turned round. \"So don't go up against him. Go round.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"How long have you got?\"\n\n\"Four days.\"\n\n\"Then it's not going to be the volumes, is it,\" said Ashcombe. \"Think, Corvain. What have you got that's not in that room?\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Ivy Thorncastle**\nShe burned them.\n\nIvy had noticed it in November and had said nothing for four months, which for Ivy was an act of self-restraint on roughly the scale of a religious vocation.\n\nRooke got a letter from Ferrow Lane most weeks. Marta the Grocer wrote them and Tessa dictated and the hand was large and careful and the spelling was doing its best. They came up on the Thursday post. Rooke would take hers and put it in her coat and not open it, and then at some point in the evening she would go to the window at the end of the third-floor corridor where nobody went, and read it twice, and then go along to the stair-head where there was a lamp, and burn it.\n\nEvery week. Since October.\n\n\"Why do you burn them?\" Ivy said, finally, on a Thursday in March, having decided at about two o'clock that afternoon that four months was enough.\n\nRooke did not jump. She had known Ivy was there, because she always knew.\n\n\"Because I read them twice,\" she said.\n\n\"That isn't an answer, that's a sentence with the same shape as an answer.\"\n\n\"It's the answer.\" Rooke turned the letter over. \"If I keep them I read them four times. Then six. There was a fortnight in December where I read the November ones every night, all five of them, in order, and by the end of it I could say them, and I was sitting in the fourth-year seminar saying *Nettle asks about you* in my head while a Reader was talking about periodic systems.\" She folded it. \"So I read them twice and burn them. I get the whole of it and then it's gone and I do my work. It's the only thing that's worked.\"\n\nIvy sat down on the window seat.\n\n\"That's the most frightening thing anybody's ever said to me,\" she said.\n\n\"It's just management.\"\n\n\"That's *why* it's frightening.\" Ivy pulled her knees up. \"Rooke, do you know what the rest of us do when we get a letter from home? We read it, and we put it in a box, and in nine years we get it out and we're sentimental about it. That's the normal thing. You've built a *procedure*.\"\n\n\"The normal thing assumes you can afford to be sentimental.\"\n\n\"Everyone can afford to be sentimental.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"You can. There's a difference and I keep having to explain it and I'm getting tired of explaining it, so I'll do it once more and then I'd like to stop. If you're sentimental about your mother's letter it costs you an evening. If I'm sentimental about that letter it costs me the seminar, and if I lose the seminar I lose the placement, and if I lose the placement there is no maintenance allowance and no Contentions and no sixty marks in March, and Barrow the rent man puts five people out on a step. That's the actual chain. It isn't self-pity, it's a chain, and every link in it is real, and you can go and check any of them.\"\n\nIvy was quiet for a while.\n\n\"I looked up the Contention statute,\" she said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you said you could only do it twice and I wanted to know why, and you wouldn't tell me on the steps in December, and I've had three months.\" Ivy picked at the seam of the cushion. \"Article nine. Stakes agreed between the parties. And article eleven, which nobody's read since about 1830, which says a Contention may be refused without dishonour where the declaring party has grounds to believe the matter is not genuinely contested.\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"That's it, isn't it,\" said Ivy. \"You can do it twice. The first time because nobody knew. The second time because somebody with something to prove will still take the bet. And after that everybody in that building knows that a Contention against you is just handing over money, so they invoke article eleven and refuse, and it's not even cowardice, it's *in the statute*.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You've had one.\"\n\n\"I've had one.\"\n\n\"So you've got one left,\" said Ivy, \"and after that there's nothing.\"\n\n\"After that there's nothing.\"\n\nIvy looked at her.\n\n\"Then don't spend it on money,\" she said.\n\nRooke's head came up.\n\n\"Sixty marks was twelve weeks,\" said Ivy. \"You told me that on the step. Twelve weeks, and it's March, and you've had one letter in four months that said the arrears were clear and three that didn't mention the rent at all, and you burned all of them, and I bet you anything you like that you know exactly which week the sixty marks runs out.\"\n\n\"The nineteenth of April.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Ivy nodded. \"So the next sixty marks is another twelve weeks and then you're standing in the same corridor in July with nothing left to stake, and that's if anybody even takes it. You cannot get out of this by winning it twice.\"\n\n\"Then what would you have me do?\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Ivy. \"I'm a mediocre reckoner and I'm going to be a very good historian and this is the first time in my life those two facts have been useful at once.\" She sat forward. \"But I'll tell you what a historian would say. You keep trying to buy time. Twelve weeks, twelve weeks, twelve weeks. Nobody in the history of anything has ever got out of a hole by buying twelve weeks. What you want is a *change in the terms*, and you haven't once gone looking for one, because you're the best reckoner in this building and you've spent five months solving the wrong problem.\"\n\nRooke stood at the window with the letter in her hand.\n\n\"Say the problem,\" she said.\n\n\"Your problem isn't that you haven't got five marks a week,\" said Ivy. \"It's that the only thing anybody in Gifford will pay you for is being a curiosity. They'll pay to watch you beat a lord. They will not pay you for the actual work, because you're fourteen and you're from Ferrow Lane and there's no writ in this city that has ever been issued to either of those things.\"\n\nShe stopped.\n\n\"Oh,\" said Ivy.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Nothing. It's a stupid thought.\"\n\n\"Say it.\"\n\n\"There's no writ,\" said Ivy slowly, \"issued to *either of those things*. But you're not either of those things any more, are you? You're a first stratum scholar of the University of the Engine, and the university holds its own writs, and it issues them under its own seal, and it has done since about eleven years before the Council existed.\" She looked up. \"My aunt holds four.\"\n\nRooke did not move for about nine seconds.\n\nThen she said: \"Where's the library index for university statutes?\"\n\n\"Second gallery, north end, and it's forty volumes and there's no catalogue.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Rooke put the unread letter in her coat pocket, and did not go to the lamp at the stair-head, and Ivy noticed that and said nothing at all about it. \"I'll want your help. You're better at forty volumes with no catalogue than I am.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Ivy, extremely pleased. \"I am.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Grandmaster Osric Kell**\nKell reviewed the year's amendments in the last week of March, as he had done every year for thirty-one years, and it took him a full day, and he did it alone with the door shut.\n\nForty-one files. He read all of them.\n\nHe had a system, and the system was that he read each file completely before he looked at the amendment, so that he had the child in his head before he had the decision. He had built that system in his first year in the post because it had seemed to him then that the least a man could do was know what he was signing.\n\nIt had not occurred to him for about twenty-five years that a system designed to make a thing harder to do, which does not stop you doing it, is not a conscience. It is a ritual.\n\nThe thirty-fourth file was a Sirrath boy out of Sootgate. Assessed fourth stratum. Amended ninth, prior conduct, magistrate's caution at thirteen for the theft of a pressure gauge from a scrap barrow, restitution made, costs eight grist.\n\nKell read the paper.\n\nHe was not required to read the paper. Nobody had read a candidate's actual paper at the review stage in the history of the office; the review was of the amendment, not of the assessment. But the papers were in the file and Kell had read them for thirty-one years, and he opened this one and worked through it as he would have worked through any of them.\n\nIt took him fifty minutes.\n\nThe boy had got section nine. He had got it slowly, with two false starts that he had marked himself and gone back to, and one of the false starts was a genuinely interesting error, the kind of error you can only make by understanding what you are doing.\n\nAnd he had got the load across the arch.\n\nKell sat back in his chair and looked at the last page of a fourteen year old's examination for some time.\n\nFour hundred and eleven human candidates in the main hall had not got the load across the arch. Kell knew that number because he had read the marking returns that morning. Four hundred and eleven, and every one of them was sitting today in the stratum the marking had put them in, and this boy was in the ninth, which said *limited aptitude*, and would be in the ninth in the ledger in a hundred years.\n\nHe drew the amendment schedule toward him and uncapped his pen and did not sign it.\n\nHe sat with the pen in his hand for a long time.\n\n---\n\nWhat Kell thought about, in that hour, was the sixty-first year.\n\nHe had been thirty-one. He had been Second Examiner then, on the west floor, and there had been a First Examiner named Halloran who had written nine pages about the amendment pattern and put them into the minute file, and Kell had read them, because Halloran had asked him to.\n\nHe remembered the conversation with complete clarity. They had been in the reading room. Halloran had been sixty-one, which Kell had thought at the time was extremely old, and he had said:\n\n*I have written it down, Osric. That's all I can do. I have written it down properly and put it where it can be found, and one day somebody will find it, and I'd like there to be a name on it.*\n\nAnd Kell had said: *Why not publish?*\n\nAnd Halloran had looked at him for a while and had then said: *Because they've told me why.*\n\n*Then tell me.*\n\n*No.*\n\n*Why not?*\n\nAnd Halloran had said: *Because you're thirty-one and you're going to run this office one day, and if you know why, you will do exactly what I am doing, and you will do it for forty years, and you will do it well. And if you don't know why, there is a chance, a small one, that you will do something stupid.*\n\n*I'd rather know.*\n\n*I know you would,* Halloran had said. *That's precisely why I'm not telling you.*\n\nHe had gone to Vaskeld in the summer.\n\nKell had spent about nine years being angry about that conversation and about twenty-two years understanding it, and had never once, in either period, gone looking for the answer.\n\nThat was the thing he thought about with the pen in his hand. Not the amendments. He had made his peace with the amendments in the sixty-fourth year and the peace had held, on the grounds that the practice was not malicious and that its purpose was protective, which was Halloran's own phrase and Kell had borrowed it and used it on himself for thirty-one years like a man borrowing a coat.\n\nWhat he thought about was that he had had a whole career and had never asked.\n\nHalloran had been offered the answer and had taken it and had let it stop him. Kell had been offered the choice of not knowing and had taken that instead, and had told himself for three decades that it was integrity, and it had been convenience, and there was a Sirrath boy in the ninth stratum with a note in the margin about a pressure gauge and the load across the arch answered correctly on the last page.\n\nKell put the pen down.\n\nHe did not tear up the schedule. He was seventy years old and he was not a fool and he understood, entirely, that a Grandmaster of Examinations who refused to sign the annual amendment review would be a former Grandmaster of Examinations by Thursday and the schedule would be signed by somebody else on Friday, and forty-one children would be in exactly the same place with the difference that nobody in that office would ever read a candidate's paper again.\n\nInstead he did two things, and both of them took about four minutes, and one of them was cowardice and one of them was not, and he was clear with himself about which was which.\n\nThe first thing was that he signed the schedule.\n\nThe second thing was that he wrote a note, in his own hand, on examinations office paper, and sealed it, and addressed it to the officer whose name he had seen on a requisition refusal in November and again on a records access log in December.\n\nIt said:\n\n*Officer Corvain.*\n\n*You have been refused the historical returns twice on procedural grounds and I signed both refusals. The second refusal was correct. The first was not, and I signed it because I did not wish to spend the attention.*\n\n*The internal minute file of this office is not covered by the requisition procedure, because when the procedure was drafted in the fifty-ninth year nobody thought of it. It is held in the reading room on the third floor and it is open to any officer of the Council on production of a warrant card. It has been open for forty-five years. Nobody has ever asked.*\n\n*Sixty-first year. First Examiner Halloran. Nine pages.*\n\n*I am seventy years old and I have signed thirty-one of these and I am not going to pretend to you that this note constitutes anything. But you are going to be told, quite soon, that a great many careful people have looked at this and concluded that it is necessary, and when you are told that I would like you to know that at least one of them never looked at all.*\n\n*O. Kell.*\n\nHe sealed it and put it in the tray for the Thursday post.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Case Race**\nShe had four days and she spent the first of them doing nothing, which was the hardest thing she had ever made herself do.\n\nAshcombe's question sat in her chest all Tuesday. *What have you got that's not in that room?*\n\nThe volumes were going into evidence on the Friday. A hundred and three of them, sealed, and after that nobody could open one without a court order, and the court order would go through the Council, and Corvain had spent enough time in that building now to know exactly what would happen to it.\n\nSo: not the volumes.\n\nShe had her own report, and Vale had that. She had her annexes, and Vale had those. She had her notes, and they were copies of the volumes and worthless without them, because a set of figures in a young officer's handwriting is not evidence of anything, it is an allegation about a book.\n\nThat was the trap and it was a good one and Vale had not even had to set it. In four days the entire evidential basis of a hundred and three years of amendments would be sitting in a sealed evidence room in this building, lawfully, as an annexe to a case about stolen bearings.\n\nOn Wednesday morning Corvain went up to the reading room on the third floor at Tallow Street and started on the thirty volumes that anybody could read.\n\n---\n\nThe reading room set was thirty years. That was the whole of it: the last thirty annual returns, bound, on an open shelf, available to any member of the public who signed the book.\n\nAnd the reading room returns did not contain the assessed results.\n\nThat was the thing that had sent her down to the sub-level in the first place. The published returns gave the strata: how many candidates in each of the eleven, by district, by year. They did not give amendments at all. The word did not appear. Whatever had been done to fourteen thousand children over a century had been done entirely inside the building, and outside the building there was simply a list of how many children had come out in each stratum.\n\nCorvain sat down at a table with thirty volumes and a sheet of paper and no assessed results and thought: then there is nothing here.\n\nAnd then, at about four in the afternoon, she thought: unless there is.\n\n---\n\nIt took her nine hours over two days and she got it at about two in the morning on the Friday, and when she got it she sat in the empty reading room and laughed until she had to put her head on the table.\n\nBecause the published returns gave the strata *by district*.\n\nEleven strata. Nineteen districts. Thirty years. Six and a half thousand numbers, all of them public, all of them printed by the Council itself every summer for a hundred and three years and posted on eleven boards on Tallow Street where anybody could read them, and Corvain had walked past those boards forty times.\n\nAnd here was the thing.\n\nIf the Calibration was what Kell said it was in the hall every year, if it asked one question of every child in the same words and sorted them by what they showed, then the strata were a *sorting*. And a sorting has a shape. A big cohort sorted by ability produces the same shape every time, in every district, with the middle fat and the ends thin, and the districts differ in where the middle sits, because Ferrow Lane children are hungrier and worse taught than Aster Row children and that shows up, and it should show up, and it does.\n\nBut the shape stays the same shape. Poorer district, same shape, shifted. That is what sorting does.\n\nCorvain laid out nineteen districts by thirty years and looked at the shapes.\n\nEleven of the nineteen were fine.\n\nEight were not.\n\nIn eight districts, all eight of them in the Under, all eight of them the eight with the highest non-human population in the city, the shape had a *dent* in it. Not at the bottom. Near the top. In the fourth, third and second strata there were consistently, year after year, for thirty years, fewer children than the shape required, and in the ninth and tenth there were consistently more.\n\nThe dent was small. In any one district in any one year it was three or four children and it looked like nothing at all, which is why it had sat on eleven public boards for a hundred and three years.\n\nAcross eight districts and thirty years it was four thousand one hundred and eleven children.\n\nAnd it was in the *published* figures. Under the Council's own seal. Available to anybody who signed the book.\n\nCorvain sat in the reading room at two in the morning with six and a half thousand public numbers in front of her and understood that she did not need the volumes at all.\n\nThe volumes proved *who*. She had lost the volumes and she was not going to get them back.\n\nThe public returns proved *that it happened*, and they proved it out of the Council's own printing office, and they could not be sealed into an evidence room, because there were sets of them in nine libraries and the university held one and the boards went up outside on Tallow Street every summer in front of six hundred children and their mothers.\n\nYou cannot seal a thing you have already posted on a wall.\n\n---\n\nAt half past four in the morning she wrote it up. Eleven pages, no annexes, no rhetoric, the arithmetic and nothing else, and she checked it four times and then walked across the seam in the dark to the enforcement building and put it on Ashcombe's desk with a note.\n\n*Sir. I went round.*\n\n*This is entirely from the published returns. Every figure in it is on the open shelf in the third floor reading room at Tallow Street and has been printed by the Council every summer since the founding. There is nothing in it from the volumes. Detective Vale can seal the evidence room on Friday and it will not touch one line of this.*\n\n*It does not prove who did it or why. It proves that it was done, and it proves it out of the Council's own numbers, and it cannot be taken back.*\n\n*You said you would like the chance to decide what you are before somebody decided it for you. I think this is that. I am sorry. E.C.*\n\nThen she went and stood on the step of the enforcement building in the cold as the fog started coming up off the Under, and she thought about Ada Marchbank laughing in a pipe-fitter's yard about not being clever, and she was not sick this time, and she thought that was probably the worst thing that had happened to her all year.\n\nAt nine in the morning a boy from the Tallow Street post brought her a sealed note in a seventy year old hand.\n\n*Sixty-first year. First Examiner Halloran. Nine pages.*\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Six.*" },
  { n: 7, title: "Whistle's Price", text: "**I. Three Doors**\nBy the middle of April all three of them had arrived at the same wall from three different sides, and none of them knew the others existed.\n\n**Rooke** had spent nine days in the second gallery of the library with Ivy Thorncastle and forty volumes of university statute and no catalogue, and had found what she was looking for on the seventh day, and had then spent two days establishing that it was useless.\n\nThe university did hold its own writs. It had held them since eleven years before the Council existed. It could issue them under its own seal to any member of the university for the purposes of instruction or enquiry, and there was no age qualification anywhere in the statute, and Ivy had been so pleased about that she had actually shouted in a library.\n\nAnd then Rooke had read the schedule of writs the university had actually issued in the last forty years, and there were nine hundred and eleven of them, and every one was for instruction, and not one was for trade.\n\n*Enquiry* was the word she needed and *enquiry* did not mean what she needed it to mean. It meant you could buy a barometer.\n\nShe sat in the gallery with the ninth volume open on her knees and understood that she had spent nine days finding a door that led into the same room.\n\nSo she wrote to the address on Cull Alley for the second time.\n\n**Corvain** had read Halloran's nine pages four times and then done what Halloran had not done, which was to go looking for the mechanism rather than the pattern.\n\nShe had her eleven pages. Ashcombe had them, and had read them, and had said nothing for two days and then said *right*, which she had learned by now was the most alarming thing he said. And the eleven pages proved that it happened, out of the Council's own printing, and could not be sealed.\n\nBut Vale had been right about one thing, and it had gone into her like a splinter and stayed. *You cannot indict a system. There's no dock big enough.*\n\nTo put a thing in a dock you need a hand on it. And Corvain had a hundred and three years of amendments and fourteen thousand children and not one single name, because there had not been a signature on an amendment since the fifty-first year. It was initialled by a clerk under a delegated authority, and the delegation was renewed every quarter by an office rather than a person.\n\nWhich office?\n\nShe had spent three weeks on that and had got nowhere at all, because the answer was recorded nowhere she could reach, and the four people who knew it were clerks, and the last clerk she had spoken to had changed a docket slip two hours too late and had not slept properly since.\n\nSo she took four sovereigns out of her own account and went back to Cull Alley.\n\n**Marrow** had put a wedge in a gate in March and had not thought about it since the Wednesday night, and that fact had frightened him more than anything in his life.\n\nSo he had gone at it the only way he knew, which was the bench. He had asked Bramwell one question and Bramwell had refused to answer it, which was a first, and the question was: *how many people work inside the Engine houses?*\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you said there were eleven of you on the gang and you never got past the second gallery, and you said half of it's not on the plan, and you said the eastern bank reset itself in eleven moves and eleven reckoners on a gallery couldn't do it in eight hours.\" Marrow had put the file down. \"Eleven reckoners. On one gallery. On one shift. How many are there altogether?\"\n\nAnd Bramwell had said, \"Don't,\" and gone to the far end of the shop, and that had been the whole of it.\n\nSo Marrow walked to Cull Alley on a Sunday with three marks in his coat, which was everything he had that was not owed to anybody.\n\n---\n\n**II. What Whistle Charged**\nThe shop had a low ceiling and Whistle had not stood up in it for nineteen years.\n\nThey came on three separate days in the same week, which Whistle noted, and did not mention to any of them.\n\n\"You have been here before,\" Whistle said, to each of them.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"Twice,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Once, in September, and you told me nobody signs an amendment,\" said Marrow. \"You told me that for nothing and I've thought about it for seven months and I'd like to buy the rest.\"\n\nTo each of them Whistle said the same thing, in the same voice, which was low and slow and came from a long way down inside a very large chest:\n\n\"I do not sell answers. I sell what it costs me to have found them out, and what it costs me is different for each of you, because the thing I would have to hand over is different.\" A pause. \"So I will set you a problem instead. If you can answer it, I will tell you what you came for, and you will pay me half. If you cannot answer it, you will pay me nothing and go home, and that is not a kindness, it is that you would not be able to use the answer and I do not sell things to people who cannot use them.\"\n\n\"That's not how brokers work,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"It is how this one works. Sit down. The stool, not the crate, the crate has a ledger under the cloth.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Rooke's Problem**\nWhistle put a bound volume on the counter. Green, gilt-stamped, old.\n\n\"You will know what that is.\"\n\n\"It's a published return.\" Rooke turned it round. \"Sixty-fourth year. That's the summer figures, they post them on the boards. Anybody can read this.\"\n\n\"Anybody can. Nobody does.\" Whistle laid a hand flat on it, and the hand covered most of the cover. \"In there is the strata by district for the sixty-fourth year. I have the full run. A hundred and three of them. I have had them for nineteen years and I bought them one at a time from a bindery on the Wick because nobody else wanted them.\"\n\n\"What's the problem?\"\n\n\"The problem is one question,\" said Whistle, \"and you may have the run of the volumes for as long as you like, and you may have a slate, and I will make tea and not speak to you. And the question is this.\n\n\"There is one thing in a hundred and three years of those returns that has never happened. Tell me what it is.\"\n\nRooke looked at the volume.\n\n\"That's not a problem, that's a search,\" she said. \"There's six and a half thousand numbers per volume. A hundred and three volumes is most of a million. You want me to find the absence of something in a million numbers and you haven't told me what kind of thing.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That could take a year.\"\n\n\"It took me eleven months,\" said Whistle, \"and I am not going to pretend otherwise, and I did it with a slate and no instruction and I was thirty-four. You are fourteen and you came first stratum out of six hundred and forty-one and Professor Thorncastle has moved you into the fourth-year seminar in your first term, which I know because I know what is worth knowing.\" The great head tilted. \"I do not expect you to search. Searching is what I did. I expect you to think about what kind of thing could be missing, and then look in one place.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke sat on the stool for forty minutes and did not open a volume.\n\nWhistle made tea and did not speak.\n\nWhat she thought about was Ivy in the corridor in March, saying: *you're the best reckoner in this building and you've spent five months solving the wrong problem.*\n\n*So do not search. Think about the shape.*\n\nA published return is a sorting. Six hundred and forty-one children go into eleven strata. Nineteen districts. That is the whole object. And the question is not *what is the smallest number in there*, because Whistle would not set a question with an arithmetic answer, and it is not *which district does worst*, because everybody in Gifford already knows the answer to that and it is Sootgate.\n\n*Something that has never happened. Not never happened in one district. Never happened at all, in a hundred and three years, in a million numbers.*\n\n*Which means it must be something structural. Something that the shape of a sorting says should happen sometimes, by accident, in a hundred and three tries.*\n\nRooke put the tea down.\n\n*The strata are eleven. There are six hundred-odd children a year. If you sort six hundred children by ability into eleven bands, the top band is going to have — what?*\n\n*It depends where you put the cuts. And the cuts are the Council's business. But if the cuts are fixed, then the number in the top band varies year on year, because cohorts vary. Some years you get three brilliant children and some years you get none, and over a hundred and three years you would expect both.*\n\nShe reached over and opened the sixty-fourth year at the strata table.\n\nFirst stratum: **1**.\n\nShe turned to the sixty-fifth. First stratum: **1**.\n\nSixty-sixth. **1**.\n\nRooke went very still.\n\nShe opened four more volumes at random, from four different decades, and then she got up off the stool and went along the counter and opened eleven more, fast, and then she stopped and stood with both hands flat on the wood.\n\n\"It's always one,\" she said.\n\nWhistle said nothing.\n\n\"A hundred and three years.\" Rooke's voice had gone strange. \"There has never been a year with two people in the first stratum. There has never been a year with none. It is one, every single year, since the founding.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That's impossible.\" She was talking fast now. \"That is not what sorting does. If the cut is fixed you get variation. You get a year with three and a year with none, you get *ties*, there are six hundred children and the top two are going to be within a mark of each other about one year in four, and in a hundred and three years there should be twenty-odd years where you cannot separate them and you'd have to put two in. There are none. And there should be five or six years where the cohort was poor and nobody reached the cut. There are none.\" She looked up. \"It's one. Exactly one. Every year for a hundred and three years.\"\n\n\"Say what that means,\" said Whistle.\n\nRooke stood in a shop on Cull Alley with a low ceiling and heard herself say it.\n\n\"It means the first stratum isn't a stratum,\" she said. \"It's a *post*. It's one appointment a year. They're not measuring where the top of the cohort falls, they're filling a chair, and if two people are good enough then one of them doesn't get in and if nobody's good enough then somebody gets in anyway.\"\n\nWhistle put the tea things away.\n\n\"That is the answer,\" Whistle said. \"You took fifty-one minutes and I took eleven months. Now you may ask me what you came for.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Corvain's Problem**\n\"Four sovereigns,\" said Corvain, and put them on the counter.\n\nWhistle did not touch them.\n\n\"Your question is which office renews the delegated authority for conduct amendments.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You have spent three weeks on it.\"\n\n\"Twenty-three days.\"\n\n\"And you have got nowhere, and you know why you have got nowhere, and I want you to say it out loud before we go on.\"\n\nCorvain sat on the stool.\n\n\"Because I've been looking for it in the examinations office,\" she said, \"and it isn't there.\"\n\n\"Why isn't it there?\"\n\n\"I don't know. That's what I'm buying.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Whistle. \"That is what you think you are buying. Here is your problem, Officer, and it is not a problem about examinations at all.\"\n\nThe great hand went into a pigeonhole and came out with a folded sheet.\n\n\"That is a list of every standing delegated authority in the Council of Gifford. All of them. Four hundred and eleven. It is not a secret document; it is printed annually for the audit and there are nine copies and one of them is in the Council library and you could have had it in November for the asking.\" Whistle laid it flat. \"Every entry gives the authority, the office that holds it, and the renewal interval. The amendment authority is on that list. I am not going to tell you which line it is.\n\n\"Your problem is to find it without reading four hundred and eleven lines, and you are going to do that by thinking about what an office is.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the sheet.\n\n\"That's a bureaucratic problem,\" she said.\n\n\"It is the only kind you are good at,\" said Whistle, \"and you should stop being ashamed of that. The girl who was in here on Tuesday would look at this sheet and see four hundred and eleven lines and start reading. You will not, because you have spent five months in a records office and you know something she does not, which is that a delegated authority is not a permission. It is a *liability transfer*.\"\n\nCorvain sat forward.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"A delegation exists so that a decision may be taken without the person who is answerable for it being present.\" Whistle folded both hands. \"So. Do not ask who wants the power. Ask who cannot afford to be in the room.\"\n\n---\n\nCorvain looked at the four hundred and eleven lines for about ninety seconds and then stopped looking at them.\n\n*Do not ask who wants the power. Ask who cannot afford to be in the room.*\n\n*The amendments are ordered. Somebody decides that a fourth stratum Sirrath boy becomes a ninth stratum Sirrath boy, and the decision is real, and it is taken every year forty-odd times, and it is initialled by a clerk under a delegation. And the delegation is renewed quarterly.*\n\n*Quarterly is fast. Quarterly is not how you renew a permission you are comfortable with. Quarterly is how you renew a thing you want to be able to stop.*\n\n*And who is not in the room?*\n\nShe thought about Kell. Kell was in the room; Kell signed the annual review, and the review was of the amendments. So the examinations office was in the room, and the examinations office was the one everybody would look at, which meant the examinations office was the front.\n\n*Who has never appeared once in fourteen thousand files?*\n\nAnd then she had it, and it was so simple and so enormous that she said it out loud in a flat voice like somebody reading a weather report.\n\n\"The Engine houses.\"\n\nWhistle said nothing.\n\n\"It's the Engine houses.\" Corvain's hands were flat on the counter. \"Not the Council. Not the examinations office. The amendments are ordered by the people who run the machine.\"\n\n\"Find the line,\" said Whistle.\n\nCorvain turned the sheet round and ran her finger down it, and she did not read four hundred and eleven lines, she read the twenty-nine that belonged to the Engine houses, and it was the ninth of those.\n\n*Authority: assessment amendment, Calibration, all strata. Held: Office of the Chief Engineer, Engine Houses, Aster Row. Renewal: quarterly. Exercised by: delegated clerks, Examinations Office, Tallow Street.*\n\nIt had been printed every year for the audit. There were nine copies. One of them was in the Council library.\n\nCorvain put her head in her hands.\n\n\"A hundred and three years,\" she said, into her palms.\n\n\"Nineteen years for me,\" said Whistle. \"I found it in the fourth. I have been sitting on it for fifteen and nobody has ever asked me the right question until this afternoon.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you tell somebody?\"\n\nThe Laachmi looked at her for a long moment over the half-spectacles.\n\n\"Officer,\" said Whistle. \"I am seven feet tall and I have horns and I live in a shop with a ceiling I cannot stand up under, and I came to this city because my own people put me out, and the strata figures for my district have a dent in them that I could see from the age of nineteen. Who, precisely, do you imagine I was going to tell?\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Marrow's Problem**\n\"Three marks,\" said Marrow. \"That's everything I've got that isn't owed.\"\n\n\"Keep it.\"\n\n\"I'm not taking charity off you.\"\n\n\"I did not say I would not charge you. I said keep it, because I am going to charge you something else.\" Whistle reached under the counter. \"You asked Bramwell how many people work inside the Engine houses and he refused to answer, and you came here, and you want the number.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You will have the number for nothing, because the number is published. Four hundred and eleven reckoners hold writs to work inside the Engine houses. It is in the Council register and it has been in the Council register every year since the fifty-first.\"\n\nMarrow sat down slowly.\n\n\"Four hundred and eleven.\"\n\n\"Now here is your problem,\" said Whistle, \"and it is the one I have been keeping for nine years for somebody with your hands.\n\n\"In the founding generation there were twelve. The Twelve built it and the Twelve ran it, and for the first eleven years of the Turn there were twelve men and women with their hands on that machine and nobody else at all. It is in the same register. Volume one, page one.\n\n\"Twelve, then. Four hundred and eleven now. And the Engine has not been extended since the nineteenth year; every plan set is a hundred and three years old and every bank in that hole was cast in the founding decade. The machine is the same machine.\n\n\"Your problem is this. Nothing about the work has changed. Nothing about the machine has changed. Explain the number.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow sat in the shop and looked at his own hands for a long time.\n\nHe thought about Bramwell saying *it isn't cleverness, son, it's nine hundred governors.*\n\nHe thought about eleven reckoners on the fourth gallery for eight hours, shouting at each other in the true calculus, while a bank of brass ran itself back through eleven positions and stopped at the right one.\n\n\"There's four ways it could go,\" he said.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"One: the machine's got harder to run. It hasn't, because it's the same machine and it's a hundred and three years old and it's been serviced, so if anything it's better understood, not worse.\n\n\"Two: they've got more to do. They haven't. Output's flat. I've read the output figures, they're on the writ notices in every compressor house in the Under, the Engine's put out the same in every decade since the twenties.\n\n\"Three: they're doing it better. Four hundred and eleven people doing a twelve-person job to a much higher standard.\" He looked up. \"Except you don't get a higher standard out of thirty-four times the people. That's not how work goes. You get thirty-four times the arguing. And the eastern feed went down for nine hours the year before I was born and eleven of them on a gallery couldn't put it right.\"\n\n\"And four?\"\n\nMarrow was quiet.\n\n\"Four is that it takes four hundred and eleven of them now,\" he said, \"to do what twelve of them used to do.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"Which means they can't do it,\" said Marrow. \"Individually. Not one of them can do what one of the Twelve could do, so they do it in committee, four hundred and eleven of them, with procedures and standing orders and a house full of clerks, and it *works*, mostly, because you can get a long way on procedure if the machine is stable and somebody wrote the procedures down properly a hundred years ago. And when something happens that isn't in the procedures, like the eastern feed, they stand on a gallery for eight hours and shout.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"They don't know how it works,\" he said. \"That's not a suspicion any more, is it. Four hundred and eleven is the *measurement*. That's what it costs to run a machine you don't understand.\"\n\nWhistle sat back, and the crate creaked under seven feet of Laachmi.\n\n\"That is the answer,\" Whistle said. \"You took nine minutes. I want you to know that, because you will spend the rest of your life being told what a ninth stratum is worth. Nine minutes.\"\n\n\"What are you charging me?\"\n\n\"You are going to fix my ceiling,\" said Whistle. \"There is a joist over the back of this shop that has been three inches too low for nineteen years and I have not been able to stand up in my own premises since I was thirty-four, and every builder in the Under has told me it is load-bearing and cannot be moved, and I do not believe them, and neither will you when you look at it.\"\n\nMarrow looked up at the joist.\n\nHe looked at it for about a minute and a half.\n\n\"It's not load-bearing,\" he said. \"It's a strut off the old dye-works frame and the frame came out in about the sixties, you can see where they cut it. That's holding nothing at all. It's been holding nothing for forty years.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Whistle.\n\n---\n\n**VI. What They Bought**\nTo each of them, Whistle told the same thing.\n\nNot the same words. Whistle was a professional and gave each of them the piece they had paid for and the piece they could use, and let them put the rest of it together in the street on the way home, which they all three did, within about eleven minutes, in three different districts, at three different times in the same week.\n\nTo **Rooke**, who had found that the first stratum was one appointment a year:\n\n\"You are this year's.\"\n\n\"This year's what?\"\n\n\"You are not a scholar. There are nine hundred scholars in that building and they are all scholars.\" Whistle folded the volume shut. \"There is one first stratum a year and it has been one a year for a hundred and three years, and every one of them goes up the Rise, and every one of them takes seven years of instruction, and every one of them ends in the Engine houses. Every one. I have the list. A hundred and three names, and there are four on it who died and two who went abroad and ninety-seven who went to Aster Row.\"\n\nRooke stood very still.\n\n\"You are the appointment,\" said Whistle. \"You were the appointment on the morning they made you sit again.\"\n\nTo **Corvain**, who had found the office:\n\n\"Now ask yourself what the Chief Engineer wants.\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Yes you do. You have known for a month and you have not let yourself finish the sentence, because you are a decent person from a good house and you have been raised to think this is about contempt.\" Whistle put the audit sheet away. \"Officer. If you ran a machine you could not repair, and your right to run it rested on a claim that your family could hear it, and once a year the city held a public examination that found the child in Gifford who could actually hear it best, what would you need to do about the other forty who could nearly do it?\"\n\nCorvain did not answer for a long time.\n\n\"You'd need there to be one,\" she said.\n\n\"You would need there to be exactly one,\" said Whistle, \"every year, for ever. Not none, because you need her. Not two, because two is a comparison. One.\"\n\nTo **Marrow**, who had found the number:\n\n\"You asked me in September who set up the amendments so that nobody signs them.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It was not the Council and it was not the examiners. The amendment authority is held by the Office of the Chief Engineer at Aster Row and has been since the fifty-first year, and it is renewed every quarter, and it is exercised by clerks at Tallow Street who have never seen the true calculus in their lives.\" Whistle's voice did not change. \"It was not done to you out of spite, and I am sorry, because spite would be easier to carry. Your paper was read by somebody who understood exactly what section nine was worth, and they moved you down five strata because you had done well, and they did it in four lines because four lines is all it takes and because nobody has ever had to explain it to anybody.\"\n\nMarrow sat with his hands on his knees.\n\n\"How many of us?\" he said. \"Not this year. All of it.\"\n\n\"Fourteen thousand two hundred and six.\"\n\n---\n\n**VII. Whistle, After**\nThey went out one at a time, on three days, and Whistle sat in the shop with the lamp hooded and did the accounts.\n\nFifty-one minutes, twenty-three days and nine minutes.\n\nThe girl had seen the shape of it out of a single number repeated a hundred and three times. The officer had found the mechanism by asking who could not afford to be in the room. The Sirrath had measured the whole collapse of a ruling class out of a staffing figure printed in a public register.\n\nThree ways in. One room.\n\nWhistle had known all of it for fifteen years and had told nobody, because there had been nobody to tell, and because a Laachmi who publishes the Council's arithmetic is a Laachmi who is found in an outfall, and because, if Whistle was honest, and Whistle was occasionally honest late at night, the knowing had been worth something in itself. Nineteen years of being the only person in Gifford who had done the sum.\n\nThat was over now.\n\nWhistle got up, and stood up, which took a moment, because it had not been done under this ceiling for nineteen years and the body had made its own arrangements.\n\nThe joist had come out on the Thursday. It had taken the boy four hours and two props and a note left in the wall cavity, signed, in a Sirrath name.\n\nWhistle stood in the middle of the shop at full height with the top of the horns eleven inches clear of the ceiling and looked at four thousand pigeonholes from a place nobody had ever looked at them from.\n\nThen Whistle wrote one line on a slip, and put it in the empty pigeonhole where the other slip had been sitting since December, and the other slip said *they will all three come back, raise the price.*\n\nThe new slip said:\n\n*They did not need me. They needed each other and they do not know it. Tell them.*\n\nWhistle looked at that for a while.\n\nThen took it out again, and tore it in half, and wrote instead:\n\n*Do not tell them. They will get there, and it will be theirs, and that is worth more than the fee.*\n\nAnd put it in the hole, and hooded the lamp, and went to bed under a ceiling three inches higher than it had been the week before.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Seven.*" },
  { n: 8, title: "The Line", text: "**I. Marrow**\nThe seduction was not money and it was not fear. It was being asked a good question.\n\nMarrow understood that afterwards, in the cellar, with sawdust in his mouth, and he understood it completely, and understanding it did not undo one single thing.\n\nIt happened in June, in the room over the Ashlight, and it took about nine minutes.\n\n\"Sit down,\" said Vane. \"I want to ask you something technical and I want you to answer it as a technical question, because that is what it is.\"\n\n\"Right.\"\n\n\"Suppose a man wanted to put the Engine out.\"\n\nMarrow's hands went still on his knees.\n\n\"I am not asking you to do anything,\" said Vane, \"and I want that on the table before we start. I am asking you a question in the way a man asks an engineer a question, and you may refuse to answer it and I will not raise it again. But I would rather ask you than the four fools downstairs who think a bomb is an argument.\"\n\n\"Why would you want the Engine out?\"\n\n\"I do not want the Engine out.\" Vane poured two glasses and pushed one across, and Marrow did not touch it. \"I want it out for *nine hours*, at a time of my choosing, in a way they cannot fix, and I want four hundred and eleven of the best-paid men in this city stood on a gallery in front of witnesses being visibly, publicly, undeniably unable to fix it.\"\n\n\"Then people die.\"\n\n\"In winter, yes. In July, no.\" Vane sat back. \"That is precisely the calculation and I have done it and I would like to walk you through it, because you are going to check it anyway.\n\n\"Vaskeld was February. Their engine went down in February and stayed down for eleven days and it killed forty-one thousand six hundred people, and every one of them died of cold or of thirst or of what happens when a city's water stops moving. Nobody died in the first day. Almost nobody died in the first three. The dying was the eleven days and the February.\n\n\"Gifford in July, for nine hours.\" He held up a hand and counted it off. \"The water towers hold thirty hours. The gas holders hold two days. The hospitals have their own compressors and the compressors have coke on site. Nobody freezes in July. What happens in nine hours in July is that the trams stop, the lifts stop, six thousand people walk home, the coke exchange loses about nine hundred sovereigns and the whole of the Rise stands in the dark for one evening.\"\n\n\"And people on the outfall gangs drown when the sumps back up.\"\n\nVane paused.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Between four and eleven, on my figures, and I will not pretend to you that my figures are good, because nobody has ever measured it. Say eleven. Say twenty.\"\n\n\"You'd kill twenty people to make a point.\"\n\n\"I would kill twenty people to end a hundred and three years of it.\" Vane said it without any heat at all, and that was what made it terrible. \"And I want you to notice that you have not asked me the actual question, which is: how many has the amendment killed? Fourteen thousand two hundred and six children pushed down the strata. Ada Marchbank in a pipe-fitter's yard off the Grieves Steps who thinks she is stupid. Corrin Sallow on the water gangs with a wet cough at fifty-three who was assessed third stratum in a year when six hundred and eighty children sat. Your Ketu on her knees on a staircase on Aster Row for thirty years.\" He turned his glass. \"Nobody counts those, because they are not deaths, they are just lives that were made smaller by four lines and a stamp. Twenty against fourteen thousand. I have done that sum and I have done it every week for nineteen years and it comes out the same way every time and I would be delighted to be shown that it does not.\"\n\nMarrow sat in the warm room and did not say anything for a long moment.\n\nThen he said: \"You'd need it to be a fault they can't fix.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not damage. Damage they can fix, damage is just a part and a shift and they've got four thousand spares at Aster Row.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It'd have to be something where the machine's still whole and still running and it's *wrong*, and they can't find why.\" Marrow was talking to himself now and he heard himself doing it and did not stop. \"Because that's the only kind of fault that makes them look like what they are. If you break something they look competent, they turn up with a crane and they mend it and everybody claps. If you put it *out of true* they've got to reason about it, and reasoning about it is the thing they can't do.\"\n\nVane did not move at all.\n\n\"Go on,\" he said.\n\nAnd Marrow, sitting in a chair in a room over a public house at nineteen minutes past six on a Tuesday evening in June, said:\n\n\"The eastern bank went out of true the year before I was born because a reckoner set a governor wrong on the fourth gallery. A twelfth out. Nine hours, eleven of them on the gallery, couldn't find it. So you don't need to break anything. You need to set one governor about a twelfth out somewhere they don't look, and you need to do it on the *feed side*, not the load side, because a load-side fault shows up on the dials in the house and a feed-side fault shows up as everything being slightly wrong everywhere, and they'll spend the first four hours checking the dials.\" He was leaning forward. \"And you'd want it on a bank that's not on the plans. Bramwell says half of it isn't on the plans. If it's not on the plan they can't even get to it in a drawing, they'd have to walk it, and walking it is a day and a half.\"\n\nSilence.\n\nMarrow heard the silence about a second and a half after he stopped talking.\n\nHe sat back very slowly.\n\n\"That was the question,\" he said.\n\n\"That was the question.\"\n\n\"You already knew the answer.\"\n\n\"I knew a version of it. I have had nine men look at this in eleven years and the best of them got as far as *put a governor out* and could not tell me which side or which bank or why.\" Vane picked up his glass. \"You did the whole of it in four minutes and you did it because I asked you nicely and you enjoy being good at something. That is not a criticism, son. That is the most human thing about you.\"\n\nMarrow stood up so fast the chair went over.\n\n\"Take it back,\" he said.\n\n\"I can't take it back. You said it out loud in a room.\"\n\n\"Then don't use it—\"\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"*Don't use it.*\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, Marrow, and listen, because there are two ways this goes and only one of them has you in the room.\" Vane had not raised his voice once. \"I am going to do this in the autumn. It was going to be November and it is going to be September now, because of what you have just told me. That is settled and it was settled before you came up those stairs.\n\n\"What is not settled is whether the man who understands the fault is in the gallery when it happens, or four streets away at a bench.\n\n\"Because a governor set a twelfth out by somebody who knows what he is doing is nine hours and twenty dead on the outfalls. And a governor set a twelfth out by Scarrow with a bar, because I sent Scarrow, because you would not come, is God knows what. Might be nothing. Might be a bank shearing and a hundred and eleven men on a floor.\"\n\nHe let that sit.\n\n\"So,\" said Vane. \"That is the shape of it, and I want you to know I am doing to you exactly what was done to me, and that I am aware of it, and that it works.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Bramwell**\nHe knew within the week, because the boy stopped talking.\n\nBramwell had spent fifty years reading machines and about the same reading the eleven or twelve people he had let into the shop, and it was the same skill and he had never once bothered to hide that he thought so. A machine tells you it is wrong before it fails. It tells you in the noise, in the swarf, in a bearing running four degrees warm. You do not need to know what is wrong. You need to notice that the noise has changed.\n\nThe noise changed on the Thursday.\n\nMarrow came in at fifth bell and worked ten hours on a compressor governor and did it beautifully and did not say one unnecessary word, and at the end of it he cleaned down and went upstairs, and Bramwell sat on his stool in the dark with the lamp off and thought: right.\n\nHe gave it four days, because a thing that is going wrong sometimes comes right on its own and a man who leaps in on the first day is a man who never finds out what was actually happening.\n\nOn the Monday he said, \"You've gone quiet.\"\n\n\"I'm working.\"\n\n\"You've been working seven months and you've never once been quiet, you talk like a burst main, it's the single most tiring thing about you.\" Bramwell put down the file. \"Out with it.\"\n\n\"It's nothing.\"\n\n\"Son.\" Bramwell took the goggles off, which he almost never did. \"I'm seventy-one. I've got a chest like a wet sack and I'll not see eighty. There's about four things left in this world I'm any use for and one of them is this, so don't waste it.\"\n\nMarrow stood at the bench with his back to him.\n\n\"If I told you,\" he said, \"you'd have to do something about it.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm not telling you.\"\n\n\"That's not a reason, that's a *courtesy*.\" Bramwell got off the stool. \"And I'll tell you what a courtesy is, it's what people do to each other when they've already decided how it ends. You've decided. You're stood at my bench being polite to me because you've worked out what happens next and you've put me on the outside of it.\"\n\nMarrow did not turn round.\n\n\"He's asked you for something,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"He's already got it.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Bramwell breathed out slowly. \"Then that's a different conversation and it's worse. What did you give him?\"\n\n\"An answer.\"\n\n\"To what?\"\n\nAnd Marrow told him.\n\nHe told him all of it, in about four minutes, standing at the bench with a governor in bits in front of him, and Bramwell listened to the whole of it without once interrupting, which he had not done in the entire seven months.\n\nAt the end Bramwell sat down on the crate by the stove and put both hands on his knees.\n\n\"The feed side,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"On a bank that's not on the plan.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because I told you it wasn't on the plan.\" Bramwell's voice was perfectly steady. \"In January. Sat right there, holding a lamp for me while I took a collar off, and I told you about the eastern feed going down and the eleven of them on the gallery, and I told you about the plans, and I have been carrying that story round for thirty-one years thinking it was a story about how nobody knows anything.\" He looked up. \"And you took it and made it a method.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, you don't, because you're seventeen, so I'll say the whole of it.\" Bramwell leaned forward. \"It's my fault. That's the first thing. I put that in your head and I did it because it made me feel clever at ten o'clock at night with a lamp in my hand, and I've done that to about nine people in fifty years and this is the first time it's come back. So don't stand there being noble at me. We're both in it.\"\n\nMarrow turned round for the first time.\n\n\"Second thing,\" said Bramwell. \"You've not done anything yet.\"\n\n\"I've told him how.\"\n\n\"You've told him a fact about a machine. That's not a crime and it's not a sin and if it were, every man who ever taught anybody anything would be in the works at Grieves.\" Bramwell coughed, and it went on a while, and he waved a hand at it. \"It's a bad thing you've done and it's not the thing you think it is. You've not made him able to do it. He was going to do it in November with Scarrow and a bar and it was going to be worse. What you've done is make it *work*, and made yourself necessary, and those are two different sins and only one of them is fixable.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Necessary,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the one you can undo. And there's precisely one way and you're not going to like it.\"\n\n\"Tell him no.\"\n\n\"No. You've tried that and he's got you by the fact that Scarrow with a bar is worse, and he's right, and that's a genuinely good hook and I'd not get off it either.\" Bramwell hauled himself upright. \"You make yourself *unnecessary*. You give the fault to somebody who can stop it.\"\n\nMarrow stared at him.\n\n\"That's grassing.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"On Vane. In the Under.\" Marrow's voice cracked on it. \"Bramwell, you know what happens. Not to me, I don't care about me, to Ketu. She's sixty-eight and she's in a house at the end of a lane with one door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"So what, I take her out? Where? Up the Rise? She hasn't got a writ and neither have I. Sister Maude's? He *funds* Sister Maude's, he's been funding it eleven years, everybody knows and nobody says.\"\n\n\"I know all of it, son.\"\n\n\"Then don't tell me to do it!\"\n\n\"I'm not telling you to do it,\" said Bramwell. \"I'm telling you it's the only way, and that it costs what it costs, and that you're going to have to decide, and that whichever way you decide I'll not throw you out of this shop.\" He picked up the file again. \"That's what I'm for. I'm not for telling you which. I've had seventy-one years and I've made about four decisions of that size and I got two of them wrong, and one of the two was in 1867 and there's a man in the ground because of it.\"\n\nHe put the goggles back on.\n\n\"Now go and finish that governor,\" he said. \"Because whatever happens in September, that dray goes out on Friday and there's a family in Sootgate that eats on what that dray earns, and it is possible to hold a very large thing and a very small thing in your head at the same time. It's most of what the work is.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Corvain**\nAshcombe read the eleven pages for the ninth time and then put them face down on the desk and said:\n\n\"Right. Here's where we are, and I want you to hear all of it before you say anything, because you're going to want to interrupt at about the third sentence.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Vale sealed the volumes on the Friday, exactly as he said he would, and he did it lawfully and there's no fault in it. Your annexe goes into his case and his case goes to the Council in September and the finding will be more constables in Sootgate. That's fixed. That's not the interesting part.\"\n\nHe turned the pages over again.\n\n\"This,\" he said, \"is not in his case. This is eleven pages built entirely out of published returns, none of it privileged, none of it seizable, and the arithmetic is sound. I've had it checked.\"\n\n\"By whom?\"\n\n\"That's the third sentence.\" Ashcombe met her eye. \"I sent it to Professor Linnea Thorncastle at the university, under my own name, on the fourteenth of April, and I asked her one question, which was: is the arithmetic sound. And she wrote back in nine days and said yes, and then she wrote back again a fortnight later, unasked, with four more pages.\"\n\nCorvain sat down without being told to.\n\n\"Sir, you can't—\"\n\n\"I've done it, so we're past can't.\" He pushed a folder across. \"And before you tell me I've exposed you, understand what I did and why. Your eleven pages prove the shape is wrong. They don't prove anybody meant it. A Council lawyer would stand up and say: districts differ, cohorts vary, the Under is poorly taught, this young woman has found a sad fact about poverty and called it a conspiracy. And they'd win, because there's no signature on anything.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why I went after the delegation.\"\n\n\"Which you've found. Office of the Chief Engineer.\" Ashcombe nodded slowly. \"And that's the single most dangerous piece of paper anybody in this building has ever put in front of me, and we'll come to it. But first, read what Thorncastle sent.\"\n\nCorvain opened the folder.\n\nIt was four pages in a hard, impatient hand, and the first line was:\n\n*Captain. Your officer is right and she has been modest, which is a fault she should be encouraged out of.*\n\nShe read it twice.\n\nThorncastle had taken the eight districts with the dent and she had done something Corvain had not thought to do, because Corvain was an investigator and not a mathematician: she had worked out how *large* the missing group was, and then she had worked out what it implied about the population of Gifford.\n\n*The deficit in the upper strata across the eight districts, over the thirty years available in the reading room, is 4,111 children. Your officer states this figure and stops. I will not stop.*\n\n*The proportion of a cohort that reaches the second stratum in a district with no deficit is stable to within a few per cent across a century. Apply that proportion to the eight districts and the shortfall is not a shortfall of promotion. It is a shortfall of people. Gifford has, on its own published figures, mislaid something in the order of four thousand reckoners in thirty years, and if the full run behaves as the last thirty do, something in the order of fourteen thousand in a century.*\n\n*I want to be precise about what that means, because it is not a moral point. Four hundred and eleven persons hold writs to work inside the Engine houses. The city has, in one century, identified and then discarded thirty-four times that number.*\n\n*I have been employed by this university for thirty-one years to produce reckoners. I have produced, at a generous estimate, eleven who were any good. It has never once occurred to me to ask where the others went, and I find that I am not able to write down how that feels.*\n\n*The arithmetic is sound. Tell your officer so. Then tell her that the arithmetic is the least of it, and that the interesting question is not why they did it but why nobody has ever added it up, and that I am fifty-nine years old and I am one of the people who did not.*\n\n*L. Thorncastle*\n\nCorvain put the pages down.\n\n\"Sir,\" she said. \"Why did you send it to her?\"\n\n\"Two reasons and only one of them's respectable.\" Ashcombe sat back. \"The respectable one is that I needed it checked by somebody whose name would stop a Council lawyer, and there are four such people in Gifford and three of them are on the Council.\"\n\n\"And the other?\"\n\n\"The other is that Linnea Thorncastle has taught the first stratum scholar every year for thirty-one years,\" said Ashcombe, \"and I have known her since we were both twenty-two, and I wanted to find out whether she was in it.\"\n\n\"And is she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He said it flatly. \"And I know that because of what she wrote in the last paragraph, and because nobody who was in it would have written that paragraph, and because I'd have been able to tell in the first line and so would you.\" He leaned forward. \"Which brings us to the Chief Engineer, and now I'm going to tell you what I've decided, and then you're going to tell me what you think of me.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"I'm not taking it up,\" said Ashcombe.\n\nCorvain did not move.\n\n\"Not because I'm frightened, though I am, and you should be too and I'd worry about you if you weren't. Because it won't work.\" He counted it on his fingers, the way he had done in the office in April. \"You have a printed audit sheet saying the amendment authority sits with the Engine houses. Fine. That authority is lawful. There is no offence. If I take that to the Council in September, what happens is a review, and the review reports in eleven months that the amendment process is operating as designed, which is *true*, and the delegation is quietly moved to a committee, and the audit sheet stops being printed, and you and I are in Sootgate counting lamp oil for the rest of our careers, and there is no longer any document in Gifford that connects the machine to the examination. That's the outcome. That's not pessimism, I've watched it happen twice.\"\n\n\"Then what?\"\n\n\"Then we go at the only thing they can't review away.\" Ashcombe stood up and went to the window. \"Corvain, you've been going at this like a case for eight months and it isn't a case, and I've let you, because you needed to find that out by doing it. There's no dock big enough, Vale was right. But there's a thing Vale hasn't got and it's the thing I've been sitting on since April.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The reason.\" He turned round. \"You've got the shape and the mechanism and the arithmetic, and you have not got the reason, and neither have I, and neither did Halloran, and Halloran was offered it and took it and went quiet for fifteen years. Voss told you that in January.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So the reason is a thing that a good man, given it, accepts.\" Ashcombe put both hands on the back of his chair. \"Which means it is either a very good reason or a very good lie, and there is exactly one way to find out which, and it is not in a volume and it is not on an audit sheet. Somebody has to be told it. To their face. By whoever has been doing the telling for a hundred years.\"\n\nCorvain went cold.\n\n\"You want me to make them offer me the chair.\"\n\n\"I want you to *be offered it*, and I want you to be sat in that room with your eyes open, knowing in advance that it is coming, having decided in advance what you will do with a good reason.\" Ashcombe's voice did not change. \"Because that is the only part of this that they cannot review, seal, annexe or lose. The reason has never been written down. It only exists in a room, said out loud, by one tired person to one young one, about once a generation. That's why it's held for a hundred and three years. And it means the only way to get it into the world is for somebody to be in the room and come back out again still meaning to say it.\"\n\n\"Eleven people have been in that room, sir. All eleven stopped.\"\n\n\"Twelve,\" said Ashcombe.\n\nCorvain looked up.\n\n\"I told you in April I'd like the chance to decide what I am before somebody decided it for me.\" He said it without any drama whatsoever. \"I was twenty-nine. It was the seventy-ninth year. And I have thought about it every week for twenty-six years and I have never told another living soul, and the reason I sent your pages to Linnea Thorncastle is that she is the only other person alive who was in a room like that in the same decade, and I needed to know whether she had gone the same way as me.\"\n\n\"And had she?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ashcombe. \"She was never offered it. That's the whole of the difference between us. She was too useful where she was.\"\n\nHe sat down heavily, and for the first time in eight months Corvain saw him look every one of his fifty-five years.\n\n\"So no,\" he said. \"I'm not taking it up. I'm going to write to the Chancellor's office and request that Officer E. Corvain be permitted to present her findings in person, and I am going to sign it, and it will be granted inside a fortnight because they will want to know exactly how much you have and they cannot find that out any other way. And then you will be in the room, and I will be outside it, and I will not be able to help you at all.\"\n\n\"Sir.\" Corvain's voice was not entirely steady. \"What did they tell you? In the seventy-ninth year?\"\n\nAshcombe looked at her for a long moment.\n\n\"If I tell you,\" he said, \"then you go into that room already knowing, and you will be able to sit there and be clever and unmoved, and you will come out with nothing, because they will see it in the first minute and they will talk about the weather.\" He shook his head. \"You have to hear it cold. It only works once. I'm sorry.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Rooke**\nShe worked it out on the ninth of July in the fourth-year seminar, from a timetable.\n\nThat was the humiliating part and she never entirely got over it. She had spent nine weeks since Cull Alley chewing on what Whistle had told her, *you are the appointment*, and she had gone at it the way she went at everything, which was to look for the mechanism, and she had got nowhere, and then it fell out of a piece of administration.\n\nThe fourth-year seminar met on Thursdays. In the ninth week of Trinity the Reader announced that the seminar would move to Tuesdays from Michaelmas, and there was a general groan, and Ivy, who was not in the seminar and had come to meet her afterwards, said, \"Why?\"\n\n\"Fourth-years go to Aster Row on Thursdays in their last year,\" said one of the others. \"Attachment. It's on the schedule.\"\n\nAnd Rooke said, \"What attachment?\"\n\nNine faces turned to look at her, and there was a particular quality to the silence that she had learned to recognise in eleven months, which was the silence of a room realising that the girl from Ferrow Lane has not been told something everybody else has known since they were six.\n\n\"The Engine houses,\" said Antony Vex.\n\nHe said it flatly, without cruelty. They had not become friends. They had become something else which Rooke could not name and which involved him telling her true things in front of other people about twice a term.\n\n\"Fourth-year attachment. Four days a week for a year, then the writ, then you're in.\" He was watching her. \"That's what this is. That's what all of it is. You did know that.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Rooke, it's the *course*.\"\n\n\"I know it's the course. I know where the fourth years go.\" She heard her own voice and did not like it. \"I thought it was one route. I thought there was, I don't know, teaching, and the instrument trade, and the university, and the Engine houses were the one everybody wanted—\"\n\n\"There's no other route,\" said Vex.\n\n\"There has to be.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nAnd Rooke stood in the seminar room in July and could not answer that, because there did not have to be, and she had assumed there was, and she had assumed it for eleven months without ever once checking, and she was the girl who had checked a footnote five times before contradicting a dead man.\n\nShe went to the library. It took her forty minutes.\n\nA hundred and three first stratum scholars. Ninety-seven of them at Aster Row, four dead, two abroad. Whistle had told her that in April and she had heard it as a statistic about other people.\n\nAnd underneath it, in the university calendar, printed every year, available to anybody:\n\n*The first stratum scholarship is founded upon the endowment of the Engine Houses and is held subject to the conditions thereof.*\n\nFounded upon the endowment.\n\n*Subject to the conditions thereof.*\n\nRooke sat in the second gallery with the calendar open on her knees and understood, with a completeness that made her feel physically ill, that she had not been rescued.\n\nShe had been *bought*.\n\nThe carriage on Ferrow Lane, the twenty minutes to pack, Pip in the doorway, Mother Halloway's hand shaking on her shoulder, Tessa on the step in the cold saying *take twenty back*, all of it, the whole of the worst day of her life, had been a procurement. Somebody at Aster Row had a chair to fill, and once a year the city ran an examination to find them somebody who could actually do the work, and this year it had been her, and the *reason* she had been chased down and made to sit again was not that Elspeth Corvain had watched a girl make herself smaller and found it intolerable.\n\nIt was that the chair was empty and there was nobody else.\n\nShe sat with that for a long time.\n\nAnd then she thought the thing that turned her, and it did not arrive as anger; it arrived as arithmetic, cold and clean, the way it always did.\n\n*One a year. For a hundred and three years. And they need her, or they would not come and get her.*\n\n*Which means the appointment is the only thing in this entire city that the Wrought cannot do without and cannot make.*\n\n*Which means every single one of the hundred and three had something, for about a year, that nobody has ever used.*\n\nRooke got up and went to find Ivy.\n\n\"I need everything you can get me,\" she said, \"on what the university's writs can and can't do. Not instruction. Not enquiry. The other schedule, the one we didn't read, the one about the Engine houses' endowment.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I've been trying to sell them my time,\" said Rooke, \"and I've had it the wrong way up for eleven months. They're not doing me a favour. They've got a chair they can't fill and I'm the only person in Gifford who can fill it, and I have spent a *year* asking that building for sixty marks like a girl fixing a pump.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Scarrow's Test**\nThey did it in the cellar under the Ashlight on a Tuesday in August, with sawdust down and eleven men round the walls, and Marrow understood as he came down the steps that he had made his decision four days ago without noticing.\n\nHe had gone to Bramwell. He had asked what unnecessary looked like. Bramwell had told him the price. Marrow had spent four days looking at the price and had not paid it, and had not refused to pay it either, and had come to this cellar instead, which was itself an answer.\n\nVane was at the back with a glass, sitting, not participating.\n\nScarrow was in the middle of the floor.\n\nHe was Sirrath, thirty-five, dark brindled, and enormous in a way that was not about height; he was Marrow's height and about half again his width. One ear gone entirely. Scarring across the throat that had changed his voice years ago into something that came out flat and wrong and made everything he said sound like a statement of fact.\n\n\"Nothing personal,\" said Scarrow.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"He does this.\" Scarrow rolled his shoulders. \"It's not a fight. It's a look at you. Don't try and win, you'll not win, just don't go down soft. Nine of them went down soft and he sent them home.\"\n\n\"That's decent of you.\"\n\n\"It's not decent, it's that I've done nine of these and the soft ones take longer.\" Scarrow put his hands up. \"Right.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow lost the first ninety seconds badly and he lost them because he tried to fight.\n\nHe was seventeen and six foot four and he had never in his life been in a proper fight, because he was six foot four and a Sirrath, and the whole of his experience of violence was that people did not start them with him. He went in with his weight forward and his hands up like something out of a picture, and Scarrow took him apart with a kind of bored efficiency: two into the body, one into the side of the knee that was not hard but which arrived in the exact place where the ankle he had wrecked in the outfall lane in December had rebuilt itself slightly wrong, and Marrow went down on the sawdust and could not get air.\n\nHe got up. Scarrow put him down again.\n\nThe third time he got up Scarrow said, not unkindly, \"Stay there.\"\n\nAnd Marrow, on his knees in the sawdust with blood coming out of his nose and one eye already going, thought:\n\n*Stop fighting. You cannot fight. You have never been able to fight and you are not going to learn it in a cellar.*\n\n*Look at it.*\n\n*It's a mechanism.*\n\n---\n\nHe watched Scarrow for the next forty seconds and did nothing at all except get hit, which cost him a rib.\n\nBut he watched.\n\nAnd what he saw was this: Scarrow had done nine of these, and Scarrow was thirty-five and had a throat that had been opened once, and Scarrow's whole method was a repeating cycle of four movements. Left into the body, right into the body, step, then the heavy one. Left, right, step, heavy. He had done it eleven times in two minutes and he had done it in the same order every single time, because he had done it nine hundred times in nineteen years and it worked, and nobody had ever made him change it.\n\n*Nine hundred governors,* Bramwell had said. *That's the only thing I've got that you haven't.*\n\n*And the trouble with nine hundred of anything,* thought Marrow, on his knees, *is that you stop measuring.*\n\nThe step was the fault.\n\nNot the punches. The step. Because on the step Scarrow's weight went across onto the left foot for about a third of a second before the heavy one came over, and for that third of a second every ounce of him was standing on one point, and it was a point about eleven inches inside his own centre, and he had been getting away with it for nineteen years because everybody he had ever fought had been busy being hit.\n\nMarrow did not have to hit him. He had learned about levers off a compressor governor in January and he had cut a cam profile in bronze in eleven days and he knew, with total precision, what happens to a loaded structure when you take away the point it is standing on.\n\n*Left. Right. Step.*\n\nHe went low and took the ankle.\n\nIt was not a fighting move at all. There was nothing in it of technique. It was a seventeen year old Sirrath who weighed fourteen stone putting the whole of that fourteen stone through one point on the outside of a man's left ankle at the exact third of a second when that ankle was carrying everything.\n\nSomething in it went with a sound that Marrow heard through his hands.\n\nScarrow came down like a wall.\n\n---\n\nThe cellar was completely silent.\n\nMarrow got up. It took him a while. He stood over Scarrow, who was not unconscious, who was in fact entirely conscious and looking up at him with an expression of pure uncomplicated astonishment, and whose left leg was at an angle that leg does not go.\n\n\"Finish it,\" said somebody by the wall.\n\nMarrow looked at the man who had said it. He did not know him. He was a man of about thirty in a good coat with a glass in his hand, and he had said it the way you would say *pass the salt*.\n\n\"Finish it,\" he said again.\n\nAnd here is what Marrow felt, standing in a cellar in August over a man with a broken leg, with a rib gone and one eye closing and sawdust in his mouth:\n\nNothing.\n\nHe felt nothing at all.\n\nHe had been waiting for it. That was the thing. Ever since March, since the wedge in the gate and the terrible discovery that a bad thing can feel like nothing, he had been carrying a promise around like a coin in a pocket: *I will stop when it stops feeling like nothing.*\n\nAnd he had just broken a man's leg with his whole body weight in front of eleven witnesses and he felt exactly as much as he had felt putting a piece of oak in a gate, which was nothing, and the nothing did not arrive as numbness. It arrived as *competence*. It felt like a good cut on a bronze blank. It felt like the beat going out of the floor at number four compressor house.\n\nHe had solved a mechanism and it had worked and there was a man on the floor.\n\nMarrow stood there and understood that the promise had been worthless, that it had always been worthless, that there was never going to come a day when it stopped feeling like nothing, because the whole architecture of him was built to find the fault and take the point away and feel good about it, and that was the thing Bramwell had trained and it was the thing Vane had bought, and it was the same thing.\n\nHe turned round and looked at Silas Vane at the back of the cellar.\n\nVane was on his feet. He was not smiling. He had the expression of a man who has been shown a piece of work.\n\n\"Well,\" said Vane.\n\n\"Get him a surgeon,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"He'll have one.\"\n\n\"Now. Not after. Now, while I'm stood here, because if you're going to have him carried up those steps and left in a room I want to be the one that says no.\" His voice came out flat and wrong and he barely recognised it. \"Ratchet. Go and get Maude.\"\n\nRatchet went, fast, and nobody stopped him.\n\n\"You've made your point,\" said Vane.\n\n\"I haven't made any point at all.\" Marrow spat sawdust and blood onto the floor. \"That's what I'm trying to tell you. There wasn't a point in it. I looked at him and he was a mechanism and I found the fault and I took the point away and I'd do it again in four seconds and it wouldn't cost me anything.\" He wiped his mouth. \"You asked me in June whether you had me. You have. That's what this was for and it's worked. I'm in.\"\n\nVane studied him.\n\n\"You do not sound like a man saying yes,\" he said.\n\n\"No,\" said Marrow. \"I sound like a man who's just found out what he is.\"\n\nHe walked to the bottom of the steps and stopped, and put his hand on the wall, because the rib had begun to arrive properly.\n\n\"September,\" he said. \"I'll do the governor. Not Scarrow, not any of them, me, because me is nine hours and twenty on the outfalls and them is a bank shearing and a hundred and eleven men on a floor, and I've run both and I'd rather carry the twenty.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And I want three things and they're not negotiable, and if you say no to any of them I'll walk up these stairs and go to the enforcement division tonight, and you know I mean it, because you've just watched me put a man down without feeling anything and you're clever enough to be frightened of exactly that.\"\n\nVane's glass stopped halfway.\n\n\"Name them.\"\n\n\"Ketu goes up the Rise in August. Not to a house, to a room, paid for a year, in a district where you've got nobody, and I'll want the address and I'll check it.\" Marrow held up a finger. \"Two: the outfall gangs come out. All of them, all eight sumps, for the whole nine hours. I don't care how you do it and I don't care what it costs you, you get eleven hundred men out of the outfalls before that governor moves, and if you can't do that then there's no operation because the whole of your arithmetic is the twenty and I've just taken the twenty off you.\"\n\n\"And the third?\"\n\nMarrow looked back over his shoulder.\n\n\"When it's done,\" he said, \"and they're stood on that gallery in front of the witnesses and they can't fix it, I put it right.\"\n\n\"You what?\"\n\n\"I fix it. Me. In front of them.\" Marrow's one open eye did not move. \"Nine hours. That's what you said. Nine hours and then the city gets its Engine back, and it doesn't get it back because it corrected itself and it doesn't get it back because four hundred and eleven of them worked it out. It gets it back because a ninth stratum Sirrath off Sootgate walks onto the fourth gallery and puts a governor back a twelfth of a turn.\"\n\nThe cellar was silent.\n\n\"That's not your point,\" said Marrow. \"That's mine. You want to prove they can't. I want to prove *we can*. And I'll do your half to get my half, and I want you to understand that I know exactly what that makes me.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Eight.*" },
  { n: 9, title: "The Ledger", text: "**I. Rooke**\n\"Ask me the question you came to ask,\" said Professor Thorncastle, \"and stop circling it, you've been in this room eleven minutes and you've asked me about the endowment schedule three times in three different ways.\"\n\nRooke put the calendar down.\n\n\"What happens if I say no?\"\n\nThorncastle's hands went still on the desk.\n\nIt was the second week of August and the room on the north side was hot and the window was open onto brown air, and the Professor of Reckoning of the University of the Engine sat behind her desk and did not answer for a very long time.\n\n\"Say the whole of it,\" she said eventually.\n\n\"There is one first stratum a year. There has been one a year for a hundred and three years, never two, never none, which means it isn't a measurement, it's an appointment.\" Rooke had rehearsed this and heard herself deliver it and did not care. \"The scholarship is founded on the endowment of the Engine houses and held subject to the conditions thereof. Ninety-seven of the hundred and three went to Aster Row. The fourth-year attachment is four days a week and then the writ and then you're in.\n\n\"So I'm not a scholar. I'm a seven-year procurement with a chair at the end of it. And I have spent eleven months in this building being grateful, and I would like to know what happens if I decline.\"\n\n\"Nothing happens,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"I mean it literally. Nothing happens, and that is the answer, and it is not a comfortable one.\" Thorncastle got up and went to the window. \"There is no penalty in the statute. There is no bond. You are not indentured, nobody has ever been indentured, and if you walked out of this building tomorrow the only thing that would happen is that your maintenance allowance would stop and you would be a fourteen year old girl on the Rise with no writ and nowhere to sleep. That is the whole of the enforcement and it has been sufficient for a hundred and three years, because it has never once been tested.\"\n\n\"Never?\"\n\n\"Never.\" Thorncastle turned round. \"Not once. A hundred and three first strata and not one has ever said no, and I have taught thirty-one of them and it has never occurred to a single one of them that no was available, and it did not occur to me either until about four minutes ago.\" She sat down on the window seat, which Rooke had never seen her do. \"Because they come up that hill at fourteen out of a house where they were hungry, and this building gives them books and heat and four meals and the first adult who has ever taken them seriously, and by the time they are old enough to ask what it is for, they want it. Every one. Including the eleven who were any good.\"\n\n\"That's the mechanism,\" said Rooke. \"That's not a trap, it's a *kindness*, and it works better than a trap because you can't be angry at it.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you know?\"\n\n\"I knew every part of it and I had never once put the parts together, which I have recently been told is the characteristic crime of my generation.\" Thorncastle's mouth went thin. \"A captain of the enforcement division sent me eleven pages in April and asked me whether the arithmetic was sound, and I checked it, and it was, and then I sat in this room for a fortnight and did the rest of the sum myself and wrote him four pages I have regretted every day since. Not because they were wrong.\"\n\n\"What was in them?\"\n\n\"That is not mine to give you and I will not, so do not press it.\" Thorncastle looked up. \"But I will tell you the one line I could not get out of my head, because you may as well have it. I have been employed here for thirty-one years to produce reckoners. I have produced eleven who were any good. And it had never once occurred to me to ask where the others went.\"\n\nRooke sat very still.\n\n\"There aren't any others,\" she said. \"That's what you mean. There's one a year and the others are in the ninth.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So you've spent thirty-one years teaching the one they sent you and the whole rest of your subject is in Sootgate with a card.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Thorncastle. \"And I would ask you to understand that I am fifty-nine and I found that out in April and I have not yet worked out what to do with it, and that a person who has just discovered they have been complicit for thirty-one years is not a reliable ally. I would rather tell you that now than have you find it out at a bad moment.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke went away and thought about it for four days.\n\nWhat she kept coming back to was not the injustice, because the injustice was enormous and old and she had known about it in some form since she was nine years old on Ferrow Lane. It was the *thinness*.\n\nA hundred and three years, and the entire enforcement was that a fourteen year old would not want to sleep outside.\n\nThat was all. There was no bond, no penalty, no statute, nothing. The Wrought of Gifford held the Engine on one thread and the thread was that they got to the clever children early enough to make them grateful.\n\nAnd Rooke had been made grateful, and it had worked, and she was clear-eyed about that. She wanted the library. She wanted the fourth-year seminar. She wanted, with an intensity that frightened her, to find out what was actually inside the Turn, and Aster Row was the only door.\n\n*So do not refuse. Refusing gets you a step on Ferrow Lane and an empty chair for one year and they fill it the next summer and nothing changes at all.*\n\n*Name terms.*\n\nShe sat in the second gallery with Ivy on the fourth day and said, \"How does a person get an audience with the Chancellor?\"\n\n\"You don't. Nobody does.\" Ivy did not look up from her volume. \"My family's been on the Rise for four generations and my aunt holds a chair and she's been in a room with Isolde Prewitt about six times in her life and four of them were at funerals.\"\n\n\"Then how does the Chancellor get an audience with me?\"\n\nIvy's head came up slowly.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"They came and got me,\" said Rooke. \"In a carriage. On the third day, with a form and two men in grey coats, twenty minutes to pack. That is not what a city does for a scholarship. That is what a city does when a chair is empty.\" She closed the calendar. \"I've spent eleven months trying to get someone up here to pay attention to me and I've been going about it like a girl fixing a pump. The way you get the Chancellor of Gifford to sit down with you is that you become a problem she has to solve before September.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"By putting it in writing,\" said Rooke, \"and sending it to the wrong people first.\"\n\n---\n\nWhat she wrote was four lines and she wrote nine drafts of it.\n\n*To the Office of the Chief Engineer, Engine Houses, Aster Row.*\n\n*I am the first stratum scholar of this year. I understand from the university calendar that my scholarship is founded upon your endowment and held subject to your conditions.*\n\n*Before I begin the fourth-year attachment I should like to know what the conditions are, since they are not printed anywhere I can find and nobody in this university appears to have read them. I should also like to know the terms on which I may decline, which I am told have never been established.*\n\n*I am fourteen. I would rather ask now than at twenty-one.*\n\n*R. (first stratum, this year)*\n\nShe sent one copy to the Chief Engineer.\n\nShe sent one copy to the Proctor of the Long Hall, formally, requesting that it be minuted, because a Contention had taught her that the Rise could not ignore a thing once it was in a minute book.\n\nAnd she sent one copy, unsigned, in a plain hand, to the enforcement division at the seam, addressed to *the officer who observed the Calibration from the gallery*, because eleven months ago a young woman in a grey coat had stood at the back of an empty hall and said *I have never in my life been so certain that I was looking at something that was not supposed to happen*, and Rooke had spent eleven months hating her for it and had recently begun to suspect that hating her had been a mistake.\n\nThe reply from the Chancellor's office came in nine days.\n\n---\n\n**II. Ketu**\nThey moved her on the fourteenth of August and she went, and she went because he asked, and she would like it recorded that she knew exactly what she was agreeing to.\n\nThat was the thing the boy did not understand and would not understand for years. He thought he was protecting her. He thought he had negotiated her out of a house with one door, in a lane where a man could send somebody, and up onto the Rise into a room paid for a year in a district where the Ashlight had nobody.\n\nHe had. That was all true.\n\nWhat he had not worked out, because he was seventeen and because he had been raised by a woman who had made a lifetime's work of not being a burden, was that a room on the Rise paid for a year by Silas Vane was not an escape. It was a *deposit*. She was collateral now. She had been collateral before in a different way, on a slate in a ledger at ten grist a week for nine years, and at least on the slate she had been paying it herself.\n\nKetu did not say any of that to him. She had thought about it for four days and decided not to, and the decision had cost her more than the move.\n\nBecause he had come to her with one eye closed and a rib strapped and his hands shaking, and he had said, \"You're going up the Rise on Thursday, it's a room, it's paid, it's Cullen Street,\" and his voice had been full of something she recognised, which was a young person who has done a terrible thing and has found one good consequence to hold up in front of it.\n\nIf she had said *this makes it worse, boy, this ties you tighter*, he would have believed her, because he was clever and it was true.\n\nAnd then he would have had nothing at all.\n\nSo Ketu packed a bag with eleven things in it, and locked a door she had opened every morning for forty-one years, and let a Sirrath boy carry her box up the Grieves Steps to the clatterlift, and said nothing.\n\n---\n\nThe room was on the fourth floor of a house on Cullen Street. It was clean. It had a window with sash panes and a range and a bed and a chair and a table, and it had a boxed-in pipe behind the panelling, which meant there was no knock in the wall, which meant that for the first night in sixty-eight years Ketu lay in a bed in Gifford and could not hear the Turn.\n\nShe did not sleep. She got up at fourth bell out of forty-one years of habit and stood at the window in the dark, and she could see the whole of the Under from up there.\n\nIt was extraordinary. She had not known. She had gone up that hill every working morning of her adult life and she had gone up it to scrub, with her eyes down, in the servants' stair, and she had never once in thirty years stood at a window on the Rise at fourth bell and looked down.\n\nThere was the fog. She had lived under it her whole life and she had never seen the top of it. It lay across the whole of the Under like something breathing, and where the vents went off it heaved, and the gaslights underneath it made it glow in patches, orange and dirty, so that the entire lower city looked like a fire seen through a blanket.\n\nAnd down there, somewhere under that, was a lane with a door she had locked.\n\nKetu stood at the window until it got light.\n\nThen she washed, and dressed, and went out, and asked the woman on the second floor where the nearest grocer was, and walked eleven minutes to it, and bought bread and tea and a quarter of dripping, and paid in coin, and came back.\n\nAnd on the way back she worked out what she was going to do, and it took her the length of Cullen Street.\n\nShe was sixty-eight. Her knees had gone. She had no writ and no trade beyond stairs and no money that was not somebody else's. She could not stop him, and she could not protect him, and she was not going to spend the year she had left in this room being kept.\n\nBut she had scrubbed the stairs of eleven houses on the Rise for thirty years, and there is nothing on this earth that hears more than a woman on her knees on a landing with a brush, whom nobody has ever bothered to learn the name of.\n\nKetu knew things. She had known things for thirty years and had never had the slightest use for any of them.\n\nShe sat down at the table in the clean room with the light coming in the sash window and began, slowly, in a hand she had taught herself at fifty because nobody had taught her at five, to write a list of what she knew about the houses on Aster Row.\n\n---\n\n**III. Marrow**\nWhistle broke a nineteen-year rule on the twenty-first of August and did it without any particular ceremony.\n\nMarrow came in to say the ceiling was still holding, which was a lie and both of them knew it, and Whistle looked at the closed eye and the strapping and said:\n\n\"Sit down. You are going to be angry with me and I would like you sitting.\"\n\n\"I'm not—\"\n\n\"In April I sold three people three answers.\" Whistle folded both hands on the counter. \"You, an officer of the enforcement division, and a first stratum scholar. Within the same week. Not one of you knew about the others, and I wrote myself a note saying I should tell you, and then I tore it up and wrote another one saying I should not, on the grounds that you would find each other eventually and that it would be worth more if it were yours.\"\n\nMarrow stared at him.\n\n\"That was four months ago,\" he said.\n\n\"It was four months ago.\"\n\n\"You've known for four months that there's—\"\n\n\"I have known for fifteen years that there was one of you at a time,\" said Whistle. \"That is the whole of my trade and I want you to understand what it has consisted of. Every four or five years somebody comes into this shop and asks a good question, and I answer it, and they go out and they do nothing, because one person with the truth in Gifford is a person with an interesting grievance.\" The great head came forward. \"Three at once has not happened before. Not in nineteen years. And I decided in April that it would be better if you found each other, and I was wrong, and I have been wrong since about June, and I am too old to sit on a thing out of vanity for a second time.\"\n\n\"What changed?\"\n\n\"You came in here on Monday and asked me what the water towers hold,\" said Whistle.\n\nMarrow went very still.\n\n\"Thirty hours,\" said Whistle. \"That is the answer and I gave it to you and I charged you nothing, and then I sat here on Monday night and asked myself why a seventeen year old Sirrath with a broken rib wanted to know the reserve capacity of the municipal water towers in August. And I could think of four reasons and three of them were nothing and the fourth one was the eastern feed.\"\n\nThe shop was quiet.\n\n\"I'm not going to tell you anything,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"I am not asking. I have never once asked a question in this shop, it is a matter of professional pride.\" Whistle reached into a pigeonhole. \"I am going to give you two things and then you may go, and what you do with them is not my business.\n\n\"The first is a name. Elspeth Corvain, enforcement division, twenty-one years old, four months in the coat when she came in here and eleven months in it now. She is the officer who had you brought in front of the strata boards in September, in the sense that she is the officer who broke the girl's concealment and started the whole of this, and she has spent a year proving what was done to you and she has got further with it than anybody in a hundred and three years, and eight days ago she was granted an audience with the Chancellor of Gifford, which is a thing that does not happen.\"\n\n\"And the second?\"\n\n\"The second is that she does not know about you.\" Whistle laid a folded slip on the counter. \"She knows there is a Sirrath boy out of Sootgate whose file she read in November. She does not know that he is seventeen and working at Bramwell's and that he understands the Engine better than four hundred and eleven men on Aster Row, and she does not know that he has been asking about water towers.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the slip and did not pick it up.\n\n\"You want me to go to a coat,\" he said.\n\n\"I want nothing. I am a broker and I have made a bad decision and I am correcting it.\" Whistle pushed the slip an inch closer. \"But I will say one thing to you as a person rather than as a trade, and then I will not mention it again.\n\n\"You are about to do something in September. I do not know what it is and I do not wish to. And whatever it is, you have arranged it so that you carry it, alone, because you have worked out that you can carry it better than anybody else available, and that is true, and it is the reason you will do it, and it is exactly what the man over the Ashlight has been counting on since the day he closed Ketu's account.\"\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"He does not recruit angry boys, Marrow. Anger burns out in two years.\" Whistle's voice was very low. \"He recruits *capable* ones, and he does it by arranging matters so that the capable thing and the terrible thing are the same act. And I have watched him do it for nineteen years and the only ones who ever got out were the ones who found somebody else to be capable with.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow took the slip.\n\nHe walked home across the seam with it in his coat and he did not open it for two days, and on the second night he sat on the end of the bed in the room over the shop with a bronze blank in his hands and thought about it properly.\n\n*Nine hours. Twenty dead on the outfalls, and I have taken the twenty off him, so it should be nobody, and it will not be nobody, because there is no operation in the history of the world that has gone the way it was drawn.*\n\n*And then I walk onto the fourth gallery in front of the witnesses and put a governor back a twelfth of a turn.*\n\n*And that is the part I want. That is the part I have wanted since I was fifteen years old with a stolen syllabus on Ketu's kitchen table, and Vane has understood that about me for four months, and he has built the whole operation so that the only way I get it is through him.*\n\nHe turned the blank over.\n\n*Unless there is another way to be in that gallery.*\n\nMarrow sat in the dark for a long time.\n\nThen he opened the slip.\n\n---\n\n**IV. The Chancellor's Ledger**\nThe Chancellor's office was on the fourth floor of the Council building and it had a window that looked out over the whole of the Rise, and the first thing Corvain noticed was that there was nothing in the room.\n\nShe had expected grandeur. There was a desk, four chairs, a wall of brass filing drawers, a green-shaded lamp and a carpet. The walls were plain. There was not one single object in that room that was there to impress anybody, and Corvain understood immediately that this was the most expensive room she had ever been in, and that the expense was the emptiness.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"Would you like tea? I have discovered that people always say no and always want it, so I have had it brought anyway.\"\n\n\"Thank you, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Isolde is fine in this room. Chancellor everywhere else.\" Prewitt poured. She was fifty-three and dressed simply and looked, Corvain thought, exactly like a woman who had been running something difficult for twelve years and had stopped noticing that this was unusual. \"Now. You have eleven pages built out of published returns, four pages from Linnea Thorncastle which she sent to Reeve Ashcombe unasked, an audit sheet establishing that the amendment authority sits with the Office of the Chief Engineer, and a hundred and three volumes of assessed results which Marcus Vale sealed into an evidence room in March and which I could have unsealed for you at any time in the last five months if you had asked me.\"\n\nCorvain put the tea down.\n\n\"You've known the whole time.\"\n\n\"I have known since November that Adelina Corvain's daughter was in the records office at Tallow Street, and I have known the extent of it since about April.\" Prewitt sat back. \"I want to establish that at the start because I am not going to spend this conversation being caught out, it is undignified and it wastes the afternoon. You have done a year of extremely good work and nobody has stopped you, and you have presumably noticed that nobody has stopped you, and you have presumably wondered why.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Say why.\"\n\n\"Because being stopped would have made me certain,\" said Corvain, \"and being left alone made me thorough, and thorough is slower.\"\n\n\"Partly.\" Prewitt inclined her head. \"Mostly it is that I have been waiting for you to get all of it, because there is no use whatever in telling this to somebody who has half. Halloran had half, in the sixty-first year, and it took him nine pages to circle it and he never wrote it down, and I have read those nine pages about forty times.\" She reached down and lifted something onto the desk. \"Now. Before I tell you, you are going to prove to me that you can read it, because I am not going to explain a hundred and three years to somebody who cannot follow the arithmetic. Ashcombe will have warned you that this was coming.\"\n\n\"He said I'd be offered a chair.\"\n\n\"He would know.\" Prewitt did not react at all. \"This is not the chair. The chair comes afterwards and you will not be surprised by it. This is the entrance examination, and it is the only one in this city that has never been amended.\"\n\nShe opened the ledger.\n\n---\n\nIt was enormous and it was old and it was not a book so much as a bound accumulation, and both facing pages were dense with figures in six different hands.\n\n\"Engine house output and establishment,\" said Prewitt. \"Annual, since the founding. It is not secret and it is not published. Nobody has ever asked for it because nobody has ever had a reason to want output and establishment on the same page.\" She turned it round to face Corvain. \"There are four columns. Output in units of delivery, which you may treat as a black box, it is the number the Chief Engineer reports. Establishment, being the number of writ-holding reckoners. Corrections logged, being faults found and rectified. And interval, being the mean time between scheduled recalibrations.\n\n\"A hundred and three rows. You may have as long as you like and you may have a pencil and paper. Tell me what has happened to this city.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the page.\n\nThen she took out her notebook and did what she had learned to do in a reading room in March, which was to stop looking at the numbers and start looking at the shape.\n\nIt took her twenty-six minutes.\n\n\"Output is flat,\" she said. \"It rises for the first nineteen years and then it is flat within about two per cent for eighty-four years. That's not a machine at capacity, that's a machine being run at a fixed setting because nobody dares change it.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Establishment is twelve for eleven years and then it climbs, and it climbs steadily, and it does not stop. Twelve, forty-one, a hundred and nine, two hundred and sixty, four hundred and eleven.\" She turned a page. \"And it is not correlated with output at all. They are doing the same amount of work with thirty-four times the people.\"\n\n\"Which means?\"\n\n\"Which means the work has got harder or the people have got worse, and the machine hasn't changed, so it's the people.\"\n\n\"And corrections?\"\n\nCorvain ran her finger down the third column and stopped.\n\n\"Corrections logged goes *up* until about the fortieth year,\" she said slowly. \"Nine, fourteen, twenty-two, thirty-one. And then it comes down. And it keeps coming down. And in the last thirty years it's...\" She checked it twice. \"It's between nought and two. It's been nought in eleven of the last thirty years.\"\n\n\"And what does a falling correction rate mean?\"\n\n\"Either the machine has stopped developing faults.\" Corvain's mouth had gone dry. \"Or they have stopped being able to find them.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"The interval.\" She went to the fourth column. \"Mean time between recalibrations. It's eleven days at the founding. It's four years now.\" She looked up. \"You can't find a fault you only look for every four years. They didn't stop finding faults because there were none. They stopped looking, because looking meant taking the thing apart, and taking the thing apart meant somebody had to be able to put it back.\"\n\nPrewitt did not move.\n\n\"Say the sentence, Officer.\"\n\nCorvain sat back in the chair in the empty room.\n\n\"The Wrought have not been able to run the Engine for about sixty years,\" she said. \"You have been operating a machine you do not understand by not touching it, and you have got away with it because it is a very good machine, and the entire establishment of four hundred and eleven exists to maintain a procedure written down by people who *did* understand it, and every year the procedure works and nobody has to find out what would happen if it stopped working.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the amendments—\" She stopped. \"Oh.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"The amendments aren't about keeping the Under down at all.\" Corvain heard her own voice go strange. \"You need one. Every year you need one person who can actually do it, because the procedure isn't enough on its own, there has to be somebody who can *reason* about the machine when something happens that isn't in the book. And you can't produce one out of your own families any more, so you go out into the city once a year and find one, and you take her.\n\n\"And the forty others who nearly could—\"\n\n\"Are the problem,\" said Prewitt.\n\n\"Are the *proof*.\" Corvain put both hands flat on the desk. \"If you promoted all forty-one, then within one generation there would be four hundred Rustbound reckoners in the Engine houses, and every one of them would be visibly better at it than every Wrought child in the building, and the claim that the true calculus runs in the blood of the Twelve would be dead inside ten years. So it isn't spite and it isn't snobbery. It's that two is a comparison.\"\n\n\"That is the whole of it,\" said Prewitt. \"You are the first person to get there in this room without being told, and it took you twenty-six minutes and a ledger, and I would like you to know that I am not being kind when I say that.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt closed the ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" she said. \"The reason. Because you have the mechanism and the motive, and you think the motive is legitimacy, and legitimacy is only the *shape* of it. Nobody maintains a system for a hundred and three years to protect their own vanity. They maintain it because they are frightened of something.\"\n\n\"Vaskeld,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Vaskeld.\" Prewitt got up and went to the window. \"Everybody in Gifford knows the number and nobody in Gifford knows the story, and I am going to tell it to you properly because it is the only argument I have got and it deserves to be made well.\n\n\"Vaskeld had an engine. Not ours; theirs was smaller and later and built by people who had read about ours. And Vaskeld had a council of about the same sort as ours, holding it on about the same sort of claim, and in the sixty-eighth year Vaskeld had a reformer.\n\n\"He was not a bad man. I want to be extremely clear about that, because it would be much more comfortable for me if he had been. His name was Aurel Sant and he was a lawyer and he was *right*. He established, publicly, with documents, that the Vaskeld council could not run their engine, that the qualification was a fiction, and that the examination by which they recruited had been managed for forty years. All of it true. All of it provable. He published in the summer.\n\n\"The council fell in nine weeks. There was no violence to speak of; they simply stopped being obeyed, which is what falling is. And a committee of the qualified took over the engine houses, and the committee was drawn from the people who could actually do the work, which was the correct principle and I have never once disputed it.\"\n\nShe turned round.\n\n\"And then in February the engine went out of true, and the committee could not fix it, and it took eleven days, and it killed forty-one thousand six hundred people.\"\n\nCorvain said nothing.\n\n\"Do you understand what I am telling you?\" said Prewitt. \"Sant was right. The reform was right. The people who took over were the best available and they were better than the ones they replaced. And the engine still went down, because knowing that a lie is a lie does not teach you how a machine works, and the forty years of managed examinations had already happened, and the people who could have fixed it in February had been sorted into the ninth stratum in the fifties and were forty-five years old and working in yards.\"\n\n\"So the crime killed them,\" said Corvain. \"Not the reform.\"\n\nPrewitt stopped.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"The forty years of amendments are the reason the committee couldn't fix it.\" Corvain's voice was perfectly level. \"You have just told me that Vaskeld fell because it had spent forty years throwing away everybody who could run its engine. And your conclusion from that is that the amendments must continue.\"\n\n\"My conclusion,\" said Prewitt, \"is that you cannot stop in the middle.\"\n\nShe came back and sat down.\n\n\"That is the whole of it, Officer, and I want you to sit with it, because it took me about four years. Yes. The amendments are the disease. I have never once pretended otherwise, not to myself and not to the eleven other people in this city who know. And a city that has been doing this for a hundred and three years is a city where the only person alive who can reason about the Engine is a fourteen year old girl who was pulled out of Ferrow Lane eleven months ago, and there is precisely one of her, because we made sure there was precisely one of her.\n\n\"So if I publish tomorrow, this council falls in nine weeks, exactly as Vaskeld's did, and it falls with my full support, and I would go quietly and I would be glad. And then the Engine goes out of true, in some February, in eleven years or forty, and there will be one person in Gifford who might be able to reason about it, and she will be a woman in her fifties by then, and if she is dead or abroad or simply not clever enough on that particular day, then this city puts forty thousand people in the ground.\n\n\"That is the reason. It is not a good reason. It is a *true* one. And every single person who has ever been in this room has taken it, and I have never once thought worse of any of them.\"\n\nThe room was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"How long?\" said Corvain.\n\n\"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"How long does it take to stop?\" Corvain leaned forward. \"That's the question and I don't think anybody has ever asked it in this room, because everybody who has ever sat in this chair has been offered a choice between *publish tomorrow* and *carry on*, and those aren't the only two things. You said you cannot stop in the middle. All right. How long is the *end*?\"\n\nPrewitt looked at her.\n\n\"Say what you mean.\"\n\n\"You need one a year because you cannot make one.\" Corvain was talking fast now. \"Fine. Then stop unmaking them. Not publish. Not reform. Stop the amendments, quietly, this year, forty-one of them, and promote the lot. That's forty-one instead of one. And do it again next year, and the year after.\"\n\n\"And in ten years there are four hundred Rustbound reckoners in the Engine houses who are all better than my grandchildren, and the claim dies, and the council falls, exactly as it did in Vaskeld, only slower.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain. \"It falls in ten years instead of nine weeks, and when it falls there are four hundred people who can run the machine instead of one. That's the difference. That's the entire difference and it is the only thing that matters in the whole story.\" She put her hand flat on the ledger. \"Vaskeld didn't die of reform. It died of reforming a city that had already thrown its reckoners away. You've got eleven years, or forty, before your February. You cannot use them to keep the lie alive, because the lie is what will kill you. You can use them to build the four hundred.\"\n\nPrewitt sat absolutely still.\n\n\"You are proposing,\" she said slowly, \"that this council should spend the next ten years deliberately manufacturing the conditions of its own destruction.\"\n\n\"I'm proposing that it should spend ten years making itself unnecessary,\" said Corvain, \"which is the only honourable thing left available to it, and you know that, because you have known for twenty-two years that it is a lie and you have spent twenty-two years keeping it warm.\"\n\n\"And you imagine the Chief Engineer will agree to this.\"\n\n\"No, ma'am. I imagine the Chief Engineer will fight it to the last breath, because the Chief Engineer holds a quarterly delegation and four hundred and eleven writs and a claim on a bloodline.\" Corvain sat back. \"But you're the Chancellor. And you asked me here to offer me a chair, and I'd like to say yes, and I'd like to say yes to *that* chair.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt got up and went to the window and stood there for a very long time.\n\n\"You are twenty-one,\" she said, without turning round.\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"When I was twenty-one I sat in this room in the chair you are sitting in, and a man called Everard Kell showed me the Vaskeld figures, and I went out onto the terrace afterwards and was sick into a flowerbed, and by the following spring I had stopped thinking about it more than once a week.\" She put her fingertips on the glass. \"Osric Kell's father. Nobody has ever known that, including his son, and I have carried it for twenty-two years.\n\n\"And I have spent twenty-two years believing there were two options, and you have been in this room for eighty minutes and produced a third, and it is not a good third, it is slow and it is dangerous and it will very probably fail. And it is the first new thing anybody has said to me about this since I was twenty-one years old.\"\n\nShe turned round.\n\n\"I am going to say no,\" said Isolde Prewitt.\n\nCorvain's stomach dropped.\n\n\"For eleven days,\" Prewitt went on, \"because that is how long it will take me to find out which of the eleven other people who know will hold, and because if I say yes to you this afternoon in the heat of having been out-thought by a girl in a grey coat, I will unsay it by Friday and you will be in Sootgate counting lamp oil. So: no, today. Come back on the second of September and I will give you an answer I can keep.\"\n\n\"Ma'am—\"\n\n\"And in the meantime you are going to do something for me, and it is not a test, and I want you to understand that I am asking rather than instructing, because you have earned that.\" Prewitt came back to the desk. \"There is a letter on my desk from the first stratum scholar. Four lines. She has written to the Chief Engineer asking what the conditions of her scholarship are and on what terms she may decline, and she has sent copies to the Proctor of the Long Hall to be minuted and to an unnamed officer of the enforcement division, and she is fourteen years old, and it is the single most dangerous piece of paper in this building.\n\n\"You know her. You are the reason she is here. Go and talk to her, and find out what she wants, and tell her the truth, all of it, including the part about there being precisely one of her, because she is going to work it out inside a month and I would rather she heard it from somebody who is on her side.\"\n\nCorvain stood up.\n\n\"Ma'am,\" she said. \"May I ask one thing?\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Ashcombe was in a room like this in the seventy-ninth year and he wouldn't tell me what he was told. He said I had to hear it cold.\" She hesitated. \"Was it you?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Prewitt. \"It was Everard Kell, and it was the same afternoon he did me, and Reeve Ashcombe was twenty-nine and he sat in that chair and said nothing at all for four minutes and then said *thank you for telling me* and went out.\" She sat down and drew a sheet toward her. \"And he has spent twenty-six years being a very good captain in a division that cannot touch it, and he has just spent the whole of that credit sending me you, and I want you to know that, because he will never tell you and it is the largest thing anybody has done in this business in my lifetime.\"\n\n---\n\nCorvain got as far as the door.\n\n\"Officer.\"\n\n\"Ma'am.\"\n\n\"The second of September.\" Prewitt did not look up. \"Between now and then, keep your evidence somewhere I cannot reach and do not tell me where. I say that as a friend and I mean it entirely, because there are eleven other people who know, and I do not yet know which of them will hold, and one of them is the Chief Engineer.\"\n\n---\n\nThe Engine went out of true at nine minutes past six on the evening of the thirty-first of August.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Nine.*" },
  { n: 10, title: "The Engine's Answer", text: "**I. Nine Minutes Past Six**\nThe thing nobody in Gifford ever understood about the thirty-first of August was that four separate plans were running that evening and not one of them was the plan that happened.\n\n**Silas Vane's plan** was nine hours in the last week of September, with the outfall gangs pulled out and eleven hundred men accounted for and a governor on the fourth gallery set a twelfth out on the feed side by a Sirrath who knew exactly what he was doing. That plan was three weeks away and it was good and it would probably have worked.\n\n**Marrow's plan** was to be in the gallery at the end of it.\n\n**Corvain's plan** was the second of September.\n\n**The Chief Engineer's plan** was the only one that moved early, and it moved because a fourteen year old had written four lines on a piece of paper and sent one copy to be minuted.\n\n---\n\nHis name was Halvard Crale and he had held the Office of the Chief Engineer for nineteen years, and he was not a wicked man and he was not clever either. He was the fourth Crale to hold it. He had come out of the university in the ordinary way, fifth stratum in his year, promoted on the strength of an uncle, and he had run the Engine houses for nineteen years exactly as they had been run for the previous eighty, which was by not touching anything and by making certain that the annual recalibration schedule slipped a little further every year, because a recalibration was the one occasion on which somebody might have to explain something.\n\nHe had received Rooke's letter on the twenty-fourth of August.\n\nHe had read it four times and had understood, correctly, that the girl had worked out that the first stratum was an appointment. He had understood, also correctly, that the letter had been minuted at the Long Hall and could not be recalled. And he had understood, entirely incorrectly, that the Chancellor's granting of an audience to an enforcement officer on the second of September and the arrival of that letter in the same fortnight were one thing rather than two.\n\nSo Halvard Crale did what frightened men in old offices have always done, which was to reach for the only instrument he was completely confident of.\n\nOn the twenty-ninth of August he ordered a full recalibration of the eastern bank.\n\nIt was, on paper, an unimpeachable act. The eastern bank was four years overdue. He minuted it as routine. And what he intended by it was to be able to stand in front of the Council on the third of September and say: *the amendment authority exists because the Engine requires unbroken competence and here is the competence, we have this month completed a full recalibration of the eastern bank on schedule.*\n\nHe gave the order to a committee of nine.\n\n---\n\nThe committee of nine began work at second bell on the thirty-first of August, on the fourth gallery, on a bank that had not been opened in four years and about a third of which was not on any plan.\n\nThey followed the procedure. The procedure was a hundred and three years old and it was excellent and it had been written by people who understood the machine, and it ran to four hundred and eleven pages, and every one of the nine had been examined on it.\n\nAt nine minutes past six one of them, a reckoner of thirty-one named Sella Vaunt who was the best of the nine and who would spend the rest of her life going over this, reached the end of section eleven of the procedure and found that section eleven referred forward to a bank of rods that was not there.\n\nNot missing. Not broken. Not there, in the sense that the procedure described eleven rods in a housing and the housing in front of her had fourteen.\n\nSella Vaunt did the right thing. She stopped, and she called the senior, and the senior looked at it for four minutes and could not account for it either, and then the senior did the wrong thing, which was to reason that three additional rods must have been fitted at some later date and were therefore not load-bearing in the original design, and to proceed with section twelve.\n\nSection twelve of the procedure required them to slacken the feed governor by a quarter turn.\n\nThey slackened it by a quarter turn.\n\nWith fourteen rods in the housing instead of eleven, a quarter turn on the feed governor was not a quarter turn on the feed. It was a twelfth of a turn out of true, on the feed side, on a bank that was not on the plans.\n\nThey had done it to themselves, following a hundred and three year old procedure, with the best of the nine standing there saying *wait*, in the exact manner that a man drowns four feet from a rope.\n\nNobody sabotaged the Engine of Gifford. That was the thing that Vane could never afterwards be told and would never have believed. The city had been holding a loaded pistol against its own head for sixty years and on the thirty-first of August it finally got round to pulling the trigger by the book.\n\n---\n\nThe knock stopped at eleven minutes past six.\n\nIt did not stop everywhere at once. That was what people remembered. It came out of the pipes in waves, district by district, and it took about ninety seconds to cross the Under, and everybody who was in the Under that evening felt it happen in their own wall before they knew what it was, because the knock is the thing you do not notice until it is gone.\n\nMarrow was at the bench.\n\nHe had a compressor governor in bits in front of him and he had been arguing with Bramwell about the ceiling joist on Cull Alley for eleven minutes, and the pipe behind the shop wall went *knock, knock, knock,* and then did not do it a fourth time.\n\nBoth of them looked up.\n\n\"That's not a vent,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey stood in the middle of the shop in the dark and listened to a hundred and three years of noise not happening.\n\nThen the gas went down. Not out; down, to about a third, all along Kettle Yard and then further, and outside somebody started shouting and then a lot of people started shouting, and Bramwell put his hand flat on the wall the way the foreman at number four compressor house had done in January.\n\n\"Aye,\" he said. \"Well.\"\n\nMarrow was already getting his coat.\n\n\"It's not September,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"I know it's not September.\"\n\n\"Then it's not him.\"\n\n\"I know it's not him.\" Marrow was at the door. \"That's what's wrong with it. If it were him I'd know which bank and which governor and how far, and I'd know the outfalls were clear, and I'd know it was nine hours.\" He looked back. \"It's not nine hours. Nobody's counting. Nobody's pulled anybody out of anything. It just went.\"\n\nBramwell stood in his dark shop and said the only thing there was to say.\n\n\"The water towers hold thirty hours,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then you've got thirty hours, son, and you've not got a writ, and there's four hundred and eleven men on that hill who'll have you in the works at Grieves if you go within a street of it.\"\n\nMarrow stopped in the doorway.\n\n\"There's a woman on Cullen Street who scrubbed those stairs for thirty years,\" he said. \"And there's a coat at the seam who's spent a year proving what they did to me. And there's a girl in that university who came first stratum out of six hundred and forty-one and is fourteen.\"\n\nHe pulled the door.\n\n\"I'm not going alone,\" he said. \"That's the whole thing I worked out in August. I'm not going alone.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Three Doors, Opening**\n**Rooke** was in the second gallery of the library when the lights went down, and she was the only person in the building who understood what she was looking at within four seconds, and she understood it because she was the only person in the building who had read the output ledger.\n\nShe had not been given the output ledger. She had asked Thorncastle for it in the second week of August, in the same conversation, and Thorncastle had looked at her for a long moment and then said *God help us all* for the second time in eleven months and sent for it.\n\nEstablishment climbing. Output flat. Corrections falling to nought. Interval stretching to four years.\n\nRooke had read all four columns in about nine minutes and had said: \"They've stopped opening it.\"\n\nAnd Thorncastle had said: \"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then the first time they open it, it'll go.\"\n\nSo when the gas went down over the whole Rise on the thirty-first of August, Rooke did not think *what has happened*. She thought, with a cold and total clarity, *they have opened the eastern bank*, and she was out of her chair and running before the second lamp had dimmed.\n\nShe got to Thorncastle's rooms at eleven minutes past six and the old woman was already standing in the middle of the floor with her coat in her hand.\n\n\"Aster Row,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n\"They won't let me in.\"\n\n\"No. They will not let you in and they will not let me in, and I have held a chair in this university for thirty-one years.\" Thorncastle was pulling the coat on. \"Which is why we are not going to Aster Row. We are going to the Council building, and we are going to find the one person in Gifford who has spent a year assembling a document that says the Engine houses cannot do this, and we are going to make her take us up that hill.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Her name is Elspeth Corvain,\" said Professor Thorncastle, \"and I owe her four pages and an apology.\"\n\n---\n\n**Corvain** was in the duty room at the seam when the knock stopped, and she was the only person in that building who did not spend the first four minutes asking what had happened, because Ashcombe took one look at the ceiling and said, \"Get your coat,\" and she got her coat.\n\n\"Sir, what—\"\n\n\"The Engine's out of true.\" He was moving fast for a man of fifty-five. \"Not stopped. Out of true. Listen to the gas.\"\n\n\"How can you tell?\"\n\n\"Because when an engine stops it stops, and that gas is running at a third and steady, which means the thing is turning and it is turning *wrong*, and I have been waiting twenty-six years for somebody to tell me it had happened.\" He was at the stairs. \"The second of September has just become tonight. Where's your evidence?\"\n\n\"In four places, sir, and one of them is a bindery on the Wick.\"\n\n\"Good girl.\"\n\nThey came out onto the step of the enforcement building at seventeen minutes past six into a city with the lights at a third, and there were two people standing at the bottom of the steps.\n\nOne was a woman of fifty-nine in an academic coat with her hair coming down.\n\nThe other was a girl of fourteen in a coat that nearly fitted her.\n\nCorvain stopped dead.\n\n\"You,\" she said.\n\n\"You,\" said Rooke.\n\nAnd then, because neither of them had anything prepared and there was no time and the whole city was going down around them, Rooke said the thing she had been carrying for eleven months and had rehearsed about four hundred times and had never once expected to get to say:\n\n\"You ruined us.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"You came down off the Rise and you were clever and you put me in a carriage and my foster mother has been nine days on a bed in a front room since last autumn and there's a nine year old on Ferrow Lane who's had two bottles of syrup in a year, and I have hated you for eleven months, properly, every day, like a job.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain. \"I know. I've read your file about nine hundred times.\"\n\n\"And I was wrong,\" said Rooke.\n\nCorvain blinked.\n\n\"I worked out in July why they came and got me. It wasn't you being certain in an empty hall. It was that there's one chair a year and it was empty and I was the only one in Gifford who could fill it.\" Rooke's voice did not shake. \"You were the *instrument*. And I've spent eleven months hating the instrument, which is a stupid way to do arithmetic, and I don't have time to do it properly now so I'm doing it badly. I don't forgive you. I've stopped billing you.\"\n\n\"That'll do,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Right,\" said Ashcombe. \"Now, if the four of us have finished, will somebody tell me why a professor and a first-year are on my steps.\"\n\n\"Because they have opened the eastern bank,\" said Thorncastle, \"and the eastern bank is a third not on the plans, and this child has read the output ledger and can tell you what is about to happen to the water in about thirty hours, and neither of us can get onto Aster Row without an officer of the Council.\"\n\n\"And I can't get onto Aster Row without a reason,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"You have got eleven pages, four pages from me, an audit sheet and a hundred and three volumes,\" said Thorncastle, \"and an engine that has been out of true for nine minutes.\"\n\n\"That's not a reason, that's a report.\"\n\n\"Then take a *warrant*,\" said a voice from the bottom of the steps.\n\nThey all turned round.\n\nThere was a Sirrath standing at the kerb in the half-dark, six foot four, white pelt, one eye still not entirely open from August, breathing like a man who had come across the seam at a run.\n\n\"Take a warrant,\" said Marrow, \"for me.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. What Marrow Brought**\nHe gave it to them on the steps in four minutes because there was no time to do it properly, and it was the worst four minutes of his life and he did it standing up.\n\n\"There's an operation,\" he said. \"Last week of September. A governor on the fourth gallery, feed side, a twelfth out, on a bank that isn't on the plans. Nine hours. I gave him the method in June because he asked me a good question and I liked being asked.\"\n\nAshcombe's face did not change at all.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Silas Vane.\"\n\n\"And you're telling me this on a step.\"\n\n\"I'm telling you this on a step because it's the thirty-first of August and it's happened three weeks early and it's *not him*.\" Marrow's voice cracked and he did not stop. \"That's the whole reason I'm here. If it were him I could tell you which bank and which governor and how far and I'd have it fixed in an hour. It isn't him. They've done it themselves. They've opened the eastern bank on a hundred and three year old procedure with fourteen rods in an eleven rod housing and they have done the exact thing I told him to do, by the book, in front of nine witnesses, and there is nobody up there who can find it.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the rods?\" said Rooke.\n\nMarrow looked at her properly for the first time.\n\n\"Because I've had my hands on the housing,\" he said. \"Bramwell cogged three rods out of an eastern bank spare in the seventies and they're hanging on a chain in his shop and I've measured them, and they're not on the plan sheet he's got a page of, and I asked him in January why there were fourteen and he said he'd never counted them.\"\n\n\"And you counted them.\"\n\n\"I count everything,\" said Marrow. \"It's all I've got.\"\n\nRooke stood in a dying street and looked up at a Sirrath she had never met.\n\n\"What's your name?\" she said.\n\n\"Marrow.\"\n\n\"Fourth stratum, amended ninth.\"\n\nHe went very still.\n\n\"How do you—\"\n\n\"Because there's forty-one of you and I've had your files since April, and you were the top of the forty-one, and I've read your paper.\" Rooke's chin came up. \"You got the load across the arch. Four hundred and eleven human candidates in the main hall didn't get the load across the arch and I've been in the fourth-year seminar for a year with nine of them.\"\n\n\"I got it wrong first.\"\n\n\"Everyone gets it wrong first. You marked your own false start and went back. That's the whole of it, that's the only thing that matters, that's the—\" She stopped. \"There isn't time. Captain, take the warrant.\"\n\n\"On what charge?\" said Ashcombe.\n\n\"On his own information,\" said Corvain. \"Sir, he's just confessed to conspiracy against the Engine in front of four witnesses on a public step. That's a warrant. That's a *good* warrant, it's the best warrant this division has had in eleven years, and it takes us onto Aster Row tonight with an officer, a suspect, a material witness and an expert, and there is not one man in that building who can refuse it.\"\n\n\"And when we get up there,\" said Ashcombe, \"you are walking a Sirrath with a confession into a room full of people who would very much like there to be a saboteur.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"They will hang him for this.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Marrow.\n\nEverybody looked at him.\n\n\"I've done the sum,\" he said. \"I did it in August on the end of a bed with a bronze blank in my hands and I've done it about nine hundred times since. If I stay in Kettle Yard tonight nobody ever knows and I keep my hands and in three weeks I put a governor out for a man I'm frightened of, and twenty people drown in the sumps, and I fix it afterwards in front of witnesses and that's the thing I've wanted since I was fifteen.\" He wiped his mouth. \"And if I get in that carriage I get the same gallery and the same governor and the same twelfth of a turn, tonight, for nothing, for a city that gave me a nine, and then they hang me for a conspiracy that never happened.\n\n\"And it's the same gallery. That's what I worked out. It is the exact same gallery either way. The only difference is who's in the water.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. The Fourth Gallery**\nThey went up on the works lift at Aster Row at nine minutes to eight and it took eleven minutes and nobody spoke.\n\nCorvain had the warrant. Ashcombe had his rank and twenty-six years and a face that had stopped four arguments at the gate. Thorncastle had a chair in the university and an expression that had stopped the fifth. Rooke had nothing at all and came in behind Thorncastle's elbow, and nobody looked at her twice, because she was fourteen and small and there were four hundred and eleven men on that hill with something considerably more urgent to think about.\n\nMarrow came in under warrant, between two constables, with his hands loose in front of him.\n\nThat was the arrangement Ashcombe had made at the gate in about forty seconds, and it was the single cleverest thing Corvain ever saw him do, and it worked entirely on one principle: a man in custody is allowed onto a site where an expert is not.\n\n---\n\nThe fourth gallery was two hundred feet long and it hung in the dark over a drop nobody had ever measured, and the whole of the eastern bank stood behind it: eight thousand square feet of brass and blackened iron, rod banks stacked to a vanishing point overhead, and the great cogs turning somewhere below at the speed of an hour hand, and the sound of it in the chest rather than in the ears.\n\nIt was turning. That was the thing that emptied Rooke's lungs when she came out onto the walkway. It had not stopped. It was running, enormous and patient, and it was running *wrong*, and you could feel that too, a kind of stagger in the floor about once every four seconds, like a man walking with one leg shorter than the other.\n\nThere were about sixty people on the gallery.\n\nThe Chief Engineer, Halvard Crale, was at the control stand with eleven of them round him. The committee of nine were in a knot further along, and one of them, a woman of thirty-one, was standing slightly apart with her arms wrapped round herself, looking at a housing.\n\nAnd there were witnesses, which was the part nobody had planned and which changed everything: four Council members, two Council clerks with notebooks, and a reporter from the *Gifford Intelligencer* who had been at Aster Row that afternoon for a piece about the endowment and had not been asked to leave because in a hundred and three years nobody had ever needed a procedure for asking a reporter to leave the fourth gallery.\n\n\"Two hours,\" said Ashcombe quietly. \"They've had two hours.\"\n\n\"They've had a hundred and three years,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n---\n\nWhat happened in the next forty minutes was the single most humiliating episode in the history of the Wrought and it was witnessed and minuted and printed, and none of it was cruel and all of it was worse for that.\n\nCrale had them on the dials. That was where he had put them at half past six and it was where they still were at twenty past eight, because the dials were in the book and the book was what he had. Nine reckoners walking the dial banks in pairs, reading and calling and writing, four hundred and eleven pages of procedure open on the stand.\n\n\"He's checking the load side,\" said Marrow, from between his constables.\n\n\"Say it louder,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"He's checking the *load side*.\" Marrow's voice went down the gallery. \"It's not on the load side. If it were on the load side the dials would say so and they've read them twice. It's on the feed.\"\n\nSixty people turned round.\n\nHalvard Crale looked along the gallery at a Sirrath in the custody of two constables and said, \"Who let that in here?\"\n\n\"Officer Corvain, enforcement division, on warrant.\" Corvain had her card out. \"That's a material witness in a live conspiracy and he stays where he is.\"\n\n\"Take him out.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"I am the Chief Engineer of this—\"\n\n\"And I'm an officer of the Council executing a warrant on Council premises,\" said Corvain, \"and you can have this argument with me at length tomorrow morning and I'll lose it, and tonight he stays where he is.\"\n\n\"The feed side,\" said Sella Vaunt.\n\nEverybody turned again.\n\nShe was the woman of thirty-one who had been standing apart. She had a procedure book open in both hands and her face had gone the colour of paper.\n\n\"Section eleven,\" she said. \"Sir. I said at nine minutes past six that section eleven referred to eleven rods and there were fourteen in the housing, and I said we should stop, and it's in the log, and then we did section twelve, which is a quarter turn on the *feed governor*.\" She was shaking. \"If the housing is wrong then the quarter turn wasn't a quarter turn.\"\n\nThe gallery was completely silent except for the machine.\n\n\"Fourteen rods,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Housing nine or housing eleven?\"\n\n\"Nine.\"\n\n\"Then you're a twelfth out,\" said Marrow, \"not a quarter, because three extra rods in housing nine is a three to one on the linkage and a quarter of a turn through a three to one on the feed side is a twelfth on the bank. And it'll be reading true on every dial in this house, because the dials are downstream of housing nine and the whole bank went out together, so everything's agreeing with everything and all of it's wrong.\"\n\nSella Vaunt put the book down on the rail and put both hands over her face.\n\n\"Two hours,\" she said. \"I've been saying housing nine for two hours.\"\n\n\"You said the housing,\" said Marrow. \"You didn't say the linkage. Nobody'd say the linkage unless they'd had one in bits. It's not in the book.\"\n\n\"Have you had one in bits?\"\n\n\"I've had three rods off one on a chain in a shop in Kettle Yard for seven months,\" said Marrow. \"And a pressure governor off a dray about nine hundred times.\"\n\n---\n\nHalvard Crale did the thing that ended him, and he did it out of nineteen years of habit, and it took four seconds.\n\n\"This is a Sirrath in custody on a sabotage warrant,\" he said, \"who has just demonstrated a detailed knowledge of the eastern bank linkage, and I want him removed and I want the gallery cleared of enforcement personnel, and I will not have the Engine of Gifford diagnosed by a *criminal* in front of the press.\"\n\nAnd the reporter from the *Gifford Intelligencer* wrote that down.\n\nAshcombe stepped forward.\n\n\"Chief Engineer,\" he said. \"How long have you got?\"\n\n\"I beg your pardon?\"\n\n\"The water towers hold thirty hours. It is now twenty-six minutes past eight and the Engine has been out of true since nine minutes past six, which is two hours and seventeen minutes, so you have twenty-seven hours and forty-three minutes.\" Ashcombe's voice was completely level and it carried the length of the gallery. \"You have used two hours and seventeen minutes checking the dials. There are nine of you and four hundred and eleven pages, and the woman who found it at nine minutes past six has been telling you for two hours and seventeen minutes and you have not heard her.\n\n\"So I am going to ask you a question in front of four Council members, two clerks and a newspaper, and I would like you to answer it, because it is going to be printed either way.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\nHalvard Crale stood at the control stand of the Engine of Gifford with eleven of his own people around him.\n\n\"We are proceeding in accordance with the procedure,\" he said.\n\n\"That isn't the question.\"\n\n\"The procedure is—\"\n\n\"Chief Engineer,\" said one of the Council members, quietly, from the rail. \"Answer the officer.\"\n\nAnd Halvard Crale, fourth of his name to hold the office, fifth stratum in his year, promoted on the strength of an uncle, said:\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. The Engine's Answer**\nIt took the four of them eleven minutes to agree the fix and forty minutes to do it, and it should be recorded that all four of them were necessary and that afterwards none of them could ever separate out whose part had been whose.\n\n**Marrow** had the linkage. He got the housing open and he was inside it to the shoulder within four minutes and he found the three extra rods by hand in the dark, and he could say what they were: not additions, originals, cast in the founding decade with the same marks, put in by somebody who had wanted a three to one where the plan said one to one.\n\n**Rooke** had the number. That was the part nobody else in the building could have done and there were sixty witnesses to her doing it. Because a twelfth out was what Marrow could say from the linkage, and a twelfth out was not enough; the bank had been running out of true for two hours and twenty minutes and a bank running out of true does not simply sit there being wrong, it *drifts*, and the correction required was not a twelfth of a turn back, it was a twelfth of a turn back plus the drift, and the drift was a function of how long it had been running and how hard.\n\nRooke sat down on the walkway with her back against the rail and Thorncastle's notebook on her knees and did it in nine minutes with sixty people watching her, and got an answer, and then said, out loud, in the silence:\n\n\"That's wrong.\"\n\n\"What?\" said Crale.\n\n\"That's wrong, I've assumed the drift's symmetrical and it isn't, it's a feed-side fault so the drift only runs one way.\" She was already crossing it out. \"Give me four minutes.\"\n\nNobody in that gallery ever forgot it. Not the answer. The crossing out. A fourteen year old girl in a mended coat sitting on the floor in front of the Chief Engineer and four Council members and a newspaper, saying *that's wrong* about her own work, out loud, with the whole city going dark, and then doing it again.\n\nIt took her six minutes, not four.\n\n\"A twelfth back, and then eleven sixty-fourths of a turn beyond it,\" she said. \"And then it'll hunt for about ninety seconds and you have to leave it alone while it hunts, and it'll settle short, and then you give it a sixty-fourth.\"\n\n\"How do you know it'll settle short?\"\n\n\"I don't,\" said Rooke. \"It should settle long. Everything I know says it should settle long.\" She looked up. \"But in the year before he was born the eastern bank went out of true and it came back on its own in eleven moves, and it stopped at the right one, and eleven moves is the number you get if it's settling short every time and correcting. So either there's something in this machine that hunts, or a man in Kettle Yard misremembered a bad shift thirty-one years ago.\"\n\nThorncastle, standing at the rail, said, \"Which do you think?\"\n\n\"I think we find out in ninety seconds,\" said Rooke.\n\n**Corvain** had the authority, and it was the only reason any of it was permitted to happen. She stood between Marrow and Halvard Crale for forty minutes with a warrant in her hand and made the space, and she did it by knowing, precisely, the four hundred and eleven lines of a Council audit sheet and which delegated authorities Crale did and did not hold on a gallery in an emergency, and she was correct four times running in front of Council members who could check.\n\n**Thorncastle** did the last thing, which was the smallest and possibly the largest.\n\nWhen Crale said, for the fourth time, that he would not have the Engine adjusted on the arithmetic of a first-year scholar, Linnea Thorncastle walked to the control stand, took the notebook, read Rooke's nine minutes and her six minutes, and said:\n\n\"I have held the chair of reckoning in this university for thirty-one years and I have examined every reckoner in this building including you, Halvard, and I got you through it. The arithmetic is sound. Turn the governor.\"\n\n---\n\nThey turned it a twelfth back and eleven sixty-fourths beyond, and Marrow did it, with his hand on the wheel and Sella Vaunt's hand next to his on the lock, because it needed two.\n\nAnd then the eastern bank of the Engine of Gifford began to hunt.\n\nIt went eleven positions.\n\nIt went them one at a time, slowly, with about four seconds between each, and the whole gallery felt every one of them come up through the floor, and nobody on that walkway moved or spoke for the entire ninety seconds. Sixty people stood in the dark over a drop nobody had measured and listened to a machine built by twelve dead people count.\n\nIt settled short.\n\n\"Sixty-fourth,\" said Rooke.\n\nMarrow gave it a sixty-fourth.\n\nThe stagger came out of the floor.\n\nIt did not happen dramatically. That was what everyone said afterwards, all sixty of them, in about sixty different ways: it was not dramatic at all. The floor had been walking with one leg shorter than the other since nine minutes past six, and at twelve minutes past nine it stopped, and the whole enormous patient sound of the thing came back into true, and about four seconds later the gaslights on the gallery came up from a third to full.\n\nSomewhere a very long way below and outside, across the entire Under, in eleven thousand houses, a pipe went *knock*.\n\n---\n\nRooke was still sitting on the walkway with her back against the rail.\n\nMarrow came and sat down next to her because his legs had stopped being reliable, and the two of them sat on the floor of the fourth gallery with their backs to the rail while sixty people had four separate arguments over their heads.\n\n\"It hunted,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Eleven moves.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So it's not a governor resetting.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"A governor doesn't hunt like that. A governor goes to a stop. That went to the *answer*, and it went there by trying and correcting, eleven times, and stopping short each time, and I have been sitting here for four minutes trying to work out what in the world has a settling behaviour like that.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"There's one thing I know of,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"A person,\" she said. \"That's how a person does a sum they can't do in their head. You try it, you see you're short, you go again. Eleven times.\" She turned her head and looked at him. \"That machine did not correct itself. It *worked it out*. And it took ninety seconds, and it took eleven attempts, and it stopped short every single time, which means it is not good at this. Which means it is doing it the hard way. Which means—\"\n\nShe stopped.\n\n\"Say it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Which means it's been doing it the hard way for a hundred and three years,\" said Rooke, \"every time one of them set something slightly wrong, quietly, in the dark, eleven moves at a time, and nobody ever knew, and the reason four hundred and eleven men can run a machine they don't understand is not that the procedure is good.\"\n\nThe gallery was very loud above them and neither of them heard any of it.\n\n\"It's been covering for them,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"For sixty years.\" Rooke's voice had gone almost to nothing. \"And in February, in Vaskeld, when their engine went out of true and the committee couldn't fix it and it took eleven days—\"\n\n\"It wasn't refusing.\"\n\n\"It was *trying*,\" said Rooke. \"It was trying for eleven days and it couldn't get there, because whatever's in it that hunts isn't clever, it's just patient, and eleven days of it wasn't enough, and forty-one thousand six hundred people died while a machine sat in the dark under a city doing a sum it wasn't good enough to do.\"\n\nThey sat on the floor of the fourth gallery.\n\n\"It waited two hours tonight,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Two hours and twenty-three minutes.\"\n\n\"With sixty of them stood up here reading dials.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And it didn't start hunting till we turned the governor.\" He put his head back against the rail. \"It was waiting for somebody to *start it off*. It got the last sixty-fourth wrong and we had to give it that too.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then it's not covering for them,\" said Marrow. \"It can't do it on its own. It never could. It needs somebody to go first.\"\n\nAnd Rooke, fourteen years old, sitting on a steel walkway two hundred feet above a drop nobody had measured, with her back against a brass rail and a borrowed notebook on her knees, said the sentence that the next thirty years of her life would be about.\n\n\"Then there's nothing in the blood at all,\" she said. \"There never was. There's a machine down there that can nearly do it, and once a year for a hundred and three years this city has gone out and found one person who can do the last sixty-fourth.\"\n\n---\n\n**VI. Ten Past Ten**\nAt ten past ten Silas Vane was standing in the yard behind the Ashlight with a glass in his hand looking up at a city where the lights had just come back on.\n\nRatchet came out to him.\n\n\"It's on the street already,\" said Ratchet. \"There was a paper man on the gallery. Crale said *not tonight* in front of four Council and it's going in the morning edition.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And they're saying it was a Sirrath. In custody. On a warrant.\" Ratchet was not looking at him. \"And a first stratum, and a professor, and a coat.\"\n\nVane did not say anything for a while.\n\nThen he laughed, once, quietly, and poured the rest of his glass out onto the cobbles.\n\n\"Nineteen years,\" he said. \"Nineteen years of pulling the cleverest boy in the Under through that yard, and I got him, and I had him, and I built the whole thing so that the only door out was through me.\"\n\n\"He went to a coat.\"\n\n\"He did not go to a coat,\" said Vane. \"That is what everybody in this yard will say by Thursday and it is wrong, and I would like one person in this city to be accurate about it, so it may as well be you.\" He set the glass on the wall. \"He found three other people who were as capable as he was, and he went with them, and I have spent nineteen years making very sure that never happened, and I did not fail because I was careless.\n\n\"I failed because a Laachmi with a low ceiling sold three answers in the same week in April and then changed its mind.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Ten.*" },
  { n: 11, title: "Reversion to the Mean", text: "**I. The Morning Edition**\nThe *Gifford Intelligencer* of the first of September ran it on the second page, under the shipping, in eleven column inches, with the headline:\n\n**ENGINE FAULT RECTIFIED. CHIEF ENGINEER ANSWERS QUESTIONS.**\n\nThat was the whole of it. Eleven inches. The Engine had gone out of true for three hours and three minutes, and the Chief Engineer had said *not tonight* in front of four Council members and a reporter, and the paper put it on page two under the shipping, and the phrase *not tonight* appeared in the ninth paragraph without comment.\n\nCorvain read it at half past six in the morning at a coffee stall on the seam and understood four things in about ninety seconds.\n\nThe first was that nobody had suppressed it. There had been no order, no visit, no quiet word. The reporter had filed what he saw and an editor of thirty years' standing had looked at eleven inches about a machine and put it under the shipping, because a fault that had been rectified in three hours was not news, and because in a hundred and three years the Engine had never once been news, and because there was no shape in that man's head for the story he was holding.\n\nThe second was that this was the whole mechanism. Not conspiracy. *Category*. The thing had been safe for a century not because it was hidden but because nobody had a place to put it.\n\nThe third was that by Thursday it would be gone entirely.\n\nAnd the fourth was that page two, under the shipping, in eleven inches, was the single most useful document that had ever existed on this subject, because it was *printed*, and it was *dated*, and in the ninth paragraph the Chief Engineer of Gifford was on the record saying that he could not fix the Engine.\n\nCorvain bought nine copies.\n\n---\n\n**II. The Second of September**\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"Would you like tea. I have discovered that people always say no.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am. Please.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Prewitt poured. She had not slept; it did not show in her face and it showed entirely in the room, because there were papers on the desk, four separate stacks of them, in a room that had contained nothing at all eleven days ago. \"You will have worked out that I have had a difficult week.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Eleven other people knew. I said that to you on the twenty-second of August and I said I did not yet know which of them would hold.\" Prewitt sat down. \"Six of them will. Three of them will not and are already moving. One of them died in 'ninety-four and I have been counting him for four years out of habit, which tells you something about how often this is discussed.\n\n\"And one of them was Halvard Crale, and on the thirty-first of August Halvard Crale ordered a full recalibration of the eastern bank because a fourteen year old wrote him four lines asking what the conditions of her scholarship were.\" Prewitt put the pot down. \"So we may take it that the eleven days did not go as I intended.\"\n\n\"No, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Say the rest of it. You have been in the building four minutes and you have something in your coat and you are being polite about it.\"\n\nCorvain took out the newspaper and laid it on the desk, folded to page two.\n\nPrewitt read the ninth paragraph.\n\nShe read it twice, and then she sat back in her chair, and for about four seconds Corvain watched the Chancellor of Gifford look genuinely, undefendedly old.\n\n\"Under the shipping,\" said Prewitt.\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"A hundred and three years.\" She put her fingertips on it. \"And it goes on page two under the shipping, and by Thursday nobody will remember it, and you have brought me nine copies.\"\n\n\"Four copies. Five are elsewhere.\"\n\n\"Of course they are.\" Prewitt almost smiled. \"So. You are here to tell me that the thing I said could not be published has been published, in the Council's own approved organ, without anybody noticing, and that the Chief Engineer of Gifford is on the printed record admitting the whole of it in the ninth paragraph, and that this is now a fact about the world rather than an allegation in a young officer's file.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"And what do you want?\"\n\n\"The same thing I wanted on the twenty-second of August,\" said Corvain. \"Ten years. Stop the amendments and build the four hundred.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt got up and went to the window and stood there for a long time, and the city below her had its lights on and its trams running and its pipes knocking, and eleven thousand people had gone to work that morning without knowing that anything had nearly happened at all.\n\n\"I am going to tell you what I can do,\" she said, \"and it is going to be very much less than you have asked for, and I want you to hear the whole of it before you decide what you think of me, because I have had eleven days and this is genuinely the most I can carry.\"\n\n\"Ma'am.\"\n\n\"I cannot stop the amendments.\" Prewitt turned round. \"I want to be exact. I cannot stop them because the amendment authority is not mine. It has sat with the Office of the Chief Engineer since the fifty-first year and it is renewed quarterly and it is lawful, and a Chancellor who attempts to take a lawful delegated authority away from a Chief Engineer without cause is a Chancellor who is testing whether the Council will follow her, and I have counted, and it will not. Six of eleven is not a majority of anything.\n\n\"What I can do is three things.\n\n\"Halvard Crale resigns on the eleventh. Not for the amendments; for the eastern bank, on the record, on his own words in a newspaper, and I have his letter in that drawer and it cost me two of the six.\" She held up a finger. \"Understand what that is worth. It is not justice and it does not touch the system. It is that for the first time in a hundred and three years the Office of the Chief Engineer will be filled by somebody who was put there by a Chancellor rather than by an uncle.\n\n\"Second. The forty-one amendments of this year are reversed.\"\n\nCorvain's hands went still on her knees.\n\n\"All of them?\"\n\n\"All forty-one, effective the first of October, retrospective to the summer. They will be placed in the strata their assessed results earned, which for eleven of them means promotion to the Rise and for the rest means writs and placements they were entitled to in July.\" Prewitt sat down. \"That is not generosity, Officer. That is the price of Crale's letter, and it is defensible in Council as an administrative correction arising from an operational failure, which is a sentence I spent nine hours constructing.\n\n\"And it is forty-one out of fourteen thousand two hundred and six, and I would like you to say that number out loud before you thank me for anything.\"\n\n\"Forty-one out of fourteen thousand two hundred and six,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the third thing?\"\n\n\"The third thing is the only one that matters and it is the one that will look like nothing,\" said Prewitt. \"From this year, the annual returns will publish assessed results alongside final strata. Both columns. In the volume, on the boards, on Tallow Street, every summer, where anybody can read them.\"\n\nCorvain sat back slowly.\n\n\"That's it,\" she said.\n\n\"That is it.\"\n\n\"That's the whole thing. That's everything.\" Corvain was not entirely in control of her voice. \"Ma'am, if both columns are on the boards then the amendments are *visible*. Not in a sealed volume, not in a records office. On the wall outside the hall, in front of six hundred children and their mothers, every summer, for ever. Anybody can count them. A grocer could count them.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You've just ended it.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"I have made it *countable*, which is a different thing and a much slower one, and I want you to be extremely clear about the difference, because you are twenty-one and you will otherwise spend the next four years disappointed.\n\n\"The amendments will continue. Crale's successor will make them, and the successor after that, and they will go on the boards in both columns, and for the first three or four years absolutely nothing will happen, because nobody reads the boards. Then somebody will. A clerk, or a grocer, or a mother on Ferrow Lane with a child in the ninth, and it will take another few years for enough of them to be angry at the same time, and then this council will fall, and it will deserve to.\n\n\"That is what I have bought. Not an end. Ten or fifteen years of daylight, and a public number, and a Chief Engineer I chose. And in those ten or fifteen years there will be forty-one promoted this October and forty-one next year if I can hold the argument, and the year after, and if it goes on long enough there will be four hundred of them, and when this council falls there will be somebody to run the machine.\"\n\nShe folded her hands.\n\n\"You proposed on the twenty-second of August that this council should spend ten years making itself unnecessary. I said I would give you an answer I could keep. That is the answer. It is a worse plan than yours and it is the best one I can actually deliver, and it will outlive me, and I will not see the end of it.\"\n\n---\n\n\"And the chair?\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Ah.\" Prewitt drew a sheet from the second stack. \"Yes. I am obliged to offer you that, and I am going to, and I would like you to refuse it.\"\n\n\"Ma'am?\"\n\n\"You are twenty-one years old and you have just spent a year doing the only piece of original work anybody in this city has done on this subject in forty-five years, and you did it because you were outside.\" Prewitt pushed the sheet across. \"That is a secondment to my office. It is a considerable promotion, it would delight your mother, and it would put you in this room for the next thirty years, and inside eighteen months you would be the most careful custodian of the whole arrangement in Gifford, because you would have seen the Vaskeld figures and you would have signed your name to something. That is not a slur. It is what happened to me and it is what happened to Reeve Ashcombe and it is what happened to eleven other people, and it works every single time.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the paper.\n\n\"Then why offer it?\"\n\n\"Because if I do not offer it, somebody else will, and it will be one of the three who are already moving, and they will not warn you first.\" Prewitt met her eye. \"Take it or refuse it. But decide it here, in front of me, having been told exactly what it does.\"\n\nCorvain sat in the empty room for a long moment.\n\n\"I'll take it,\" she said.\n\nPrewitt's eyebrows went up about a millimetre.\n\n\"On one condition, ma'am, and it isn't negotiable, and if you say no I'll go back to the division and I'll be a nuisance for thirty years instead.\"\n\n\"Name it.\"\n\n\"You publish both columns this summer, in September, before the Council can debate it, and you do it by administrative notice.\" Corvain leaned forward. \"Because the moment I sit in this room I become somebody who can be reviewed, and moved, and given a seat on a committee, and I know exactly what happens to me in eighteen months, and so do you. So the boards go up first. Then I'll come and be careful for you.\n\n\"That's what Ashcombe would have wanted twenty-six years ago and there was nobody to make the trade with. I'd like to make it.\"\n\nIsolde Prewitt looked at her for a long time.\n\nThen she reached for a pen, and wrote the administrative notice out in her own hand, four lines, dated the second of September, and sanded it and put it in the tray.\n\n\"It goes out on Thursday,\" she said.\n\n---\n\n**III. Aldous**\nThe journals turned up in October, and they turned up because Ketu could write a list.\n\nShe had been at it since August. Eleven pages, in a hand she had taught herself at fifty, of what a woman on her knees on a landing for thirty years knows about the houses on Aster Row: which families had sold what, which had a room nobody went into, which had a great-grandmother who talked, and where things went when a house was cleared.\n\nItem nine on the fourth page was this:\n\n*Fenwick house, Aster Row. Cleared 1881 on death of old Lady F. Papers went to the bindery on the Wick with the books because the family took the silver and did not want the paper. The bindery kept what would not sell. I know this because I carried four boxes of it down the area steps myself and the bindery man gave me a shilling.*\n\n*Old Lady F was a Wrought before she married. Her grandmother's name was Wrought. I do not know if this matters.*\n\nRooke read that on a Tuesday, out loud, in Bramwell's shop, to four people and a stove.\n\nThe bindery on the Wick was the one Whistle had bought a hundred and three published returns from, one at a time, over nineteen years, because nobody else wanted them.\n\nIt took Ivy Thorncastle nine days in the back room of that bindery, and she found them in a tea chest under a run of parish registers, and she came out into Kettle Yard with her hair full of dust and four leather books in her arms, and she was fifteen years old and she was crying, and what she said was:\n\n\"I told you I was going to be a very good historian.\"\n\n---\n\nThey were not what anybody had hoped for.\n\nThat was the first thing and it took Rooke about a week to stop resenting it. There was no revelation in them and no confession. Aldous Wrought had been sixty-one when he wrote the last of it and he had been a working reckoner all his life, and four fifths of the four volumes were working: page after page of it, in a small furious hand, and about a third of it was wrong in ways that had been sorted out fifty years later, and about a sixth of it was ahead of anything the university had taught anybody in 1897.\n\nBut there were eleven pages at the back of the fourth volume, dated the winter before he died, and they were not working. They were a memorandum, addressed to nobody, and the handwriting had got worse.\n\n*On the matter of the qualification, which they have begun to speak of as though it were settled.*\n\n*It is not settled and it is not true and I have said so at four meetings and I am tired of saying it.*\n\n*There is nothing in the blood. I would know. Of the Twelve, four were the children of gentlemen and eight were not, and the best of us was Halloran's mother, who could not read until she was nineteen. There is no faculty. There is a habit of mind, which is that one is willing to be wrong out loud, and it can be taught to almost anybody who is fed and left alone with paper, and it is destroyed by hunger, humiliation and being told at fourteen what one is worth.*\n\n*They will not accept this because it is not useful to them. They wish there to be a faculty, because a faculty may be inherited and a habit may not, and a man who has inherited a faculty need not go on demonstrating it.*\n\n*The Calibration was my proposal and I record here that it was a bad one.*\n\n*I proposed it in the eleventh year for one purpose, which was to find, out of the whole city, the children who had the habit, so that we might feed them and leave them alone with paper. It was to be temporary. It was to run until we had built enough schools that we could stop looking for the habit and start making it, which I estimated at forty years.*\n\n*It has been running nineteen and there are no schools and there is now a schedule of eleven strata, which I did not propose and which was added by the Council in my absence, and I am told it is administratively necessary.*\n\n*I understand what it will become. It will become a machine for finding the habit in other people's children and then, because two of them is inconvenient, for putting all but one of them back.*\n\n*I am sixty-one and I have a bad chest and I will not see it. I record it so that when it is happening somebody may find this and know that it was foreseen, and that it was foreseen by me, and that it is therefore mine.*\n\n*The Engine will manage. It is better than we are at almost everything and it is patient, and it will cover a great deal of incompetence for a very long time, and that is the worst thing about it, because it means nobody will find out until it is far too late to teach anybody anything.*\n\n*It cannot do the last part. Whoever reads this: it cannot do the last part. It will get within a hair and then it will stop and wait, and somebody must go first. There must always be somebody who can go first, and there must never be only one of them.*\n\n*A. Wrought*\n\n---\n\n**IV. What Each of Them Got**\n**Marrow** was charged.\n\nThat was the thing nobody outside the four of them ever quite understood, and it was the correct outcome, and Ashcombe had told him it would be on the steps of the enforcement building on the thirty-first of August and had not softened it once.\n\nHe had confessed to conspiracy in front of four witnesses. Vane's operation was real, the method was his, and there was a man in Kettle Yard with three rods on a chain who could confirm every part of it. Marcus Vale prosecuted, and did it thoroughly, and did not once use the word Sirrath.\n\nHe was not hanged. He was not hanged because Marcus Vale, on the ninth day, in front of Magistrate Colworth, put into evidence the fact that the accused had come forward voluntarily on the night of the fault, in the middle of the emergency, in the certain knowledge of what it would cost him, and had then diagnosed the eastern bank linkage on the fourth gallery in front of sixty witnesses including four Council members.\n\n\"I am obliged to prosecute him,\" Vale told the court, \"and I am obliged to tell you what he did, and I am not going to pretend that both of those things sit comfortably. They do not. I have been doing this for thirty years and it is the first time.\"\n\nEleven months in the works at Grieves. He served nine.\n\nAnd on the day he came out there was a letter waiting at Bramwell's shop from the Office of the Chief Engineer, signed by Crale's successor, offering him a probationary place on the eastern bank maintenance establishment, subject to writ, subject to conduct.\n\nMarrow read it twice and put it on the bench and did not answer it for four days.\n\n\"Take it,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"It's the same building.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"It's the same four hundred and eleven men.\"\n\n\"Four hundred and fifty-two, now,\" said Bramwell. \"They took the forty-one in October, and eleven of them went up the Rise and the rest went in with writs, and there's a Laachmi girl out of the chapel behind the hall in your year who's on the *third gallery*.\" He coughed for a while. \"So it's not the same building. It's the same building with fifty-two of us in it, and in nine years it'll be a hundred, and there'll be a day when somebody opens a machine in this city and finds a Sirrath name on a repair that held.\"\n\n\"You said that in January.\"\n\n\"I say it about once a year, it's one of about four things I've got left.\" Bramwell handed him the pen. \"And there's the other reason and I'll say it once. I'm seventy-two and I've a chest like a wet sack and the writ dies with me. It does not descend to a Sirrath with an amendment. That's still the law and it'll be the law when I'm in the ground.\"\n\nHe tapped the letter.\n\n\"But a writ *issued* to a Sirrath on the eastern bank establishment is a writ, and a man with a writ can hold a shop, and I'd like there to be a shop in Kettle Yard in ninety years, and I'd like the note in the housing at number four compressor house to be signed by the man who owns it.\"\n\n**Rooke** did not go to Aster Row in her fourth year, and she did not decline the appointment either.\n\nWhat she did was write a second letter, in November, four lines again, and this one had nine drafts as well.\n\n*To the Office of the Chief Engineer.*\n\n*I understand that the conditions of my scholarship require the fourth-year attachment and the writ. I do not decline them.*\n\n*I decline to be the only one. In the year of my Calibration forty-one candidates were amended down and eleven of them are now on the Rise, and there is no reason whatever that the first stratum should be a single appointment, and there never was.*\n\n*I will take the attachment in the same year as ten others, or I will take it in nine years' time when there are ten others, and I am fifteen and I can wait longer than the Office can.*\n\n*R.*\n\nShe sent one copy to the Chief Engineer, one to the Proctor to be minuted, and one to a secondee in the Chancellor's office.\n\nIt took four years.\n\nShe took the attachment at eighteen, in a cohort of nine, and one of the nine was a Laachmi and two were Sirrath and four were out of the Under, and Rooke was not the best of them, which she said out loud, twice, in front of witnesses, and meant.\n\nShe sent money down the hill every month of those four years and it was never enough and it was never nothing. Mother Halloway died in the February of Rooke's third year, at seventy-four, upstairs in her own bed in the back room, which had taken eleven months of arguing with a landlord and four marks a week that Rooke did not really have. Nettle's cough went in the spring of the second year and did not come back. Tessa Brindle kept Marta's ledgers for six years and then bought the shop, slowly, in pieces, on a chalk, and never learned to hold a column in her head and never once needed to.\n\nPip came out of the doorway eventually. It took about two years.\n\n**Corvain** sat in the room.\n\nShe was thirty-two before she understood that Prewitt had been right about the eighteen months, and thirty-four before she understood that Prewitt had also been right that this did not make the trade a bad one, and thirty-nine when Isolde Prewitt died and Elspeth Corvain became the youngest Chancellor of Gifford in sixty years and the first who had ever entered that room as an accusation.\n\nThe boards went up in September, both columns, by administrative notice, and nobody noticed for four years.\n\nIn the fifth year a grocer on Ferrow Lane called Tessa Brindle, who could not hold a column in her head and had spent six years doing somebody else's ledgers with a finger and a slate, stood in front of the boards on Tallow Street with her nephew's card in her hand and counted the difference between the two columns for the eight Under districts, out loud, slowly, in front of about forty people who were waiting to find out what their children were worth.\n\nIt took her most of an hour.\n\nThat was the beginning of it, and it was nothing to do with any of them, and it was exactly what Prewitt had said would happen and it happened eleven years earlier than she had estimated, and Corvain was in the room when the argument reached the Council and she did not have to say very much at all.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Boards**\nOn the last morning of the eleventh summer after, six hundred and eighty-one fourteen year olds queued down the pavement on Tallow Street in the sootfall.\n\nThe eye was still cut into the lintel. *By reckoning, order.* Nobody read it. Everybody had been told what it said.\n\nThere were eleven boards under the lintel and there would be eleven boards next year, because the strata had not gone; eleven years of daylight had not abolished the strata and nobody in that queue had been promised that they would. What there was, now, was two columns on every board instead of one, and a printed line at the foot of each in type nobody had bothered to make small:\n\n*Where the assessed and final columns differ, the difference has been made by administrative amendment. Enquiries to the Office of the Chief Engineer.*\n\nThere were nine hundred and eleven enquiries that year. There had been four in the first year.\n\nA clerk came down the line with a box of wooden tokens and a ledger and asked each of them their name, their district and their household.\n\n\"Name.\"\n\n\"Halloway.\"\n\nThe clerk's pen stopped. \"First name?\"\n\n\"Just Halloway, sir. It's the household name and I've took it.\"\n\n\"Right.\" He wrote it. \"District.\"\n\n\"Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"Foster?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\nHe put the token in her palm and moved on, and she stood in the queue in the black rain turning it over in her fingers, and up on the boards in the gilt frame at the top of the lintel there were two names in the first stratum this year, and there had been three the year before that, and nobody in the queue knew that this had once been the single most impossible thing in the city.\n\nSomewhere a very long way below the pavement, under the sumps and the black flooded tunnels nobody had names for, the Turn went round.\n\nIt got within a hair of the answer.\n\nThen it stopped, and waited, the way it had waited for a hundred and fourteen years, for somebody to go first.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Eleven.*\n\n*End of Book One.*\n\n**THE END**" },
];
